const e={21:["This installment turns again to Wei Pincai. After dwelling in the Hua mansion for more than a month he had cultivated everyone above and below with such thorough success that Young Master Hua granted him some dignity and his purse grew easier; thereupon he began to show the petty man's triumph and would no longer keep his place. Within were Gu Yueqing and Zhang Xiaomei; outside Yang Meichuang, Feng Zipei, and such company — nightly revelry — and whatever he saw and heard ran to schemes of influence and trimming to power; his heart grew fat and his daring swelled. Formerly at the Mei residence Academician Mei had been at home: though he did not lecture Pincai, fear naturally restrained him. Moreover Ziyu kept company only with gentlemen of name — none of those idle roamers. Like a horse stalled in its manger, however much it wished to bolt the traces it could not. Now he might indulge every whim without scruple.","One day Zhang and Gu both had business; Pincai therefore went out of the city alone, hired a fashionable thirteen-panel glass-windowed heated carriage, dressed up Fourth Boy as well, and with an air of triumph came expressly to inquire after Ziyu's illness.","Arriving at the Mei house he paid his respects to Lady Yan and went straight to Ziyu's chamber. Ziyu had now been ill over a month; though not gravely sick, he felt leaden and weary, ate far less than before, and his spirits flagged utterly. Sometimes he diverted himself with books, but his vitality was too weak for any pursuit; he sat alone without speaking, wearing the vacant brooding air of one who reads into empty air. Even Lady Yan could not guess what ailed her son — she assumed only that study had worn his blood and prescribed ginseng and tuckahoe tonics; useless as medicine ill-matched to the ailment. As the proverb runs, the heart's sickness needs a heart's cure; passions stirred from the seven emotions cannot be soothed by roots and bark. Only Ziyu himself understood — unless Qinyan came in person and talked freely through a whole day and night he could not suddenly recover. Pincai guessed part of this; entering, he asked many questions. With no visitors these few days Ziyu felt stifled — Pincai's presence eased him somewhat. Asked how things stood at the Hua mansion, Pincai praised Young Master Hua as unequalled under heaven, boasted how fine were his attendant Lin Shanzhi and the Eight Ling troupe — and declared even Qinyan could not match them.",`At mention of Qinyan Ziyu's old sorrow stirred; he said to Pincai: "You were the first to speak of Qinyan — when I met him he seemed peerless in the world; how can there now be so many said to surpass him?" Pincai replied: "Qinyan's looks are indeed fine, but his temper is cold — like a blossom whose color is superb yet hangs on the highest branch where you cannot climb to reach it; whoever loves flowers naturally loves what lies near the hand. Or like winter's moon: brilliantly clear, yet when you lift your eyes that cold radiance pierces flesh and bone — beside the moons of spring's third month or autumn's eighth it seems less kindly though brighter." "Such are the shallow metaphors of the mundane world," Ziyu countered. "A flower unplucked preserves its own pristine fragrance; a moon unadmired shines absolute in its solitary grace. If a high blossom eludes the grasping hand, it is meant for the rare plumage of exotic birds, for the caress of immortal dew and celestial winds—its very unattainability is its crowning glory! And though the winter moon may seem too austere for casual admiration, when it illuminates the blooming winter plum and the pristine snow, when it dances on clear waves and silvers the drifting clouds, its cold radiance attains an ethereal purity. How can mere frost diminish its splendor? Roughly speaking, Qinyan's living fragrance and vivid color anyone may note; Qinyan's deep earnest feeling few can grasp. Taking him only by ordinary likeness misses him — hence vulgar and refined part ways. That Qinyan should meet fortune such as this is Heaven shaping the man — not turning him into roadside peach and plum." In these words Ziyu spoke from true insight into Qinyan; without noticing he treated Pincai as a vulgar sort of different taste. Pincai inwardly smarted but answered dryly: "Quite so, quite so — Qinyan's merits I said long ago — few in this world beside Your Excellency and myself appreciate him so far. I mean to visit him — what truly is his illness?" Ziyu said: "Will you go today?" Pincai said: "If errands permit — I'll pass by." Ziyu said: "If you see him, do not say I am ill. If he asks, say you do not know." Pincai said: "I understand — what message shall I carry?" Ziyu said: "Nothing much." After a pause: "Only tell him not to be ill." Pincai laughed: "If you can bid him not to be ill, he can bid you the same." Ziyu knew he had slipped and smiled, then amended: "Since he is already ill, nothing can be done — only urge him not to heap illness on illness. If he hears I am ill, do not hide it — say mine is slight and will clear in days. Say Xiangwan is the kindest soul — he may often seek him out and talk away gloom — then recovery comes faster." Pincai did not fully grasp this but agreed. Ziyu added: "I cannot visit him myself — seeing Xiangwan will do." As he spoke his look grew bleak. Pincai saw clearly this sickness rose from Qinyan and thought: "Yuxiang is useless — if he loves Qinyan why not summon him often and pour out both hearts — then neither would ail — why bottle it — he is no boudoir maiden barred from sight." Taking leave he did not seek Yuanmao but exchanged brief civilities at accounts and gate offices, then went straight to Cherry Lane and Qinyan's lodging.`,`As it happened Changqing had gone out; Pincai walked straight into Qinyan's bedchamber. Green windows were tightly shut; the small yard stood empty; a plum tree bore clusters of yellow fruit, half flushed red on one side, several fallen on the ground. Suddenly a voice cried: "A guest — do not enter!" Looking up he saw a white parrot hung under the eaves — at sight of Pincai it began to speak. Someone stepped from the opposite wing and barred the way: "The young gentleman is ill and cannot receive visitors — sir, please sit in the outer guest room." Pincai said: "I am no stranger — we are closest of friends. Go in and say my surname is Wei; I come from Academician Mei's house to see him about his illness — I have something to say." The man relayed this; Qinyan's cough sounded twice within; then he heard: "If he is from Academician Mei's house, show him in." The fellow came out smiling: "Sir, if you please." The suite ran to three bays — outer room held a table and stools. A page lifted the curtain; stepping through the curtain, a delicate mingling of floral fragrance and herbal medicine instantly refreshed the senses. This was still the antechamber. Pincai took a seat, noting how the emerald glass of the windows cast a soft, verdant luminescence over the white plush carpet. Above the heated platform hung a scroll, "Picture of Shouyang Forehead Dots." Beside it hung a couplet: "A heart cradling the autumn moon in a vase of ice / A soul resting by the plum blossoms against a paper canopy." On the kang table a vase held a spray of plum; to one side four panels of red plum by Xiao Cixian; to the other four seal-script scrolls by Xu Ziyun. Before the window stood an antique brick-and-pearwood qin stand bearing a plum-patterned duan qin. The inner chamber hung a moonlight autumn-gauze embroidered curtain patterned with every variety of plum — this was the sleeping room.`,'Pincai was about to step further when Qinyan lifted the curtain and came out. At first sight he wore a pale lake-green lined silk jacket and blue gauze short-sleeved surcoat — somewhat thinner than before, like plum in snow that prefers cold — only the more pitiable and lovely. He seated Pincai in the place of honor but himself sat far off on a plum-blossom stool by the qin table; they brought tea and a lad offered a white brass water-pipe and filled several bowls for Pincai. Pincai said: "I heard you were unwell — I came out of the city especially to see you — are you better lately?" At the words "out of the city" Qinyan pondered — so Yuxiang had not visited because he had moved inside the walls — and asked: "When did Yuxiang move into the city? Which ward? How far from here?"',`Pincai realized Qinyan had misunderstood: "Yuxiang has not moved — I now lodge in the city; I no longer stay at his house." Hearing this Qinyan fell silent; his spirit seemed to slacken and vexation crossed his face. Pincai thought: "Ask after Yuxiang and he brightens — speak straight to me and he turns ice." To probe he asked: "Have you seen Yuxiang this month?" Qinyan said: "Since New Year's meeting at Yiyuan we have not met." Pincai smiled faintly: "I gather Yuxiang no longer remembers you." Qinyan started: "What do you mean — remembers?" Pincai feigned hesitation: "Nothing — only Yuxiang has been busy lately — naturally he forgets." Qinyan pressed: "Busy how?" Pincai said: "Nothing special — friends, opera, wine." Qinyan reflected — something felt wrong — "I heard Yuxiang was ill — that he went to Yiyuan several times — I never crossed him." Pincai gave a cold laugh and said nothing. Qinyan's doubts sharpened: "Could Yuxiang truly have forgotten me — taken up with someone else?" Yet he recalled how Yuling had flirted that day and Yuxiang flared — hardly likely. Pincai's manner was evasive — half swallowed — as if hiding something. "He said he lives in the city — not at Yuxiang's — yet how does he know Yuxiang's affairs? If Yuxiang had nothing against me why would he lie? Still Pincai has never been trustworthy." Resolving that Yuxiang could not mean ill, he asked aloud: "Yuxiang is an upright man — not one for idle romps." Pincai calculated: "If I name someone he knows he will verify — I'll name someone distant." Slowly he said: "Desire is never fixed — without companions to lure one, one never thinks it; without tasting sweetness one never seeks it. They say 'near vermilion you turn red — near ink you turn black.' Bad company hooks you onto that road; a taste of honey draws you in." He sighed: "Pity Yuxiang — he began so principled and tasteful — would not run wild. At first when people gossiped I doubted — today I visited him — I enter without announcement as always — reached his study outer room — heard laughter inside. Yun'er cried loudly: 'Guest!' When I stepped in Yuxiang lay on the couch face flushed — flustered — I saw no one — the laughter was not his — I thought nothing of it."`,`"When we talked he fenced with me — answers missed questions. Behind the bed-curtain came rustling — someone hiding — I could not ask openly — anyone who might meet me would not hide. I sat on his bed awhile; the curtain stirred again — my fan dropped; I stooped as if to retrieve it and lifted the hem — beneath I glimpsed boots and the hem of white patterned crepe — I guessed an actor — thought perhaps you — yet you were sick — unlikely out — nor would you hide from me — Yuxiang's face burned; he feigned sleep — spare him embarrassment — I withdrew. At the gatehouse pages gossiped with Lianzhu troupe Rongguan's boy Dezhi — then I knew it was Rongguan. Yuyong — why keep company with such — even an actor need not be hidden — I cannot fathom our brother's temper. I know you bore deep feeling for him — at first he missed you too — they say he sought you several times — you refused — his heart cooled. You did not ask — I kept silent — now that you ask I cannot hide — frolicking with actors is common — I hate only that he cast you aside for Rongguan — wasting true feeling and staining his own clean name." Qinyan listened blankly, half believing; hearing this his heart twisted — tears slipped — he forced them back — finger slowly traced the gold studs on the qin — "When you saw Yuxiang — nothing else was said?" Pincai said: "Leaving he only asked what troupe I would hear." Qinyan said: "All this is true?" Pincai sneered: "When do I lie — believe what you like." Qinyan said: "You sat so long — only that?" Pincai said: "I did not stay long — seeing him troubled I did not press." Qinyan said: "Truly only one sentence?" Pincai said: "Not two — else may I perish." Qinyan's chest tightened — he could ask no more. Pincai added: "They say at Yiyuan you were close — you gave him a qin — why drift apart — I hear the qin passed to another." Qinyan bent his head — could not answer. Pincai added: "Perhaps because you often visited Duxiang — he grew jealous — if you truly cared you should not always haunt Duxiang — some distinction of near and far is owed — no wonder he turned sour." Qinyan's bitterness welled — near tears — he burst out: "What words are these — what 'close' — what 'jealous' — I understand none of it." With that he wept outright. Pincai secretly rejoiced and soothed: "I misspoke — you cannot bear jest — forgive me." He stepped up to wipe the tears — Qinyan flushed with indignation, rose, and withdrew to the inner room — called the boy: "See Master Wei out — I am exhausted and cannot keep company." With that he lay down on the bed.`,`Pincai heard this and rage flared — almost to explode — then thought: "He is Yuxiang's darling — ruin him and Yuxiang may blame me — endure for now — settle slowly — time and again his cold eyes treat me as worthless — hateful. If I cannot master him what am I as Young Master Hua's man?" Swallowing fury he swept out, climbed his heated carriage, and flew off.`,`Qinyan lay on the bed — wave on wave of acid grief — tears without end — could not puzzle it out. He recalled Sulan's words that day — he and Yuxiang had come but the master was feasting guests and could not enter — then someone cut the tale short; these days he could not find Sulan — sent for him; Sulan changed to plain dress and came; Qinyan received him and they sat in the room. Sulan opened: "You wanted me — not again to play Yuxiang's stand-in?" Qinyan forced a smile: "A puzzle I cannot fathom." Sulan said: "Speak." Qinyan asked: "You mentioned Yuxiang — have you seen him lately?" Sulan smiled: "As expected — aside from Yuxiang you never send for me. The other day at Yiyuan watching dragon boats Duxiang invited him — illness kept him away. Duxiang spoke of his illness — more than a month — face grown thin — ten days ago he did visit Duxiang. There's a joke — hilarious — I'm afraid you'll weep — maybe I should not tell." Qinyan's heart already shifted — he pressed:`,`"What joke — say it now." Sulan said: "On Meixiang's birthday Tian Xiangfan wrote a short preface — everyone praised it — Duxiang copied it. The day Yuxiang came Jingyi showed him the piece; Yuxiang admired it. Standing by, Duxiang said: 'Xiangfan's style — the thicker the richer — the richer the better.' Jingyi and Duxiang at first did not catch Yuxiang's drift — then realized: he misheard 'thick and rich' as asking which of you or Qiguan ranks higher — he answered: 'Yuyong naturally above Yuyan — Yuyan though fine still falls short of Yaoqing and Meixiang — while Yuyong stands like trees of jade and blossoms of pearl — no place on human flower-lists.' Jingyi and Duxiang laughed until they could not stand — Yuxiang saw his error — deeply embarrassed — covered with a few mumbled words — so one moment he has not forgotten you — this illness may be hard to cure — will you cry?" At this Qinyan could not hold back — silent sobs — tears strung like thread — his blue gauze half-sleeve dark with a spreading wet patch across the chest.`,`Sulan soothed: "Why weep — you are barely mending — grief only heaps frost on snow — that is why I would not tell." Qinyan stamped: "Wei Pincai is less than human — slandering and staining people for nothing." Sulan asked who Wei Pincai was; Qinyan hid his face in the sleeve and wept without stopping; Sulan coaxed until he ceased, then told the drinking game the other day with Baozhu and Huifang — Yaoqing was middling — Meixiang sharp and unwilling to lose yet had swallowed that dull loss; also how Pan San plagued him — Meixiang would not tell — gossip said Meixiang feared him and hid at Xiangfan's for days — now he instructed servants: if Pan San called, always answer not at home — rumor added he had opened a door behind his bed through to the kitchen to dodge Pan San.`,`Hearing this Qinyan smiled slightly. Sulan asked who Wei Pincai was; Qinyan sketched last year's boat north and lodging at Mei's — then said: "Listening close, that Wei is a petty man — why ask — better not speak of him." Sulan said: "Nothing special — yesterday at Chunyang Tower I heard the boss tangled with a secretary of the Hua mansion — dragged to the constabulary — twenty blows — dozens of strings fine — two tables of wine besides."`,`"People said the man was also surnamed Wei — something Cai — from the Hua mansion." Qinyan said: "I heard him say he lives in the city now — not at Yuxiang's — I did not ask where." Sulan said: "The waiters told the tale end to end. New Year's week this Wei and others brought Baozhu and Er Xi — fifty-odd strings — the boss did not know him and must have spoken out of turn when tallying credit."`,`"Later the Wei man repaid in scraps — account still unsettled. Another day he brought five actors from si hour to shen — seventy-odd strings — the boss, not knowing he was from the Hua house, stalled on the chit and mentioned old debt — the Wei drunk smashed brush and shoved the inkstone off — shattered a table of crockery below — boss raised uproar — crowd parted them — ticket named Hua mansion secretary — next morning constables yoked the boss away — Zhang Zhongyu spent dozens of strings to settle — twenty blows only — two feasts for Master Zhang — they said the name was Wei something — hearing you curse him I guess it is Pincai." Qinyan said: "Whether or no — anyone called Wei Pincai is no good." He asked again what Sulan and Yuxiang had begun to say the day the master feasted guests. Sulan recounted that day in detail — "I've talked myself dry — you offered no tea." Qinyan laughed: "Talk ran urgent — forgot — brew tea." Sulan drank and smiled: "Yuxiang matches your heart — cast from one mold." Qinyan asked how. Sulan said: "Your rooms and dress — everywhere plum blossoms — because his surname is Mei — you cherish the token. Yuxiang lately wears nothing but qin motifs — brocade pouches, fan tassels — fans painted front and back with two qins — isn't that the same bent?" Qinyan laughed through tears: "Don't tease — how carry heavy instruments on the body?" Sulan laughed: "Fool — did you plant plum blossoms on your skin?" Qinyan smiled; Sulan added: "Duxiang says Yuxiang's every trinket is qin-shaped — fans show two instruments, one face, one reverse." Qinyan wept again; Sulan said: "You cry again and I'll hush." Qinyan swallowed: "Go on — I won't cry." Sulan asked why Pincai earned his hate; Qinyan repeated Pincai's lies about Yuxiang. Sulan reflected: "Yuxiang would never — you must not believe him." Qinyan said: "At first his tale seemed true — hearing you I know it cannot be — why must that mongrel invent slander — is there no decency?" Sulan said: "Perhaps Yuxiang crossed him — perhaps he seeks to drive you apart for some scheme." Qinyan sneered: "His schemes — do I flatter him because he entered the Hua mansion?" Sulan warned: "Now that knave rides Young Master's favor he may throw weight — wrong no petty villain — evil men make mischief — guard yourself as against thieves." Qinyan said: "I ignore him — what can he do?" They talked on until past second drum; Sulan rose — parting she said: "In a few days I'll devise a way for Yuxiang to meet you." Whether Pincai could swallow such cold treatment without further plots — hear the next installment.`]},l=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations21:e},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),a={22:["The last chapter told how Lu Sulan promised Qinyan that he would arrange for Ziyu to come out for a meeting. But no sooner was the promise spoken than the next day proved to be the Dragon Boat Festival, when there was no leisure to be had; and then came three days running of private engagement performances, which left Sulan so worn that he had to rest quietly for several days more. Qinyan meanwhile passed each day as if it were a year, sending someone daily to press him, until Sulan was quite at his wits' end. Moreover his own lodgings saw too many comers and goers to be convenient; and to borrow another man's rooms, or a wine-house or restaurant, would be more awkward still for intimate talk — nor could he well invite companion guests, leaving the two with full hearts and no way to pour them out. He turned it over and over, and could only borrow the pretext of an outing on the canal: a quiet day's excursion, and secluded enough. His mind made up, he sent a man outside the Great East Gate to hire an elegant boat, and had some of his own curios and ornaments, brushes, inkstones, and flowering plants carried down and arranged aboard. He then sent word to Qinyan to come down to the boat early next morning, that they might enjoy the day to the full, inviting no one else. Then he considered: how was Ziyu to be invited? Only by borrowing Duxiang's name. So he wrote out in detail the reasons for the invitation, sealed the letter, and sent a man to deliver it, instructing him to tell the gatekeepers simply that Master Xu of Yiyuan was inviting the young master to an outing on the canal.",`The messenger, as instructed, went straight to the Mei residence and presented the letter. Ziyu was sitting in listless gloom with the jade qin that Ziyun had given him, turning over the notation and picking out the easier pieces to practice; then he turned the qin over again and recited its inscription several times through. He felt only that green shade filled the courtyard and the long day stretched like a year, and he could think of nothing to dispel his tedium. Just as he sat fretting, Yun'er came in with a letter and laid it on the table, saying that Master Xu of Yiyuan had sent it, with an invitation for an outing on the canal tomorrow, and that a reply was wanted. Ziyu took up the letter and saw the envelope inscribed "For the hand of Young Master Mei" in neat, upright characters, quite unlike the hand of Ziyun or Cixian, and thought: "Perhaps he set a page-boy to write it." He opened it, and the first line read: "Lu Sulan respectfully addresses the poetic altar of Master Yuxiang" — at which his heart gave a leap. Why should Xiangwan be writing to him? Could something be afoot concerning Yunong? He read it word by word, and having finished, read it again, two and three times over, until a smile rose unbidden to his face. He said to Yun'er: "Go tell the messenger to wait a little; I will write a reply at once." When Yun'er had gone out, Ziyu read the letter once more and felt the flowers of his heart open wide — his illness was nine-tenths gone. He hastened to grind ink and spread paper. The first half of his reply was words of gratitude; the second half, words of promise, engaging himself to keep the appointment at the chen hour next morning. When he had finished he read it over, sealed it, and was about to write the direction when he suddenly paused: how should it be addressed? After a moment's hesitation the answer came to him: naturally, to "Master Xu" as well. He finished it, stamped his seal, and bade Yun'er hand it to the messenger with word that he would come without fail on the morrow. The messenger, reply in hand, returned at once and presented it to Sulan, who, seeing with what earnest warmth it was written and how inexhaustible its gratitude, was delighted in his turn, and taking the letter walked over in high spirits to Qinyan's.`,`He was just inside the second gate when he heard an uproar of shouting. Startled, Sulan lightened his step, slipped into the guest room on the east side, and peered through a crack in the window. There were two men: one seated, the other standing in the middle of the room, pounding the table and cursing. The sight frightened Sulan badly. The seated one wore a dark silk gown; he was about thirty, his black, oily face heavy with brutal flesh, a pair of iron balls turning in his hand as he dropped cold remarks that half stirred the quarrel and half feigned to soothe it. The other, also past thirty, was short-necked and barrel-chested, thick of waist and broad of shoulder, a great queue coiled on his head; he wore a dark silk short jacket over dark silk trouser-leggings, and dragged black satin embroidered slippers. Swinging his arms, he bellowed: "What sort of thing is he? A xiaodan, nothing more — and who isn't a patron of his? Whoever has money may summon him. Yet this little whelp must play the spook and sham sickness and see no one! Xianggong far redder than he wait on the gentlemen whenever they're called: do well, and there's a few strings of cash for reward; do ill, and they can roll their mother's egg and be off. Little turtle's spawn! If you don't roll yourself out here, Third Grandpa will wreck this little bastard's dog-kennel and thrash that old turtle of a master of yours into the bargain!" The seated one said: "Lao San, don't lose your temper yet — bide a while. Mark me: now that we've come today, he won't dare stay in."`,`The several members of Qinyan's household did their best to placate them, pleading: "Qinguan is truly ill — it's still touch and go with him. If he mends within these few days, his master will surely bring him to both your lordships' gates to kowtow. Today we beg you to hold your hand high: his sickness lies very heavy on him; curse him as you may, he simply cannot come out. And his master has gone into the city. We beg you to show a little grace. Let today pass, and tomorrow we may speak again; we will make his apologies for him — pray cool your anger." With that he made a low bow, and patting the man on the back, begged him to be seated. The man, still fuming, would not sit. The one in the dark gown spoke again: "Lao San, listen — what he says is fair enough. Let us spare him this once and come again tomorrow or the day after. If he still won't come out then, we'll take a horsewhip to him — and what can he do about it?" At this, Qinyan's man turned to the dark-gowned one: "We beg you to persuade this gentleman to wait until the illness has passed. He cannot possibly be well by tomorrow — you must grant us a few days' grace. Tomorrow his master shall first wait upon the mansion to apologize, and when Qinguan is recovered they will come together to make amends." He bowed again, presented two cups of tea, filled the man's water-pipe with tobacco, and offered it up. The fellow could only take in his sails, saying: "It's not that I have a bad temper — but this passes all bearing. I've seen sixty-two catties and a half in my day, but never so grand a xianggong as this. Go ask about: Chunlin, Fenglin — boys as red as they are come running the moment your Third Grandpa nods his head. Never once have I stood at a door and been denied, blocked by servants and fobbed off with lies about sickness. The monkey's cub! What is he skulking for? He's not missing an eye, nor short a leg, that he can't be looked at." The dark-clothed one rose: "Lao San, enough — we ought to go get our dinner." The other said: "Go where for dinner? Let them lay a meal for us here; we'll eat first and talk after." And the two sat down again. Qinyan's people, seeing the look of this, suspected extortion was the game, and reflected that once you have run into a plague-god, he will not be exorcised without burning paper-money. There was nothing for it but to go in and consult Qinyan, then bring out two strings of cash, saying with a placating smile: "We ought by rights to keep your lordships to dinner, but the cook is away today, and poor fare would only slight you. Qinguan offers this small treat — pray take yourselves to a restaurant with it."`,`And he presented the money with both hands. The dark-gowned one was on the point of taking it when the short-jacketed one saw there were only two strings, and fell to cursing again: "His mother's! Two strings of cash — what are the masters to eat on that? I'll have you know the masters don't eat at boiled-pork shops and dumpling lofts. When did a dinner ever cost us less than eight or ten strings — and this is two?" And he glared with bulging eyeballs. Qinyan's man, quick of wit, smiled again: "No haste — that was meant to honor one of your lordships only; there are two strings more, we'll fetch them straight out." He turned and brought out two more strings of cash, made a bow, and begged them again and again to accept. The short-jacketed one still wore his mask of fury, but the dark-gowned one picked up the money, slung it over his shoulder, and with his free hand hauled his companion out.`,`Sulan, still spying at the window-crack, had been struck quite dumb, and did not think of their coming out; as he hurried away the short-jacketed one caught sight of him and shouted: "And who are you, you little bastard? Where are you running? Come here — your grandpa has been wanting you!" Sulan fled for his very life, and the man chased him out the main gate; luckily Sulan was quick and had already got home. The lane was a short one — the two houses stood diagonally opposite, both at its mouth — and the man, supposing he had run clean out of the lane, gave up the chase and asked Qinyan's people: "That little rabbit just now — what troupe is he in, and where does he live? He runs at the sight of your Third Grandpa; then your Third Grandpa will make a point of finding him."`,`Qinyan's man answered: "He's from the Dengchun troupe — I can't recall his name. He lives a long way off, in Shitou Hutong." The two men, still spouting their wild talk, went reeling and swaying away. Within, Qinyan, who had heard himself so abused, was already faint with rage.`,"And who do you suppose these two were, coming to raise a row out of a clear sky? One was a carriage-driver of the Hua mansion; the one in the dark gown was the third boy of the resident cook — bought for the work by Wei Pincai at a cost of eight strings of cash.",`Lu Sulan, meanwhile, had run home so frightened that his heart pounded wildly and both temples flamed scarlet; he had very nearly burst into tears. He hurried to his room, sat down, and steadied his spirit. But after a while his heart went back to Qinguan: after such a storm of insult, who knew to what state rage had brought him? He wished to go over to see him, but feared to meet those two again; and after wavering a long while, still unable to set his mind at rest, he sent someone ahead to look. The coast being clear, he hastened across in a few quick steps. In Qinyan's room the blue gauze bed-curtains hung lowered, and from behind them came a sound of sobbing. Sulan lifted the curtain and patted Qinyan: "Up with you! Good news has come. Put away your anger for now — here is a letter for you to read." Qinyan turned over, and at the sight of Sulan only grieved the more. He heaved a sigh and said: "I am going to die in any case. To live on and suffer like this — death were cleaner. Brother Lan, you are my great benefactor. Since we have loved each other so well, be my friend to the very end. When I am dead, beg Duxiang for me to bury my bones beneath the plum trees of Meiyan in Yiyuan; then even as a ghost I shall be happy. Or else burn me to ashes, and where the mountains are high and the waters deep scatter them down the wind, and spare the world one wretched relic of an ill-fated life — so that none who think of me need hate, and none need ache. Brother Lan, Brother Lan! You love me — then let me die, and do not dissuade me. I am but sixteen, and already weary of living. Born from childhood into a poor man's house, and made a singer of plays, I have drunk every shame and insult. I cannot tell what Heaven means to do with me — if it wants my life, let it be quick about it. Why must it trample a man so?"`,"So saying, he burst into loud weeping, and his words set Sulan weeping too. He had meant to console him; but hearing such talk, with those two men fresh before his eyes, he felt sorrier for him the more he thought, and so wept on with him and could not stop.",`The two of them wept face to face for a full hour. Qinyan, seeing Sulan grieve so on his account, was filled with gratitude; he took Sulan's hand and wept afresh. Sulan, feeling Qinyan clasp him as he cried, knew it was meant in thankfulness, and thought: "With such talent and such beauty, Qinyan meets with such torment — then Heaven and Earth are jealous indeed of all who have talent and beauty. I, Sulan, count first or second among the flowers myself; if Heaven and Earth should grow jealous of me too, and grind me with torments like these, I should be even as Yunong — and at such an hour, perhaps, without one intimate friend to comfort me."`,`And he thought further: "Those two chasing me out with curses just now — that was the first time in all my life. Who knows what mischief may yet spring from it?" At this his heart wound through its nine turns, and he gave way and wept aloud facing Qinyan, till the whole household took alarm and came crowding in to stand about, dazed, not daring to offer comfort, knowing well it was over the day's ugly business. Two of them at last came forward to soothe them, and when each had somewhat quieted, went out and fetched two basins of water for their faces and steeped two bowls of tea, then withdrew. The two, though their weeping had ceased, could bring out no words, but still sat swallowing sobs, their eyes brimming. After another long pause, Lu Sulan spoke: "What happened today I saw with my own eyes, and I am grieved to death for you. That fellow looks like some country clod — only how did it all begin? Do you know where he is from?" Qinyan waited a moment, and answered, still weeping: "No one here knows them. They say the pair looked utterly savage — who can tell where they came from, storming in for no reason at all? It is only my bitter fate: such fiends and demons are written in my destiny." Sulan, whose mind was quick, pondered a while and said: "To my eye those two look like carriage-drivers from some great house — or some Third Master's men. What if it were that Wei fellow who set them on? It is not impossible." Qinyan said: "Who can say? But what quarrel has Wei Pincai with me, that he should send men to raise a row?" Then, thinking again, he saw it all at once: "Yes — yes! It must be Wei Pincai's doing. Otherwise there is no sense in men walking in at the gate and cursing without cause or reason."`,`"But this dog Wei — what hatred does he bear me, that he must trample me so and hound me to my death?" Sulan said: "I told you the other day to be on your guard and not to offend him — and see, he has struck the first blow." Then he added: "Though after all this is only our guessing, and can't be taken for certain." Qinyan said nothing, staring blankly; then: "I shall be dead soon in any case. Let anything more come — I am not afraid of it." Sulan said: "Now you are talking folly. Life and death are written in fate — would you go seeking death yourself? Besides, if you truly died, you would drag another down with you, who would die too." Qinyan said: "I have no father or mother, no brothers, no sisters — whom should I drag down? I go clean, and I go alone." Sulan said: "No one else would be dragged down, true; many love you, but not to the point of dying for you. How is it that today of all days you cannot call Yuxiang to mind — would he not die for you? Look, then — whose hand wrote this?" And he handed Ziyu's reply to Qinyan, who took the letter, glanced at it, and laid it down, saying: "This is a letter from someone to Master Xu — why show it to me?" Sulan laughed: "Not so fast — that is the envelope. Look what is written inside." Qinyan could only draw the letter out. He read it from beginning to end, then began at the top again, reciting it sentence by sentence; read it through once more, and then smiled faintly: "Is this not Yuxiang's reply to you? He is to see the canal tomorrow — and by this letter he is certain to come." Sulan said: "Would you rather he came, or rather he did not?" Qinyan smiled again and answered: "It is you who invited him. Only who knows whether the weather will hold tomorrow. In the fifth month rain and shine are never settled; if it should start pouring at first light, then he cannot come." Sulan laughed: "Heaven bends to man's wishes. The tears we two have shed today may fairly pass for a day's rain — tomorrow is sure to be clear. Sleep soundly tonight, rise early, and come to my place to go together; to your master say only that you are going to Yiyuan. Your health is frail and the weather uncertain — bring an extra garment or two, for the wind may be strong on the water." So they talked on, and little by little the two faces brightened. Sulan took his evening meal with Qinyan and sat talking a while longer; not till past the second watch did he go home, and Qinyan too retired to rest.`,"By overnight the illness had retreated by eight-tenths, but a new ailment appeared: the more he wanted to sleep, the less he could.","He heard the fourth watch strike; then all at once several great gusts of wind came roaring up, and with them a downpour of rain, thunder and lightning interlocked. Qinyan sat up in bed and sighed long and deep. When the cloudburst had passed there lingered still a thin, soughing drizzle, and the thunder went rolling on without pause; only toward daybreak did it cease.",`Qinyan, utterly spent, sank upon his pillow and fell fast asleep after all. He dreamed that Sulan and Ziyu were already in the boat, and he himself just stepping aboard, when suddenly two men came running out upon the bank: one in dark clothes, bare to the waist, queue coiled on his head, rushed up, seized him, and hauled him back, cursing: "You little bastard! Shamming sick by day and seeing no one — and how is it you've run out here now?" Qinyan screamed for help and wrenched his body free with all his strength — and there he was, still on his own bed, startled into a cold sweat, the red sun already filling the window.`,`Then he heard the parrot outside the window begin to talk: "Yesterday's men have come again!" — and it gave Qinyan another great start, thinking those two had indeed come back for him. In fact it was Sulan who, having waited a while without sign of Qinyan, had sent a man to fetch him, with word to his master that they were going together to Yiyuan. Changqing gave his consent and hurried Qinyan up. He washed his face, ate a bowl of iced bird's-nest, had his attendant pick out a few garments and wrap them in a bundle to take on the cart, took his leave of Changqing, and went straight to Sulan's.`,`On seeing Sulan he asked: "Did you settle with Yuxiang yesterday that he should come here?" Sulan said: "I arranged for him to go straight to the boat. And lest he fail to find it, I sent a man besides, passing as one of the Yiyuan people, to guide him there; by this time he is surely aboard before us. I am only waiting until the wine and all the gear are complete, so that nothing runs short at the last moment — then we shall be off too." Observe what a man this Sulan was: precise and clever both, very nearly the match of Huifang, wanting only Huifang's ruthless touch that gulled Pan San; set against the general run of men, he might well be called deep in wit and full of stratagem.`,`As for Ziyu, from the moment he lay down at the second watch he could scarcely wait for morning; and when he heard that storm of rain he sighed short and long and cursed his luck, fearing that if the morning brought more of the same, his mother would never let him leave the house. He rose and opened the window to look at the sky, just as the south wind rose in force and drove the rain straight in. Looking up he saw black clouds like ink, and where the lightning split them, golden serpents flickering. Then came a thunderclap that shook the very corners of the house. He shut the window in haste, trimmed the lamp, and sat alone. By good fortune the rain stopped at daybreak, though the eaves went on dripping, now broken, now resumed, for a good while after. It was too late by then to sleep again, so he woke Yun'er. Day had fully come, and the red sun was on the point of rising. He washed his face, drank his tea, took a few refreshments, and went to the upper rooms, where Lady Yan was not yet risen. Ziyu had a maid dress his hair in the outer room and came out again; everywhere all was still and quiet. Returning to his study, he thought: "Sulan has shown me such feeling, and I have put him to trouble again and again. He may set no store by such things, but I cannot rest easy on it. If I gave him silver he might well take offense, as though I held him cheap; best send him a few things instead."`,`So he opened his chests and picked out several of the odd trifles that kinsmen and friends had given him over the years, adding two bolts of Jiangnan silk and two bolts of Huzhou silk, and took along a dozen-odd taels of loose silver. He had just put all in order and was about to go to the upper rooms to take his leave when Li Yuanmao, a short jacket thrown over his shoulders and his feet bare, came bustling in all in a flurry: "I made a point of rising early today — who'd have thought you would be up earlier still!" Ziyu said: "I have business abroad today, so I am somewhat early." Yuanmao said: "There is a matter I want to talk over with you." Ziyu was about to ask what, when Yun'er came in: "Master Xu has sent a man to say the guests are all assembled, and requests the young master to come over now." Ziyu, with no more time for Yuanmao, hurried in to the upper rooms, where Lady Yan was at her toilet, and announced his outing. Lady Yan said: "You have been better these last days, and an airing will do you good. Only come home early; don't go courting the cool or sitting in a draft. Take a few more people with you, and pack an extra garment or two." Ziyu replied: "The clothes are packed already; and I don't need many people — Yun'er alone will do." Lady Yan said: "As you please; but mind you come back early, and be careful what you eat and drink." Ziyu answered "Yes!", went out, dressed, and had the gifts and clothing-bundles carried out to the cart beforehand.`,`He was on the very point of leaving when Li Yuanmao once more planted himself in the way: "Not so fast! I have a pressing matter that must be talked over." Ziyu said impatiently: "What is it then? Out with it!" Yuanmao rubbed his eyes, gave a yawn, and hemmed and hawed without bringing anything out. Ziyu said: "Well? If you have something to say, say it short and quick — someone is waiting for me at the gate. Speak!" Yuanmao said: "Who's waiting for you? Such a hurry! It's still early yet — listen, the seller of sweet congee hasn't even cried his wares past the door, and here you are in a fluster. What for?" Ziyu was growing truly weary of it: "You said you had a matter to discuss; asked, you will not speak, but run on instead with idle chatter without end. What is it, once and for all?" Yuanmao said: "These days I am poor past bearing. I want to borrow a few strings of cash of you — there, that's the whole of it." Ziyu said: "And was that worth so much ceremony? Speak to the accounts room — it comes to the same thing." And he made to go; but Yuanmao caught hold of him: "Good fellow, good fellow — you must send Yun'er to say a word for me. I have borrowed of the accounts room before, and am ashamed to go again for fear of a snub." There was no help for it: Ziyu called Yun'er in once more and sent him to speak to the accounts room; and when word came back that it was agreed, Yuanmao at last let Ziyu go.`,`What with all this entanglement, his watch showed the first quarter of the si hour. Ziyu hurried onto the cart and made for the Great East Gate. The way was long, and by the time he was out of the city it was the beginning of the wu hour. Sulan had long since arrived ahead of him, and had besides posted a man at the head of the road to watch. Before long, catching sight of Ziyu's cart approaching, he came up the bank in his neat and elegant dress to welcome him as he alighted, paying his respects and asking after his health. They boarded the boat together, where Sulan relieved Ziyu of his hat and outer robe, and himself changed into informal dress. Ziyu thanked him: "I am deeply touched by your elegant kindness, so complete in every point; my lingering ailment lifts from me at a stroke, and my breast is opened wide. Receiving such generous feeling, how am I ever to repay it?"`,`Sulan smiled: "My poor services have fallen short. As luck would have it, Yunong's illness has taken a turn for the worse today, and he cannot come out. Yesterday, besides, two ruffians gave him a savage tongue-lashing, and rage has made him iller still. Having invited you yesterday, I could hardly send round today to cry off; so we two must simply sit together a while, and then part. With Yunong so ill, my own spirits are none of the best. In short, 'good things are ever crossed' — there is not a hair's error in the saying." These few words fell upon Ziyu like ice-water poured over his body. He was silent, staring blankly at Sulan a long while; then he sighed and said: "So today's affair has indeed turned out thus — not a jot beyond what I foresaw. Xiangwan, the pity is only that you have spent your pains for nothing: a luckless man like me is not permitted to enjoy them. You do not know how last night, because of that storm of rain, I lay amid a thousand griefs and ten thousand cares, knowing already that today I should never see Yunong. That I forced myself to come notwithstanding was, first, for this beautiful kindness of yours, which must not be betrayed; and second, because Heaven sometimes deals in winds and clouds past all reckoning, and something unlooked-for might yet fall out — who could say? But no: in this world the thing one longs for is not to be had for any dreaming, while the thing one dreads comes at the first thought. That Yunong could not come I had long foreseen; what I cannot tell is whether Yunong at this moment guesses that I came out, or guesses that I did not. If he guesses I did not come, well and good; but if he guesses I did, then I fear the heaviness on him at this hour outweighs mine by no small measure."`,`As he spoke his eyes had already reddened, and he shook his head, saying: "It is strange — it is strange indeed." Sulan could scarcely keep from laughing, and said to him: "Come, let us go together now and find Yunong, and see how his illness does." Ziyu considered a moment and said: "Better not. Since I cannot meet him even here, some fresh hindrance would be sure to spring up there, and I should not see him even so." Sulan said: "He lies sick in his bed — how should you fail to see him?" Ziyu said: "The other day when you and I went together — was not Yunong sick in his bed then? Afterward I went twice more, and never saw him. If I go again today, I most surely shall not see him." At this point his tears fell, and he went on: "Yunong! Yunong! Between you and me, it seems, there was fate for that one meeting only." Then, turning to Sulan: "I, for my part, can see through it and think it through; only you must persuade him to see through it too, and think it through too — it will spare him many a sorrow." Sulan laughed: "So now you are grown enlightened! But suppose Yunong had come out today, sick as he is, to see you — would you still see through it, or would you not? If you truly saw through it, you would naturally speak your mind plainly to him, and thenceforth neither side need pine for the other. If you cannot see through it, then each will go on longing as before. At this moment, not having met him, you think with perfect clearness; I fear that once you meet, your thinking will cloud again." Ziyu had no word to answer. Sulan laughed once more: "Since Yunong could not come himself, he has found a substitute to meet you — will you see him or no?" Ziyu asked: "What sort of person is he? Does he know me?" Sulan said: "One of our own troupe, whose face is much like Yunong's. Yunong means no more by it than that you should gaze at plums to quench your thirst. What do you say — shall I have him out?"`,`Ziyu pondered a while, then said: "If he is unlike Yunong, I might well meet him; but if he is like Yunong — I committed affront enough that day at Yiyuan; why set the maid to ape her mistress a second time? Not only am I unwilling to see this person: for Yunong's own sake I could not bear it. Xiangwan, you are a man of understanding and will surely see my meaning — this is no affectation of nicety in me." Sulan said: "What you say is all very well; but though you will not see him, I mean to call him out all the same." Ziyu was still trying to prevent him when Sulan called out from the rear cabin a figure lovely as flower and jade. At first sight Ziyu was in some confusion: for one thing, he had conversed with Qinyan but a single time; for another, these past months had worn Qinyan's beauty sadly thin. Once he had been the very glory of the nation and fragrance of heaven, lucent, full, and splendid; now he was like fallen petals that say nothing, a figure grown pale as the chrysanthemum. But by the time Ziyu saw him clearly, Qinyan had already hidden his face in delicate weeping, his ice-silk sleeve soaked through, and sat turned away, doing nothing but cry. Ziyu said: "This is strange — is this not Yunong himself? Xiangwan, why did you spin me that tale?" Sulan said: "Be sure you are not mistaken — is it he, or is it not?" Ziyu said: "How should it not be? Only somewhat wasted. That immortal maid of Miaogushe — could any common mortal counterfeit her?" Then he said: "Yunong, you need grieve no longer; all that is in your heart I know." Before he had finished, Qinyan checked his weeping and said: "Is your illness mended? I know you came to me several times; but I never once saw you, and so could hardly go to you. Only yesterday, when I read your letter to Xiangwan, did I understand it all at last — it is I who have done you harm." And with that he fell to weeping again. Ziyu said: "I have no great illness — only a certain unease of body. And I know how to take care of myself. If you too will only see things a little more lightly, mending will come easily." And his own tears ran down as he spoke. Qinyan said: "Had Xiangwan not come over yesterday, I should be dead by now, and you would not have seen me today." And he wept anew. Ziyu could not fathom what he meant, and only watched Qinyan — like a pear-blossom laden with rain, so soft and tender he seemed ready to fall.`,"And seeing how with every phrase he spoke came a fresh sob, Ziyu felt a rush of bitterness drive straight up through his heart, and could hold back no longer, but wept too. It ended with Sulan quite at a loss: watching the two of them so desolate — for all the world like parting at death or separation in life — before he knew it he was weeping with them.",`The three wept in one knot, till at last it was Sulan who first came to himself and urged them: "I asked you here today for a day of pleasure — why all this sobbing and wailing? Half the day is gone already, and before evening we must race for the city. We have but a few hours left for gladness; better we should all laugh a little." Ziyu forced himself to assent: "Xiangwan is quite right; Yunong, grieve no more." Qinyan said: "And what is there to laugh over? From our one meeting at Yiyuan to this day is five months. For a second glad meeting we may well wait a year; and within that year, who knows whether I can last to see it? Most likely this hour ends it all." So saying, he wept again. Ziyu comforted him: "No matter — only let your health mend, and we may meet every day; why speak of a year?" Qinyan wept on: "Mend I might; only I fear this Wei Pincai will not suffer me to mend — nothing short of my death will content him." Ziyu was startled: "You must not wrong Wei Pincai there — behind your back he praises you to the skies." Qinyan stamped his foot: "You do not know yet! If he spoke well of me, he would not have spread slanders about you, nor sent men to raise a riot at my door." Ziyu, quite in the dark, asked again: "Of all this I understand nothing. How has Pincai made slanders? And how came he to raise a riot?" Qinyan said: "Ask him, and you will know." Then Sulan recounted in detail all that Pincai had said that day, till Ziyu's face turned ashen and rage left him speechless. After a long pause he said: "Strange! Strange! He lived half a year under my roof, and I never once offended him — why must he trample me to such a pass? And how comes it that entering Duke Hua's mansion has turned him wicked? It passes all dreaming. Henceforth I have done with him, that is all. But this sending of men to raise a riot — what was that?" Sulan and Qinyan, hearing that Pincai had entered Duke Hua's mansion, understood at last that the row at the Chunyang Restaurant had been his doing too — and then yesterday's affair no longer left room for doubt. Sulan then recounted also the two men's cursing of the day before, and how he himself had been chased. Ziyu, hearing it, cursed and raged by turns, and could not help weeping once more.`,"By this time the boat was under way, and Sulan's servants set out the wine and dishes. Sulan poured the wine, urging and coaxing; Ziyu and Qinyan could only take their seats, but with sorrow flooding up in them they sat wordless, face to face — what heart had they left for drinking? Under Sulan's patient pressing they forced down a few cups, but it was gaiety worn as a mask, and they scarcely knew why they kept up the pretense. In Qinyan's heart the feeling would not leave him that this was a parting of the living from the dying — this one meeting, and after it, no more. Ziyu likewise felt that fate had set itself against them, and made certain they could not often meet. So both hearts, thinking their way to the very end of things, bred sorrow of themselves — in this all human hearts are alike; only in feeling do some go to the uttermost and some fall short.","By now the boat had traveled three or four li; the three sat quietly, drinking clear wine for a time. As Ziyu held his cup, he gazed at Qinyan, who appeared like a rose washed in dew, or a peony veiled in mist—truly a figure of the same immortal stripe as Prince Qiao or Master Shi. Merely to sit beside him felt like a profound karmic blessing, far beyond vulgar fortune. He then looked at Sulan, who possessed a different kind of loveliness—graceful in bearing, refined and elegant in every gesture—truly a perfect match of pearl and jade. This gathering today felt like a once-in-a-lifetime chance; sudden joy welled up, and their sorrows gradually dissolved. Qinyan, in turn, saw Ziyu as a tree of jade and coral, an autumn moon in an ice-pure vessel; his deep and tender affection was unlike anyone else's. He was indeed the powdered He Yan, the incense-scented Xun Yu; let alone the elegant Wang and Xie clans, even a whole flock of Black-Clad youths could hardly match him. If he could only form a bond of incense with him, to be confidants of flowers and moonlight, it would be something unachievable even in several lifetimes of cultivation. Even if they only had this one or two fleeting encounters, his heart was deeply satisfied. Gradually, their gazes flowed and mingled, warming their icy hearts.","Sulan watched the two of them facing each other, words forgotten but feelings encompassing and complete. Their brows were silent yet wishing to speak; their eyes just met then pulled away. They were truly a perfect pair, their hearts tied in a single knot, as if putting all the gifted scholars and beautiful ladies in the world to shame. No wonder this one pined morning and night, while that one forgot food and sleep. Since both were born with such qualities, heaven was naturally jealous, decreeing they must part often and meet rarely. If they were allowed to be together every day, it would not show heaven's intent to cherish and hide them away. Feeling deep admiration, Sulan walked over and sat at Ziyu's side, warmly and gently offering him three cups of wine, then offering one to Qinyan. At this moment, the deep affection among the three was perfectly complete; it was as if they had become the Boundless Celestials of the Pure Land. They only wished Duke Yang of Lu would swing his halberd in fierce battle once more, forcing the red sun to roll backward and refusing to let it set.","Just at the height of this fullness of happiness, a boat was seen approaching from ahead; at a distance came the sound of silk-string and wind instruments.",`Listening again, they heard hurrying pipes and thronging strings, and wanton, garish songs. In a little while the boat came rowing past, and Ziyu, peering through the cabin blind, saw three men in it, blowing and drumming away at full blast, every one of them stripped to the skin; one held a xiaodan in his arms, and the pair were drinking mouth-about from one cup, while two more xiaodan sat at the side, one plucking the strings, the other singing. The din of their merriment was like quick thunder bursting from the earth, their wild laughter like a torrent racing down the rapids — so that Qinyan in alarm made to hide in the rear cabin, and Ziyu let down the boat window, wondering who they might be. Sulan, looking through the crack of the window, said to Qinyan: "Come and see." Qinyan came and peeped in his turn, then said: "Those vulgar louts — what is there to look at?" Sulan pointed to the one seated in the lower place: "That is the Pan San who kept pestering Meixiang." Qinyan cried: "Aiyo! Such a creature — a wonder Meixiang could endure his acquaintance; and however did he contrive to gull him?" Sulan said: "You did not see them — yesterday's two were ten times fiercer than he!" Qinyan sighed, walked back, and sat down. Ziyu asked: "What manner of man is this Pan San?" Sulan told over the whole story of them, and Ziyu exclaimed again and again: "Detestable! Detestable! That this Pan San should dare nurse such wild designs! It was lucky he met Su Meixiang; had it been another, the fellow would likely have ruined him." Then he asked Qinyan: "Do you know those xianggong?" Qinyan said: "I know not one of them — what troupe are they?" Sulan said: "I know them all. The one held in his arms is Yumei of the Dengchun troupe; the one at the strings is called Chunlin, and the singer Fenglin — both of the Fengtai troupe." Ziyu said: "Look at them at their pleasures — what pleasure is really in it? And were they to see this scene of ours, no doubt they would call it dreary and flavorless."`,`Sulan laughed: "Each person finds pleasure in their own way — if they did not behave like this, they would not consider it pleasure." Looking on, the red sun was nearing the western horizon. Ziyu's heart at this moment was full of joy — he almost felt the pleasure of having forgotten to return.`,`Qinyan knew in his heart that the day was growing late, yet could not bear to hurry the parting. Sulan, fearing that if it grew later they could not enter the city, called to the boatmen to ply their oars faster, for the hour pressed; and meanwhile they began to pack up. Ziyu then divided the things he had brought between the two of them, and they, unable to refuse, accepted. Ziyu also shared out the loose silver from his bundle in rewards to Sulan's and Qinyan's servants, saying, "You have all had a weary day"; and the company kowtowed their thanks for the bounty.`,`The boat came to the Great East Gate, and they went each to his own cart. Ziyu held Qinyan's hand and said: "We shall meet again another day; in all things you must learn to console yourself" — and his tears fell once more. Qinyan answered, choking: "Go, and set your heart at rest; the gates will soon be closing. When we meet, it will be either at Xiangwan's or at Yiyuan — one place or the other." Ziyu nodded, and could only harden his heart as each mounted his cart. The carters, fearing the hour, laid on a lash apiece, and away they rattled at a gallop.`,"Ziyu returned home; the lamps were already lit. Lady Yan asked where he had been; he could only give a vague answer to put her off. As for what happened next, listen to the following chapter to find out."]},u=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations22:a},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),t={23:["On the very day Ziyu set out to tour the canal, Li Yuanmao came to borrow money from him. Shortly after, the accounts room brought out eight strings of large cash; upon receiving them, Yuanmao felt his heart blossom with unrestrained joy. Yet he thought to himself, 'This heavy cash is cumbersome to carry on my person; paper notes would be far more convenient.' He thus ordered his page boy, Wang Bao, to take five strings of cash to the tobacco and money shop at the alley entrance and exchange them for ten paper notes. Yuanmao meticulously counted them one by one, tucked them into his areca-nut pouch, and fastened it to the lapel of his robe. Unable even to wait for a meal, he immediately took Wang Bao and went out to seek his uncle, Sun Sihui. As it happened, Sihui was not at home; Siyuan invited him in, they conversed for a while, and Siyuan kept him for a simple meal. Yuanmao and Siyuan had never much seen eye to eye, and since Siyuan frequently contradicted him, Yuanmao did not linger long. Taking his leave of Siyuan, he wandered aimlessly, his heart unable to forget that woman playing the pipa he had seen before.","Wandering until he reached East Garden, he saw that before every door still stood a throng of people. He casually glanced at two or three places—some with two or three people sitting, others with clusters of three or five—all coquettish and alluring, putting on airs and striking poses. He looked and looked again, but nowhere could he find the woman who had played the pipa. Yuanmao's judgment in such matters was naturally lacking, unable to distinguish the exquisite from the vulgar, yet he yearned to venture inside for a closer look. However, intimidated by the crowds and being a complete novice, his mind wavered, and he dared not enter. Looking further toward another doorway, he noticed only two people standing there. Approaching the threshold, he saw a man lower his head as he emerged from the house and walk straight away. Yuanmao's heart burned with an unbearable itch; he pressed his body close to the door, placing one foot upon the threshold, and gazed at a woman of some thirty years. She was full-figured, with a pile of dark hair like black clouds; though her complexion was somewhat coarse, her cheeks flushed with a vibrant rosy hue. Endowed with a pair of fine, expressive eyes, she cast liquid glances this way and that. This sight ignited a raging fire within Li Yuanmao. He could only bend his waist, prop his elbows upon his knees, rest his chin in his hands, put on his spectacles, and scrutinize the woman in detail. The woman smiled as she looked back at Li Yuanmao, finding him far more presentable and clean than the usual crowd: his head shaved perfectly smooth, his brow broad and full like a purple-glazed alms bowl, his stature neither too tall nor too short, with a rounded waist and sturdy back. He wore a fresh shirt of white spun silk and new satin boots upon his feet, while half an areca-nut pouch peeked from his lapel. She nodded to him and said, 'Won't you please come inside and have a cup of tea?' Yuanmao's heart pounded wildly; he longed to enter yet dared not answer. The woman smiled again, 'Don't be bashful. Look at all the people coming and going every day—just come right in!' Yuanmao's face had already swelled flush red. The woman laughed once more, 'I suppose that little head of yours has never crossed the red threshold to taste meat; you're still a vegetarian.' The two men outside the door burst into laughter, and one tugged at Yuanmao's clothes. Yuanmao turned his head to see a man in his thirties, wearing a short jacket of white cotton, his hair tied in a 'Victory' topknot, holding a small sparrowhawk. The man said with a beaming smile, 'The little wife invites you in, so go on in. What are you afraid of? I'll close the door for you, and no one will see a thing.'","Li Yuanmao muttered something unintelligible. The man, not hearing him clearly, continued: 'If you wish to go in, just walk in boldly. We are all friends here; I will guard the door for you, and I guarantee no one will come. When you come out, treat me to four ounces of wine and a bowl of braised pork noodles, and that will seal our friendship. If you haven't the money, it doesn't matter. It's just a bit of fun—what's the big deal?' Saying this, he let out a hearty laugh. The other man, wearing a blue cotton jacket, chimed in: 'Your skin is too thin! What are you afraid of? If you want to play, play. Three or four hundred cash is more than enough; where else could you find a cheaper bargain?' Assailed by their barrage of words, Yuanmao felt an unbearable itch in his heart. Hearing they would guard the door, he felt even more assured, and asked, 'Is it truly alright to go in? I don't know how to lie, and it truly is my first time; I feel terribly embarrassed.' The man with the hawk smiled, 'Why wouldn't it be alright?' He then gave Yuanmao a shove, pushing him through the door, casually pulled it shut, and latched it from the outside, saying, 'Don't panic. With us here, just relax and enjoy yourself.' Yuanmao squinted his eyes, still not daring to step forward. The woman stood up and said, 'My good boy, don't put on such airs. I haven't even tasted the mutton, yet I've already caught the stench.' With that, she lifted the straw curtain and went into the inner room. Just then, from the back of the house emerged a man in his forties, his short hair standing on end, bare-backed, with a grimy checkered towel draped over his shoulder. Looking utterly filthy, he stretched out a hand to Yuanmao and demanded, 'Count out the money!' Yuanmao was taken aback; having come this far, he could not back out. His face flushed red as he said, 'I didn't bring any cash.' The man retorted, 'If you brought no cash, why did you wander in here? You can't play for free.' Yuanmao replied, 'I only have paper notes.' The man said, 'Notes will do just as well; just use your notes.' Left with no choice, Yuanmao reached into the pouch at his lapel and pulled out a note—a one-string denomination. He thought to himself, 'That man just said it would only cost three or four hundred cash. Won't this one-string note be a bargain for him?'","He therefore said to the man, 'This note is for one string of cash. Just bring me the change for whatever you owe me.' The man gave a smirk, glanced at the note, stuffed it into a large leather drawer, and disappeared into the back once more.","His courage now bolstered, Li Yuanmao lifted the curtain and stepped inside, instantly feeling assaulted by a pungent, overwhelming odor. He saw the woman sitting upon the heated brick *kang*. On it lay a simple straw mat and a red pillow, with a long wooden bench placed beside it.","In a daze, Yuanmao sat down upon the bench. From a clay pot on the *kang* stove, the woman poured a cup of lukewarm tea and handed it to him with a giggling laugh. She then pulled out a wooden basin from a hollow in the wall behind the *kang*. Someone on the other side took it, filled it halfway with water, and placed it back into the hollow. The woman retrieved the basin, crouched down, slipped off her undergarments, and casually splashed the water beneath herself a couple of times. Yuanmao listened to the sloshing sounds, watched as she wiped herself with a dry cloth, pushed the basin aside, and then lay flat on her back upon the *kang*. Stretching out one leg, she smiled at Yuanmao and said, 'Hurry up, come here!' Seeing this, Yuanmao's lust flared like wildfire. He hastily stripped off his long robe, tossed it onto the bench, twisted his body, and clambered onto the *kang*. The woman did not undress; she merely rolled up one trouser leg. Panting heavily, Yuanmao knelt upon the *kang*, lifted that leg, and rested it upon his shoulder. He brought his face close to examine the spot intimately, looking again and again, like a bearded man who had drunk milk tea and failed to wipe his mouth, reaching out a hand to touch it. Seeing his ridiculous demeanor, the woman spat in disdain and said, 'You fool, if you want to play, play. What are you staring at? Even your own wife has one of these; your staring is making me angry.' Yuanmao was just about to press forward when a loud crash echoed from outside, sounding as if the street gate had been burst open. A cacophony of shouting and clamor filled the courtyard, surging right up to the curtain. The woman hastily shoved Yuanmao aside, sat up, pulled on her trouser leg, scrambled off the *kang*, and rushed out through the curtain.","Left behind, Li Yuanmao was frightened out of his wits. He hurriedly tucked in his trousers and was just about to put on his clothes when a violent brawl erupted outside the curtain, sounding like falling blossoms and flowing water. A crowd of people surged in to watch, squeezing so hard the curtain fell to the floor. Panicking, Yuanmao found nowhere to hide—he could not squeeze under the *kang*, nor could he burrow into the wall hollow. He felt desperately trapped, with no way up to heaven and no door down into the earth. The shouting drew closer, and looking closely, he recognized the two men from before. The one in the blue cotton jacket seemed to have lost the fight and fled into the room. Yuanmao panicked even more. The man with the hawk followed closely behind, and the two tangled in a chaotic scuffle. Two more men rushed in from outside to break them up, but without distinguishing friend from foe, they seized Yuanmao and pinned him ruthlessly to the ground, leaving him unable to move a muscle. He only saw four men and eight hands strip him utterly bare, before they scattered in a sudden uproar. Stripped stark naked, Yuanmao was at least spared a beating; he had hoped to enact the clouds and rain of the sun terrace, but instead found himself emerging from a hot spring bath. Slowly, he crawled up from the floor, entirely devoid of clothing, tears streaming down his face, unable to step outside.","The little wife and the money-grubbing man had vanished without a trace, drawing wave after wave of onlookers from outside, pointing and laughing. Some said, 'What a chaotic mess!' Others remarked, 'This is the retribution for whoring.' Helpless, Yuanmao could only wrap the fallen straw curtain around his lower half, squat in the room, and shout loudly for Wang Bao. It turned out that Wang Bao, being only thirteen or fourteen, had understood what was happening when Yuanmao went inside and had run off to play. When he saw the men fighting their way in, he knew trouble had struck, but dared not step forward, hiding behind a tree in terrified tears. Seeing the crowd disperse and hearing his master's cries, he hurried inside. At the sight of Yuanmao's state, he stood dumbfounded and asked, 'Young master, how will you get back?' Yuanmao replied, 'Hurry back and fetch my clothes, shoes, socks, and trousers! You must not breathe a word of this to anyone. Even if someone asks, do not answer them. Quick, quick! When I return, I will reward you with twenty cash to buy pastries. Fly as fast as you can!' Wang Bao dashed off and returned shortly after with a bundle of clothes. Yuanmao discarded the straw curtain, put on his trousers first, and dressed piece by piece, emerging once again looking immaculate and bright. The idle onlookers pointed and laughed at him. Feigning dignity, Yuanmao walked slowly, turning back to say, 'You audacious slaves! You may hide now, but in a moment I will have people come arrest you and send you to the Ward Warden's office; I fear you will pay me back double.' Pitiable Li Yuanmao—his money, notes, and clothes were worth twenty or thirty strings of cash, which was of little consequence, but to suffer such immense humiliation and terror just as his lust burned like fire, one could only fear that some inner illness might arise from the shock. He truly was a man of supreme misfortune.","In truth, those two men and the woman were accomplices. Such places were never frequented by decent folk; the patrons were usually cart drivers, coal porters, and the like. Seeing Li Yuanmao's foolish, absent-minded demeanor and obvious inexperience, combined with his fresh, expensive clothes, they guessed he carried silver and notes, and indeed targeted him for a lucrative swindle. Having suffered this torment, Yuanmao could not tell a soul, finding nowhere to air his grievances. Yet among the crowd watching the commotion, there were those who recognized Yuanmao, knowing he resided at the Mei mansion and was the prospective son-in-law of Department Director Sun. The tale slowly spread. Later, when Wang Bao accidentally broke a teacup and was slapped twice by Yuanmao, the resentful boy recounted the events of that day to the servants, and the story circulated further until even Sun Lianggong heard whispers of it. Naturally, after the marriage, he would take his son-in-law to task—but that is a story for later.","Meanwhile, Sun Sihui had gone out that day to seek a relative, a cousin-in-law named Ji Liangxuan, a native of Jinkui County in Changzhou Prefecture, Jiangsu. Ji had long worked as a legal and financial secretary, earning hefty salaries, but due to his unsavory character, his reputation had grown notorious, making people wary of him. His scholarship was mediocre, and he relied solely on flattery. With his ill repute spreading, he could no longer secure a post in the nearby provinces. Forced to bring silver and goods to the capital, he hoped to curry favor with high-ranking officials and land a lucrative assignment. Relying on his distant ties to Sun Lianggong, he sent a lavish gift, begging for his patronage and recommendation. Having been in the capital for two months, he had managed to acquaint himself with a few people, acting with utmost obsequiousness and unctuous humility.","Speaking of legal secretaries, there are indeed true scholars among them, and they cannot be universally condemned. There is a class of men born to letters, of excellent learning, who pass the provincial examinations but fail the metropolitan ones; growing older, they turn to the secretarial path. Such men are compassionate and worldly, treating others with righteousness, handling affairs with all their heart and strength. Inwardly they protect their patron's reputation; outwardly they guard against the censure of the common folk—upright and luminous, without a trace of negligence. If they later prosper and purchase official rank, they make fine officials, capable of doing good deeds and bringing real benefit to the people. Being inherently astute, they are not easily deceived. Yet such noble secretaries make up barely two in ten. As for those inferior, corrupt secretaries, whether their posts are large or small, they are equally despicable—shrugging their shoulders, flashing fawning smiles, and relentlessly scheming for advancement. They call their patrons 'Old Uncle' and swear brotherhood with the gatekeepers. Once they secure a post, they gather a clique of fox-friends and dog-associates: relatives form one faction, family friends another, fellow townsmen yet another. They squeeze out those they dislike and recommend their cronies. When they manage to place one or two allies, they sit back and take their cut of the illicit spoils. Caring nothing for local customs or the compatibility of host and guest, they care only for the size of their stipend. They demand eighteen hundred taels from the start, refusing anything less, entirely heedless of whether they possess the competence to manage the affairs.","Once installed in the yamen, they merely treat past cases as sacred precedent and view the clerks' work as their guiding model. They drag in a couple of idle, impoverished friends to handle the copying and writing, while they themselves bask in comfort and dignity, not exerting their minds in the slightest. Donning a few sets of new clothes, riding in a grand sedan chair, and wearing stiff-toed square boots, they pass themselves off as perfectly respectable gentlemen. But when the time comes that they lose their post, they are reduced to panic-stricken chickens. There is yet another, utterly useless sort: unable to feed themselves, having read only half the Four Books and knowing nothing of history beyond the current dynasty, when driven to utter destitution, they devise a new livelihood. They host a banquet to formally acknowledge a master and learn the secretarial trade, henceforth eating and living at their master's expense. Yet they cannot remain dutiful, indulging in eating, drinking, whoring, gambling, and swindling. As soon as they enter a yamen, they don official robes and ride in sedan chairs. One hears their father-in-law is nothing but a cook in the county magistrate's kitchen. This Mr. Ji was likely exactly this sort of man.","On this day, Sun Sihui invited him to dine and listen to an opera. First, they enjoyed a performance by the Fengtai Troupe, then brought along the actor Fenglin, chose a restaurant, and settled into a private elegant room. This Mr. Ji was accompanied by a rather handsome young attendant of about fifteen or sixteen, a native of Huizhou who had been an apprentice in a barber shop, named Ba Laoying.","Seeing his delicate features, Liangxuan had purchased him for ten thousand copper cash, renaming him Yingguan. Dressed up, the boy looked quite charming. By day they maintained the guise of master and servant, but by night he served as a concubine. Upon seeing him, Sihui could not help but drool with desire. The two ordered dishes, and Fenglin offered a few toasts of wine. Ba Yingguan stood behind them, seemingly brimming with resentful anger. Fenglin, being exceptionally perceptive, realized the boy was a fellow practitioner of the trade. He hastily greeted him, asked his surname, and called him 'Second Master Ba' a few times before stepping out, which finally put Ji Liangxuan at ease. Having now seen the young *dan* actors of the capital, Ji felt they were vastly superior to those in the provinces: first in elegance of style, second in the finery of their garments, and third in their skill at socializing and conversation. He therefore said to Sihui, 'The young *dan* of the provinces often possess excellent features, but their dress and grooming carry a rustic air, and they rarely wear proper boots.'","'Take this summer weather, for instance: they wear a simple gown and a cool summer hat, and they come into the yamen in flocks of three or four—they absolutely refuse to come alone. Their fee is casually set at one or two taels, any amount will do.' Sihui remarked, 'So cheap! If one came alone, could I then scale the host's wall and embrace him?'","Liangxuan laughed, 'My dear brother-in-law jokes! How could one scale the host's wall? Even if it is quite late, the second or third watch of the night, the mansion gates can still be called open.' Sihui replied, 'Calling at the gates during the third watch causes such a fuss and commotion; it remains rather inconvenient. Why not open a back door? It would be far more convenient, and no one would be the wiser.' Liangxuan, speaking with solemn earnestness, replied, 'Brother-in-law, you are truly a character! What an absurd joke. For us gentlemen, the first priority is character and conduct. This back door must be blocked absolutely tight, so tight not even a fart could slip out—only then will the patron feel at ease.' Still not understanding, Sihui repeatedly asked the reason. One was spouting absolute nonsense, while the other had a chest utterly devoid of ink; their exchange became a tangled mess of non sequiturs, spiraling into total confusion, until Liangxuan gave up trying to converse with him. Instead, he turned to Fenglin and asked, 'You young gentlemen, how much money can you make in a year?' Fenglin replied, 'The bulk of the money goes to the master. We only keep whatever garments the gentlemen bestow upon us. Only after our apprenticeship is complete does the money become our own.' Liangxuan asked, 'Then how much does the master earn in a year at present?'",`Fenglin said, 'It's hard to say for certain. Averaged over a year, three or four thousand strings of cash is typical.' Liangxuan stuck out his tongue in astonishment: 'That much? That's more than we legal secretaries earn in tuition fees! I truly regret it now; I should have learned to perform theater in the past. It would have been far more joyous than learning the secretarial trade. When we take on apprentices, we lose money feeding them, and if they fail to learn, we can't get rid of them for eight or ten years. Even if we recommend them for a post, they might return in three to six months. How many in a lifetime ever see an apprentice show true filial respect to their master? It truly cannot compare to you young gentlemen!' Saying this, he burst into laughter. Sihui looked at Fenglin and quoted awkwardly: '"The phoenixes fly together, resting in that central forest. Seeing him, my heart is filled with joy."' Fenglin laughed, 'You're showing off your literary flair again! In our troupe, we could certainly use you. The one with the bald, patchy nose who plays the scoundrel looks exactly like you! Why speak in such a mouthful of classical jargon? We know you've read some books.' Liangxuan chuckled, 'This is the scholarly essence of Young Master Sun. If it were us secretaries, when two or three gather, we talk of case files—never straying three sentences from our trade. Just like you playing the young *dan*, why do you always walk with that twisting, swaying gait?' He then asked Sihui, 'Will His Excellency, your father, be able to leave the capital for a post this year?' Sihui replied in garbled classical prose: 'My father has been selected, yet has not undergone the capital evaluation. Not this year; we wait for the next. The burden is heavy and the road is long; it is yet unknown.' Liangxuan asked, 'Is he appointed concurrently as Intendant and Prefect?' Sihui said, 'Of Intendant and Prefect I have not heard; the old gentleman is appointed solely as Prefect.' Liangxuan said, 'A Prefect is akin to an Intendant, and there are many lucrative postings. When His Excellency takes up his glorious post next year, I must beg for your patronage and recommendation.' Sihui replied, 'Naturally, naturally. This position for you, elder brother, is beyond doubt, steady as a massive boulder.' The two chatted and laughed, drinking several cups of wine. Sihui then said, 'Today I see my elder brother possesses a five-foot youth, with beautiful, longing eyes. Should you find yourself alone in the dark of night, does your heart not stir?'`,"This struck a chord in Liangxuan's heart, and he smiled: 'I must give credit to this little boy. He perfectly fulfills two roles: both donkey and concubine.' Sihui exclaimed, 'How extraordinary! What do you mean by donkey and concubine? I wish to hear the details.' Liangxuan explained, 'Right now, I employ him purely as a servant. For example, if you live in the West City and I in the South City, and we need to discuss something, I would normally have to ride a donkey along the city wall to reach you. With him, I just write a letter and send him to deliver it, saving me the walk. Is he not acting as my donkey? When we travelers go out during the day, we pass the time wandering about, but at night, sleeping alone is bitterly lonely. With him around, we can chat and keep each other company. In times of urgent need, he can also relieve that urgency. Can he not be considered a concubine? A monthly wage of eight hundred cash for food and lodging, buying him a few old clothes—it costs less than twenty thousand cash a year. Compared to you calling for young actors, it's the price of barely two or three visits. Is this not a tremendously cheap bargain?'","Sihui replied, 'This matter, I wish to learn of it. Comforting him when he comes, nourishing his back; upon entering the door, the mind is suddenly enlightened. How should one proceed? And all the countrymen say: it is like a great road. I cannot yet believe this; enlighten me clearly, let me attempt it.' Fenglin had no idea what nonsense he was spouting, so he offered him a cup of wine and secretly counted the pockmarks and red rosacea spots on his nose—totaling more than thirty. He asked, 'It would be best if you spoke clearly so people can understand. I truly don't know if this face of yours will ever heal. I have a prescription for you: take ten catties of fragrant wine lees, three catties of lard, one catty of sheep pancreas, four ounces of soap locust, and four ounces of silver nitrate. Spread it all in a steamer and steam it thoroughly. Then press your face against it, wait for the essence of the lees to drill into your skin, and it will draw out all that rotting stench from your face.' Sihui retorted, 'You speak the farts among farts! You must have been steeped in lees yourself. I want to smell if your face carries the fragrance of lees or not.' He pressed his face against Fenglin's and deliberately rubbed it back and forth a few times. Fenglin felt utterly repulsed; his face began to itch, and after scratching it with his fingers, he said, 'Great, you've passed your red ringworm onto me.' He actually scratched a small red welt on his cheek and reached out to pinch Sihui's face. Sihui laughed, 'You say I passed my ringworm to you, but why hasn't it passed before, only passing today? It has not passed; what harm is there?' He then pulled Fenglin onto his lap and said, 'The hare moves slowly, truly capturing my heart.'","Fenglin lightly slapped Sihui's face, glaring at him with wide eyes, 'Others may despise this red nose of yours, but I quite love it.' He then boldly grabbed Sihui's face and rubbed it vigorously, jumped down, and laughed, 'Consider that a hand-job!' Sihui chased after him, intent on twisting his mouth. Fenglin ran out of the room; when Sihui pursued him out, Fenglin slipped back in. Sihui, his mottled face flushed with anger, threatened, 'If I catch you, I will definitely not let you up!' Liangxuan pleaded on his behalf, offering a cup of wine and picking up some food with his chopsticks, and only then did Sihui forgive Fenglin.",`Fenglin offered several more toasts to Liangxuan. Meanwhile, Ba Yingguan, his face flushed with jealousy, paced back and forth under the veranda. Ji Liangxuan called him in to prepare the tobacco pipe, but he ignored him and walked away again. Seeing this, Sihui said, 'Elder brother, earlier I wished to ask for your instruction. I only know that the vast, flowing waters of the Mi River can soothe hunger. As for the taste of steamed piglet, I have yet to taste it, nor do I comprehend its subtle wonders. What benefits does it truly possess? And how does it compare to the union with a wife?' Liangxuan smiled: 'In my view, each has its own unique merits, but everyone often says men are more convenient than women.' Sihui pressed, 'Please expound on the subtleties within, so that I may be thoroughly enlightened.' Liangxuan chuckled: 'This matter can only be sensed intuitively; it is difficult to convey in words, and speaking of it sounds too coarse and vulgar. If I compare it to riding a boat or a cart, it seems the cart is lighter and more convenient. Furthermore, as travelers, we cannot bring our families everywhere; having him is just like having a family. As for the specific flavor, it is the same for everyone, hard to describe fully. There is a couplet that says: "Lean is wide, plump is tight, pockmarked yields much dung; white is wet, yellow is dry, black yields oil." The most exquisite is oil; next is water. As for the inner workings, during the rhythmic breathing of opening and closing, it leaves one's joints numb and tingling, the soul entranced and the spirit bewildered. The marvel of a boat lies entirely in its tossing and rocking. One that cannot toss and rock is no different from a rigid beam. But a cart—with its jolts and bounces—is superior even to the tossing and rocking of a boat. Its supreme wonder lies in being tight, compact, and perfectly compliant.'`,"Before he could finish, Fenglin flushed and interrupted: 'You cart driver, you certainly explain things thoroughly! What kind of cart is yours? Sounds like a covered Henan wagon. I fine you three cups of wine—you are forbidden from speaking further! You speak with such vulgarity.' Sihui exclaimed, 'Splendid, splendid! Truly unheard of! I ride the boat every day, but I can never manage to board the cart. Upon reaching the gate, it seems beyond human strength to pass through; and I fear that if I sit in a dung cart, people will cover their noses as they pass by.' Liangxuan laughed: 'There is a method for that; even a dung cart can be ridden. Take a large piece of wood ear mushroom, soak it in water until soft, use it as your hat, and as the cushion inside the cart—then, no matter the dung cart, it can be ridden.' Sihui rejoiced greatly: 'I am instructed, I am instructed!' Turning to Fenglin, he said, 'I shall ride the cart tomorrow.' Fenglin spat in disgust: 'Stop talking such nonsense. It's getting late, and I have two other places to visit. Let us eat. Otherwise, I will leave first.' Because Ji Liangxuan was drinking with an actor and knew his Ba Yingguan would grow jealous, he dared not fully indulge and urged the food to be served. After eating, they prepared to disperse; Sihui had no choice but to eat. Once everyone finished, Sihui brought out two paper notes totaling five strings of cash, paid Fenglin, adding a domino tile as a tip. They settled the restaurant bill and went their separate ways.","Why, in this chapter, does the narrative dwell purely on such indecent and obscene matters? Is it not a waste of ink? The truth is, the world indeed contains such classes of people who engage in such acts. Moreover, this book is widely said to speak of beauties and renowned scholars who love beauty but are not licentious. Yet how could one know that the paths of righteousness and depravity run parallel without contradiction? To speak only of those who are not licentious, while omitting the few who are supremely licentious, would fail to create a tapestry of five colors or a harmony of eight tones. Therefore, compelled by imaginative speculation, I have fathomed these chaotic hearts; roughly speaking, this is how it must be, and I trust the reader will not harbor doubts. To distinguish the righteous from the depraved, pray listen to the explanation in the following chapter."]},g=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations23:t},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),n={24:[`Our story now turns to Yan Zhongqing and Wang Xun. Idling at home one day, Zhongqing turned to Wang Xun and asked, "Have you heard the flurry of strange rumors going around these past few days?" Wang Xun replied, "My sister-in-law has been unwell of late. I've been waiting on the doctor every day and haven't stepped foot outside." Zhongqing leaned in. "Just yesterday, Zhang Zhongyu was telling me about a certain Pan the Third, who runs a silver shop. Ever since the third month, he has been badgering Su Huifang, insisting on taking him as an adopted son. At first, he dragged Zhang the Second along to pester Meixiang. Left with no choice, Meixiang put on a show of profound hospitality. After lending Pan two hundred strings of cash, she feigned a fishbone caught in her throat to avoid his lecherous 'leather-cup' toast. She then proceeded to drink them both under the table until they were completely insensate, finally having them hauled off to sleep together in the guest room. Pan rolled out of bed, relieved himself into his own boot, and woke in a stupor. When told that Young Master Hua had summoned them away, Pan raged through the night before slinking home in defeat. In the fourth month, he returned to pester them again, only to run headlong into a fake night watch; terrified, he fled, losing a gold bangle in the process." Wang Xun chuckled, "Meixiang is as sharp as a needle. Only Xiangfan could ever tame her. A mere silversmith was destined to be played for a fool." Zhongqing continued, "I also heard that Li Yuanmao made a spectacular fool of himself over in the East Garden." When Wang Xun asked how, Zhongqing elaborated, "Someone spotted him at a sordid brothel. A gang of local thugs burst in and stripped him bare. He was forced to wrap himself in a straw mat, unable to step outside, drawing such a crowd of onlookers that the entire hovel collapsed around him. How he ever made it back home is anyone's guess." Wang Xun frowned. "Could such a tale be true, or is it mere slander?" "Cai Sheng saw it with his own eyes," Zhongqing affirmed. "Zhongyu corroborated it." Wang Xun sighed, "Yuanmao always appeared so honest. How could he stoop to such things?" Zhongqing laughed, "It is precisely the 'honest' men who stumble into such folly; the cunning ones know better. Lately, that bookworm brother-in-law of yours has been leading him entirely astray." Wang Xun laughed in return, "That was your doing! A handsome face hiding a dull wit. If my in-laws ever found out, they would burst with rage." Zhongqing went on, "Then there is Wei Pincai. Ever since he entered the Hua mansion, he has completely changed his tune. He behaves with utter insolence outside. He caused a massive uproar at the Chunyang Restaurant—the proprietor was beaten twenty strokes, and that wasn't even the worst of it. Lu Sulan says Pincai hired a groom and a page boy from the Hua household to ruthlessly bully Qinyan. They hurled abuse for half the day, until Qinyan's people were forced to kowtow, apologize, and pay them several strings of cash just to make them leave." Wang Xun shook his head, "How strange. So many absurdities in a matter of days. We used to think both of them were so refined; truly, faces can deceive." Zhongqing smiled, "But I do have some more uplifting news. Yuxiang spent an entire day drifting along the canal with Qinyan and Sulan. Recently, both of them seem to have recovered from their illnesses." Wang Xun smiled faintly, "Yuxiang revels all on his own now, never inviting us." Zhongqing replied, "It was Sulan who arranged their meeting, allowing them to pour out their lovers' grievances. Outsiders could hardly intrude on such a moment." Wang Xun mused, "I simply cannot fathom the depth of Yuxiang and Qinyan's bond. Where does such profound passion spring from among voluptuaries like us? Surely it cannot be from a single glance. On the sixth day of the New Year, Yuxiang saw Qinyan perform 'Waking from a Dream' without even knowing his name. On the night of the lantern festival at Yiyuan, Yuxiang solved the lute riddle, and Duxiang brought them together. Baozhu mentioned that a fake Qinyan was sent to tease him first, and Yuxiang was so offended he nearly stormed out. Only when the true Qinyan appeared, weeping, were their hearts pledged to one another. Since then, their rare meetings only seemed to breed illness; everyone guessed it was lovesickness. On Meixiang's birthday, Qinyan stayed away due to illness, and Yuxiang grew so restless he fell ill himself. Yuxiang is utterly smitten—yet from whence did this root of passion grow? Qinyan is stranger still; in a boundless sea of faces, a single glance was all it took for him to cling so fiercely. If they had conversed daily and understood each other's temperaments, it might make sense. Yet they met by mere chance, like dew on the grass, and now cling to one another like a shadow to a body. Could this truly be what the Buddhists call karma?"`,`Zhongqing replied, "Their passion is among the most upright and pure; there is indeed a root, and I shall explain it to you. Ironically, this most righteous seed of love was planted by a rather unworthy man. I once asked Yuxiang how he became so captivated. Before Qinyan had even arrived in the capital, Wei Pincai shared a boat with him and spent his days and nights endlessly praising Qinyan's grace. Yuxiang listened until a deep longing took root in his heart—that was the beginning. Later, when he witnessed Qinyan's art firsthand, seeing that his beauty and skill soared far above the crowd, and learning of his proud, aloof temper, his affection blossomed into deep respect, and that respect, in turn, deepened into profound love. Had their paths never crossed again, it would have merely scattered like colorful clouds in the wind. Yet Heaven sent Duxiang to play the matchmaker. The false tested the true, casting light into hidden corners, until Yuxiang's entire heart was imprinted upon Qinyan's breast. Had the false Qinyan succeeded in deceiving him that day, Qinyan's feelings would surely have cooled. Consider this: to read a person's face and station from a single meeting, to say, 'I love you for your beauty, but also for your rank; beauty without such rank is ordinary.' Most people might scarcely feel the weight of those words, but Qinyan's proud soul fears any subtle slip into mockery. Yet, in the blink of an eye, Young Master Mei saw through him completely, like a flash of lightning illuminating his very core. People often assume that love is purely for one's physical beauty, but what heart truly loves a face alone? Though left unspoken, the truth hides within. Even if there were no such underlying depths, when one loves someone, they would never prefer rejection over reciprocation. Thus, Yuxiang's fierce anger only deepened Qinyan's gratitude, just as Qinyan's tears deepened Yuxiang's love. Though they had met but once, their hearts aligned as seamlessly as breath. The roots and branches of their bond have spread everywhere—how can you say none exists? Baozhu claims that Qinyan had taken note of Yuxiang even before the gathering at Yiyuan, wandering in a dreamlike daze ever since catching a glimpse of him on the sixth day of the New Year. That part remains a mystery to me, and perhaps karma truly does hold sway over it all." Wang Xun marveled, "You preach with the clarity of the Surangama Sutra, stripping away every veil. Their love stands deepest and straightest, elevating it far beyond common folk. How, then, does it compare to the bond between Xiangfan and Meixiang?"`,`Zhongqing laughed, "That is entirely another species of love. As I see it, Xiangfan's devotion to Meixiang was born initially of physical desire. He squandered oceans of silver, and upon gazing at such transcendent loveliness, he endured insult without rage and mockery without shame. He was like a mortal who had caught a glimpse of heaven, desperate to gaze upon it every waking hour. When Meixiang finally took pity on his hidden suffering and recognized his tireless patience—yielding but a fraction of warmth to acknowledge him—Xiangfan revealed his elegant speech and gentle demeanor. He was humble without lewd intent, possessing a pure sincerity that eventually softened her heart. Once she was moved, they clung to each other like lacquer, their bond growing firmer with each passing day. In the beginning, Xiangfan scarcely considered Meixiang's station; he saw only her flower-like face and tender disposition. Yet, upon finding her stern, unapproachable, and ultimately capable of aiding and advising him, his love transformed into deep respect, and that respect deepened his love. It was like a friend's earnest warning or a stone from another mountain used for polishing jade—his wrongs were righted without the need for preaching. Compared to Yuxiang, they are like Sima Xiangru and Yang Xiong: sharing the same craft, yet playing an entirely different tune. Only those who love beauty without succumbing to debauchery can hold true passion; feelings born of sheer lust do not stem from one's true nature. Take Pan the Third, for instance—he covets Meixiang, but would you call that 'passion'?" Wang Xun countered, "It is still a kind of feeling. If he felt nothing at all, he wouldn't spare her a single thought." Zhongqing laughed dismissively, "If Pan possessed true feeling, he would never lust after Meixiang so basely. His lust is the very proof of his heartlessness." Wang Xun chuckled, "That logic feels a bit forced. Passion can be both upright and crooked. Pan's is a crooked lust, unlike Xiangfan's, but to say he felt absolutely nothing—why then would he persist in pestering her five times over?" Zhongqing smiled, "The answer is simple. If Pan truly loved Meixiang, he wouldn't insist on making her his 'adopted son.' Being casual friends without any lewd thoughts would suffice. Had Meixiang accepted his silver without yielding any physical joy, I fear Pan would have hated her to the very marrow of his bones. Who heaps insult upon the one they supposedly love? If someone slights me when I have done them no offense, yet I can still forgive them—that is love. When feeling is reduced to crawling inside a miser's chest, Heaven's gate of passion will have to be torn down and rebuilt by a new generation of immortals." Wang Xun clapped his hands in delight. "You part the veils of mystery! I see it clearly now. You are truly a transcendent master of passion." He then asked, "And what of Duxiang's passion?" Zhongqing replied, "Duxiang is a grand spendthrift, yet he carries himself with effortless ease and vast tolerance. His love is wide but never promiscuous, his spirit strong but never arrogant. For him, the boundary between self and other dissolves entirely; he is the very host of passion." He continued, "Xiao Cixian is like a wild crane or an idle cloud, retaining the habits of a wandering scholar. Yet in moments of stillness, none can match him; one could converse with him all day without weariness—his passion reveals itself in that quiet endurance. Nanxiang is proud of his talent and can be fierce, but where his feelings run earnest, he shows little cruelty, moving as freely as cloud and water. His wit is sharp, yet toward those he favors, he never utters a cruel syllable; his is a devoted passion. Qianzhou resembles you—possessing a mild, thick kindness, preferring to let others wrong him rather than wronging them. As for me, I forget no feeling, yet I spend it rarely. It is easy to begin and hard to end; if I were to throw it about without closure, I would surely drown. It is better to lend my passion out and watch others. Where their feelings crack or block, I lend a little of mine to complete theirs, and in doing so, complete my own. Passion is the world's common treasure; it need not be hoarded for oneself." Wang Xun nodded thoughtfully, "That is a vast philosophy, turning all under heaven into one unified feeling and assigning each its proper place. Even sacrificing one's life for duty springs from this same source. At a rough glance, Zhu Guojie lent his passion in this way. You, my friend, are a knight of passion." Zhongqing waved off the praise, "You flatter me undeservedly; natures simply differ. At Duxiang's, I hear much of Young Master Hua. Though we have not met, his generous feeling matches Duxiang's source, though it flows from a different stream. Duxiang honors him greatly. As for Wei Pincai, Feng Zipei, and Pan the Third, they are mere worms devouring the grain of passion. To be near them rots the body, and to follow them rots the heart—they were born to plague decent folk. Then there is Xi Shiyi, who ruins actors by trapping them in a wooden barrel. Many have been poisoned by him. Qiguan was caught in it, but broke the barrel through sheer strength, tearing his hands raw; they are still healing. Xi is a true bandit of passion. And your bookworm brother-in-law, along with his counterpart, haven't the faintest idea what feeling is. Their joy and grief are entirely alien to mankind." Wang Xun laughed, "Those fools—every vulgar joke belongs to them. Sun Laoda recently gained another brother-in-law, much like Pincai but even duller. He is bound to make a scene."`,`Zhongqing said, "That worm-eaten 'Thousand-Character Classic' needs to be force-fed a bowl of ink to stop his loose talk." Wang Xun asked what remedy Li Yuanmao should be prescribed. Zhongqing laughed, "Yuanmao is so profoundly dull that all seven of his orifices are blocked tight. He needs a heavy dose of rhubarb and mirabilite to purge the muck from his system." Wang Xun then asked about Zhongyu. Zhongqing replied, "He hovers somewhere between good and ill. He still recognizes decent folk, so his conscience isn't entirely dead." As they bantered, Li Yulin arrived with Guibao; after exchanging greetings, they took their seats. Zhongqing asked, "We haven't seen you for two days. Have you been busy at home?" Wang Xun added, "I go out often, yet I never seem to run into you." Guibao explained, "We've been rehearsing new plays over at Yiyuan." When Zhongqing asked which ones, Yulin replied, "The sixth of the sixth month is the Lotus Birthday, and Young Master Hua plans to tour the garden. Since Duxiang knows he loves the opera, he consulted Jingyi. Jingyi said that Young Master Hua has already heard all the ordinary plays, and no troupe could possibly surpass his own Eight Ling company. Therefore, it would be better to pick the brightest stars from several different companies, assemble them into one grand cast, and compose entirely fresh pieces. It will be unlike anything outside ears have ever heard—altogether new." Zhongqing nodded approvingly, "That sounds wonderful. But what plays have they chosen?" Yulin said, "Once, the scholar Mao Shengshan listed several titles in his commentary on 'Story of the Pipa'—a collection he called 'Stone to Mend Heaven'—which had never been set to music."`,`Zhongqing laughed heartily. "What a vile mouth! Mao was merely teasing his readers. He only listed titles in his commentary on the Lute, such as 'Li Ling Returns to Han,' 'Prince Dan Destroys Qin,' 'Zhuge Yan Nian,' and 'The Bright Consort Returns to Han'—eight or nine in total." Yulin continued, "Jingyi has added four more: 'Green Pearl Throws Herself from the Golden Valley Garden,' 'Consort Yang Follows the Carriage at Mawei Post,' 'Li the Banished Immortal Receives the Edict at Yelang,' and 'Du the Remonstrator Honored in the Golden Hall.' These require huge casts, with nearly everyone on stage." Zhongqing nodded, "Those four are magnificent; they give vent to the sorrows of lovers and scholars across the world. The silken noose at Mawei breaks every heart. As for An Lushan's rebellion, the fault lay squarely with Yang Guozhong—what crime did the Lady commit? Popular tales love to slander the harem. The scandalous bath scene appears in neither the Old nor the New Book of Tang; it stems entirely from Yuan Zhen's cheap line about washing the child in the golden-rooster tent, which posterity mistakenly took as historical proof. It is hateful. The traitorous minister was executed, and the army was pacified—that was reasonable enough. But Chen Yuanli lacked both a loyal heart and proper troop discipline, and should have been cast out. If we were to reverse these historical wrongs, so many more would remain." Wang Xun asked how many actors were rehearsing. Guibao replied, "Ten female roles, alongside twenty-some sheng, jing, lao, and chou actors drawn from five or six different companies." Zhongqing listed the ten female names aloud, to which Wang Xun remarked, "That makes a Ten-Beauties company." Guibao added, "The guest list isn't entirely settled yet, but you two are certainly included. I heard Duxiang wrote to Baoding to invite Qianzhou back, likely just for this occasion." Wang Xun noted, "It's drawing close. Today is already the twenty-sixth. Are ten days enough to prepare all these new plays?" Yulin laughed, "You've forgotten the intercalary fifth month! We still have over a month remaining." Wang Xun chuckled at his own absentmindedness, "I've been so secluded lately, I've completely lost track of the days." Zhongqing then asked if Qinyan was still ill. Yulin replied, "After spending that day on the canal with Yuxiang, he mended almost immediately." Wang Xun smiled, "Joy truly lifts the spirit." Zhongqing then inquired if Qiguan's hands had healed. At this, Yulin lowered his eyes, his brows knit together, a dark shadow of anger crossing his face. When they pressed him for the reason, Guibao explained that Zhongqing's mention of Qiguan had touched a raw nerve. He recounted the horrific trick Xi Shiyi had played with the wooden barrel at the workshop. Zhongqing and Wang Xun cursed aloud. "That such vile people exist—how do their victims manage to live?" Guibao sighed, "He is currently hiding out in Tianjin, but he may yet return." Zhongqing asked about Xi's background, and Guibao traced it back: a merchant grandfather, a father who was now a river intendant, and twelve brothers scattered across trade and office. Xi had nearly bought a circuit intendant's seat, but when his funds fell short, he purchased a sub-prefect title instead. Wang Xun recalled the old men calling him 'Story of the Pipa,' 'Xi Old Earth' because of the massive profits he made peddling opium. As Yulin and Guibao rose to leave, Wang Xun pressed them to stay for supper, sending word to the kitchen.`,`Presently, the sun slanted westward. Zhongqing ordered the curtains to be rolled up and had the table moved out to the gallery, setting out four seats. Wang Xun said apologetically, "It will just be a plain meal with no extra dishes; I certainly cannot match Duxiang's extravagance." Guibao waved it off, "You say that, but I have lost count of the plain feasts I've enjoyed here. The Dongcheng Kitchen couldn't possibly equal this. Duxiang spends far too much—who can even eat such massive spreads anyway?" They took their seats. The kitchen, hearing that actors were present, thoughtfully added eight plates and four special dishes. Yellow wine and light snacks were brought out first. Yulin and Guibao offered toasts, and the conversation flowed easily as they savored the mellow wine. Zhongqing and Wang Xun asked for more news, but noticing that Yulin was abstaining, they asked why. Yulin explained that his throat was hoarse from the summer heat and he dared not drink. Zhongqing ordered fresh fruit to be brought out, remarking that drinking without playing games could hardly be delightful, though few people knew how to limit them properly. Guibao proposed a round of "Affixing the Kingfisher Command," to which Wang Xun eagerly agreed. Dice were brought to the table, and as the rounds passed, many cups were emptied in good cheer.`,`Earlier in the day, Wang Xun had listened intently to Zhongqing judge the depths of each man's passions, which naturally led his thoughts to the actors of the Catalogue of Flowers and their own emotional temperaments. He asked Yulin and Guibao, "Of the ten of you rehearsing at Yiyuan, do you know everyone's true temperament? Whose company is the easiest to keep?" Guibao replied, "There are ten entirely different sorts. Yuyong is by far the coldest, but the rest are mild enough." Yulin elaborated, "For refined taste and a keen, penetrating mind—someone who doesn't chase power and possesses a proud, independent spirit—that is Yaoqing. Spotless and bright, harboring a fiercely rivalrous talent, that is Meixiang. For gentle grace, carefully measured speech, harmonious yet firm, and sweetly poised—that would be Xiangwan. Frank talk, a lofty manner, welcoming to both high and low with a free spirit, is Jingfang. Quiet ease and perfectly apt words belong to Shouxiang.`,`"Quick of mind and nimble of speech, with an elegant bearing—that is Xiao Mei. Highly versatile and nursing a romantic self-regard—" Yulin nodded toward Guibao, "that is him. A secret heart that few can ever truly grasp, steadfast until the seas run dry—that is Yuyong. Pure fidelity, entirely heedless of personal gain—that is Yuyan. As for me, I have no merit whatsoever; I am dull, and people despise me. That is who I am." Guibao interrupted, "You sell yourself far too short! Here is my verdict on you: fragrant restraint and pleasant grace, neither overly proud nor lowly base, harboring neither intense love nor bitter hatred—that is Peixian." Zhongqing and Wang Xun applauded, "Sharp indeed! Ruixiang's insight grows by the day." Yulin modestly demurred. Wang Xun then asked for their judgment on Rongguan, Er Xi, Yumei, Chunlin, and Fenglin—the outsiders. Guibao laughed, "They belong to an entirely different sect! We are the disciples of the Yuqing gate, crusading for the Zhou; they are of the Tongtian sect, aiding the tyrants—they are like the tortoise-spirit and Shen Gongbao. They possess uncanny tricks and are highly dangerous. When fate forces us to sit together, we tread very softly and dare not jest. Peixian's scandal—was it not Rongguan who exposed it? Qiguan's agony—did Er Xi not engineer it? Honestly, I fear for Du Yuyong. He offends absolutely everyone, and his foes are multiplying. His memory is terribly weak; after only a few days, he forgets faces completely, and everyone despises his cold pride. He is even cool toward Duxiang. The only one he calls his life's soulmate is Young Master Mei; he was actually sick with longing for him! Who exactly is this Master Mei?" Zhongqing clarified, "You met him—Mei's son, styled Yuxiang." Guibao recalled, "Ah, yes! His beauty certainly pairs well with Yuyong, but does Yuyong's temper truly suit him?" Yulin added, "That day when Yuyong was absent, no wonder Yuxiang drooped so noticeably. He was completely silent, sipped very little, eventually lay down, and though he promised to visit Yaoqing's house, he never came." Wang Xun noted, "The other day, he was boating on the canal with Sulan and Qinyan." Guibao clicked his tongue knowingly, "No wonder Yuyong returned fully healed." They laughed and chatted well past the second watch. Finally, the actors took their leave and rode their carriages home, while Zhongqing and Wang Xun talked a while longer before retiring for the night. As for the upcoming feast at Yiyuan, the new plays, and how Young Master Hua will respond—you must hear the next installment to find out.`]},y=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations24:n},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),o={25:["In the previous chapter, Yulin and Guibao at Wang Xun's place were speaking of the new plays being rehearsed at Yiyuan in preparation for Young Master Hua's garden visit. Time flew swiftly; in the blink of an eye a month passed, and Liu Wenze had returned. Of this circle of distinguished scholars, the one Young Master Hua had long been acquainted with was Liu Wenze alone; the rest he had mostly not yet met.","On the sixth day, taking advantage of the early morning cool, he came to Yiyuan at the chen hour. He arrived in a carriage with two horses, his attire fresh and splendid; as he entered the garden gate, a servant went ahead to announce him. Wenze meanwhile admired the garden's scenery as he walked slowly. Though many people had visited Yiyuan, those who could remember their way around were surprisingly few.","The garden ran some three or four li around its circuit. The small hills within were piled from Lake Tai rock; the long ridge of great hills sat on packed-earth footings crowned with towering peaks, dressed in flowers and trees and framed by pavilions — indistinguishable from living mountains. Inside the grottoes water was secretly pumped with chain-pumps and sprayed upward to form cascading waterfalls.","The pool belt linked to an outer river, winding and circling, broad or narrow as terrain allowed. Within the grounds stood also archery ranges, ball courts, fishing lodges, rice paddies, wineshops, tea arbors — all run by gardeners, uniformly neat and clean — to occupy those with duties in the garden and keep them from slipping outside to neglect work: such was Duxiang's arrangement. Formal courtyards and halls numbered twenty-four in all — linked yet broken apart, never repetitive — if one toured conscientiously, a whole day might cover only three or four spots; eight or nine days were needed to finish the whole.",`Even the garden's lord might not recall every turn at once. At the center rose a great hall named Han Wan Lou — "embracing the myriad things and resolving them into light" — the garden's chief building, windows on every face, spirit vast and open. Outside stretched a stone terrace with rails on three sides and seven tiers of steps at center. Before it ran a belt of parasol trees screening like a folding screen; beyond rose tier on tier of towers and galleries. Along the eastern willows lay the sheet of water connecting to the Chanting-Autumn Waterside Pavilion — lotus now in countless bloom, white and red, emerald canopies and feathered parasols — a slight breeze and fragrance filled every courtyard.`,`At that moment Ziyun welcomed Wenze in, and they seated themselves below Han Wan Lou. Ziyun first inquired about the situation in Baoding; Wenze recounted his news, then asked Ziyun: "Apart from Young Master Hua, what other notable guests are expected today?" Ziyun said: "A few elderly gentlemen in their gauze caps would have nothing to say to Young Master Hua — they care only for rank and stipend. Among those we regularly associate with, some know Hua and some do not. If there were even one stiff moralist at the table, the whole company would be ill at ease, so I invited only our usual circle. Apart from Gao Zhuoran, who has not yet returned, there were Shi, Yan, Tian, Wang, and Mei — enough for three tables. Who would have expected that yesterday they all declined at once? By sheer coincidence, not a single one could come." Wenze asked why. Ziyun said: "Yuxiang's old illness has flared up again. Shi Zhujun drank himself sick yesterday — vomiting blood, unable even to leave his room. Xiangfan says he has never met Young Master Hua and refuses to come. Yong'an's wife is about to give birth any day now, so he will not leave her side for a single step. Jiantan, seeing that no one else was coming, also sent his regrets. Yesterday I had no choice but to invite Zhang Zhongyu, who does happen to be acquainted with Young Master Hua. Besides him there is only Jingyi — five of us in all, which leaves just two tables. The others have never met Young Master Hua and have no idea what sort of lordly, extravagant manner he cuts, which is why they would not come. You have seen him often enough yourself — in truth there is nothing extraordinary about him; he merely carries himself with a naturally lofty bearing, and his retinue is resplendent, that is all."`,`Wenze then asked where Cixian was. Ziyun said: "Jingyi is directing today's new plays in person. There are still a number of passages where the staging is not quite right, and the musical modes remain somewhat discordant in places, so he is correcting each one himself. He will be along shortly." While they were speaking, Zhang Zhongyu arrived. Ziyun went out to welcome him in, and Wenze rose to greet him. Looking at Zhongyu's attire, Wenze noticed it was different from the usual: formerly Zhongyu had held the humble rank of a vice ninth-grade officer, yet today his cap and robes were suddenly those of the sixth rank — the same as Wenze's own — so he must have purchased a promotion. Wenze asked him: "Congratulations! Congratulations! When did you buy your advancement? You did not even send me word — are you afraid I shall want a share of the celebration wine?" Zhongyu laughed: "Well, you hid yourself far away, for fear I would come asking to borrow money! This rank of mine is nothing to boast of — indeed it is shameful — yet I would tell people all the same. It is merely a few taels of silver spent so as to look a touch more presentable, and spare myself being mocked as a 'kowtow beetle.'" Ziyun, it turned out, had known all along and had even advanced him a thousand taels the other day. He said to Zhongyu: "That was quick work — all done and settled. You said you were buying the rank of sub-prefect." Zhongyu said: "It was only thanks to you, Second Master — otherwise it would never have come together. I originally wanted to purchase a sub-prefect's commission, but aside from you, Second Master, I could not scrape together even two poles' worth. And Wenze happened to be away in Baoding — otherwise I would certainly have wrung five hundred taels out of him. As it stands, this regular guard commander's post cost me close to four thousand taels all told, and even then it was only possible because Pan Laosan of the Qisheng Silver Exchange advanced five hundred taels on my behalf."`,'Wenze said to Ziyun: "Zhang the Second is truly a capable fellow — sharp and shrewd in every way. Once he takes up his post, he will undoubtedly prove a most efficient official, and fame and fortune are sure to follow." Ziyun laughed: "Fame and fortune he will certainly reap in abundance; his superiors will certainly be delighted — only the common folk may suffer somewhat." Wenze burst into hearty laughter. Zhongyu too laughed and said: "You have guessed exactly right. If I were to claim I would never take a single coin, I myself would be too ashamed to utter such a self-deceiving lie. Besides, this office of mine was purchased with capital — I am not like you fortunate gentlemen, who step straight from the study into officialdom. In the future, I shall simply distinguish between money that may properly be taken and money that may not — that is all." This set the whole company laughing. At that moment Cixian emerged from behind the screen, and everyone greeted him; the celebrated dan actors all followed him out and were introduced as well. They all sat chatting for a while longer when a household servant came forward and announced: "Young Master Hua is nearly at the gate." Ziyun ordered sedan chairs prepared at once and stationed at the garden gate, then asked Cixian to keep Wenze and the others company while he himself hastily straightened his cap and robes and went out from Han Wan Lou to meet the guest.',"After a pause footsteps rustled everywhere — one figure in sixth-rank robes rounded the artificial hill; next four retainers in caps and feathers bore a sedan-chair — Young Master Hua inside in lustrous silk and stitched gold — some twenty followers trailing — Ziyun led the party forward to greet him.",'It emerged that Lord Hua and Duke Xu had been deepest friends twenty years and served two campaigns together — friendship moss-thick as hill herbs, oath-bound as metal and stone. Xu Ziyun and Young Master Hua had sworn brotherhood and revived the tie between houses. Ziyun was five years elder; Young Master honored him as elder brother — courteous and yielding. At once Ziyun yielded Young Master Hua the seat of honor while servants poured tea. Young Master Hua said: "Days ago I meant to pay respects — imperial escort duties stacked up and heat warned against disturbing you. Today cooler — we may talk half the day happily — pity one city wall divides us — we cannot carry lamps all night — hard to satisfy the heart." Ziyun said: "Repeatedly you honor us — glory reaches grove and spring. I idle alone, lazy as habit — long wished to invite you yet feared official duty left no leisure — and the wall between blunts cheer. Tonight I hoped to detain you till dawn — will you lodge one night in the humble hall?" Young Master Hua smiled: "Your famous garden fills my dreams — I long for a full roam. Later I shall trouble your studio — not one night — I want ten days like Lord Pingyuan before this petty temper clears — today I must return — tomorrow perhaps an imperial commission waits — vulgar dust blocks refined pleasure — summer swells and the bright moon is not yet round — wait till mid-autumn for our all-night revel?" Ziyun laughed: "Sound — moonless nights dull drinking — no need for mid-autumn — seventh month will do — shall we fix late seventh month?" Young Master Hua agreed: "Cooler autumn lifts the heart — pity my grounds are cramped — less than half yours — built by no master hand — little hill-and-stream surprise — summer stays sultry — only autumn and winter suit short rests."','"If elder brother does not disdain plain fare, I shall send a carriage of welcome." Ziyun said: "Excellent — whenever I cannot leave the city I shall trouble you. Your Western Garden is superbly laid — could it join the Eastern Garden it would be finer." Young Master Hua said: "So many main halls stand between — cannot link — and the east quarter lodges many guests — mixed folk — nowhere to ornament." While they spoke drums and pipes sounded behind; Ziyun urged Young Master Hua inward; past the vestibule they reached Chengyin Hall steps — three men inside advanced to the gallery to greet him — all old friends — Young Master Hua exchanged bows with each.',`He bowed to Cixian: "Master Jingyi took trouble to arrange these plays — how shall we watchers repay? Today we offer a few extra cups." Cixian laughed loudly: "Rustic tunes may wound refined ears — if you nod once I shall gladly drain a full cup for everyone." All agreed it was good — wine was ready — servants bore jars forward — Ziyun poured and set seats — Young Master Hua held chief east seat with Zhongyu attending; Wenze chief west with Cixian attending; Ziyun played host beside Young Master Hua's table.`,`Young Master Hua said: "There are no further guests — just the five of us. Why not merge into a single table? Seated far apart, conversation is difficult; and once the opera begins, we shall not hear each other at all." Wenze said: "In that case, let us combine into one table." Ziyun said: "Very well, though it will be rather crowded — let us exchange for a round table instead. I only fear it may seem insufficiently formal." Young Master Hua said: "Pray do not stand on ceremony! Between brothers, I can hardly be counted a guest. If Second Brother is so rigid with etiquette, I shall never dare impose upon you again." Ziyun answered promptly, and the servants placed a round table in the center, rearranged the cups and dishes upon it, and cleared away the two side tables. On the stage the drums and gongs had already struck up. From the greenroom, ten exquisite blossoms came gliding out in a graceful procession — lotus steps barely stirring, fragrant breezes already wafting — each holding an ivory tablet. They approached the banquet, knelt together, and rose again. First Baozhu, Huifang, and Sulan stepped forward, bowed to Young Master Hua, and presented their ivory tablets. Young Master Hua knew that this troupe of young dan actors were all Ziyun's prized favorites, and that Yuan Baozhu was especially cherished and lived in the garden every day. Putting on his most worldly manner, he took Baozhu by the hand and asked: "How many days have you all been rehearsing this play?" Baozhu replied: "Over a month now, but we rehearse separately — each person has only three to five scenes." Young Master Hua then casually selected one piece for each of them. The remaining seven all came forward to request selections as well. But before choosing plays for them, Young Master Hua first studied the dan actors carefully one by one — he did not recognize them, and so inquired after each one's name and courtesy name. Among the seven, he singled out Qinyan for particular admiration and asked Ziyun: "This one seems to be new." Ziyun smiled and asked: "How can you tell?" Young Master Hua said: "I notice his bearing seems not quite seasoned, and yet he is beautiful without and wise within — there is a transcendent quality about him that sets him apart from the dust of the world." He then selected a piece for Qinyan and chose plays for all the rest as well. The program was passed to Wenze; Wenze, Zhongyu, and Cixian conferred together and added several more selections. The performance opened with the auspicious prologue, after which the "Conferring of Blessings" figure appeared, presenting the inscription "Generations Blessed by Imperial Grace." Lin Shanzhi then stepped forward, took out a reward packet, and tossed it toward the stage. Wenze and the others likewise presented their rewards.`,`The opening plays were "Li Ling Returns to Han" and "The Bright Consort Enters the Pass." Next came "The Envoy Receives the Edict" — principal male role — imperial wine and golden flowers bestowed, farewell money along the route — bearing proud and free, tones bold and grave — conjuring Li Bai's wandering soul entire. When singing ceased a figure emerged — guests fancied Li Bai himself had stepped down.`,"The second bill was 'Consort Yang Enters Shu.' First came the execution of Yang Guozhong; then Chen Yuanli admonished the emperor on the mutual obligations of ruler and minister, and the six armies roared their approval. The Bright Emperor journeyed to Mount Emei and ascended the tower with the consort. He played the jade flute himself while the consort sang the 'Clear Peace' verses; the palace lady Hong Tao performed the 'Whirling Wind' dance, and the court musician Li Guinian played the eight-tone instruments. From amid the ethereal clouds on high, colored luan-birds and vermilion phoenixes descended. There appeared eight female immortals — Dong Shuangcheng, Duan Anxiang, Xu Feiqiong, Wu Cailuan, Fan Chengjun, Huo Xiaoyu, Shi Gongzi, and Ruan Linghua — in rosy-mist robes and cloud-green gowns, garments of gold thread and sheer silk, riding upon the wind. Amid them swirled innumerable colored clouds, in which golden boys and jade maidens were arrayed, singing and dancing. Mount Emei was built upon a scaffold frame, and the eight female immortals stood together at its summit, while below them clouds spiraled and celestial flowers blazed in splendor. Incense of hundred-blend and ambergris was burning, its fragrant smoke coiling and curling, the warmth of human presence mingling with the mist, until the entire stage seemed transported into the midst of brilliant clouds. Young Master Hua applauded without cease, and the whole company joined in with enthusiastic acclaim, drinking freely round after round. Looking again at the stage, all ten performers were displayed — truly a vision spanning heaven and earth, a realm of color and a city of fragrance. This one possessed a beauty to topple kingdoms; that one wore hair like wind-swept clouds. This one coiled a serpentine chignon; that one bore the fresh rouge of the 'falling-from-horseback' style. This one pressed her heart and mimicked the frown of Xi Shi's neighbor; that one's loveliness would make a king forget his meal. This one wore the crescent-moon hairpin of the hundred jewels, lovely as Chanjuan; that one sported kingfisher-feather ornaments and jade pendants fit for a celestial maiden. The beauty contest of Yan Shifan's famed courtesans could not have been more splendid; the screen of concubines belonging to Yang Guozhong might not have been so uniformly lovely. Young Master Hua was so entranced that his brows danced and his face glowed; heroic spirits surging within him, he called for a larger cup and first toasted Cixian. Cixian, his own spirits soaring like rosy clouds, was in the highest of moods and promptly drained three great goblets in succession. Young Master Hua matched him with three cups of his own. He then ordered a servant to carry wine to the stage, commanding Baozhu, Sulan, Qinyan, and Huifang each to drink three cups, and sent four dishes of fruit from the banquet table as a gift; the four dan actors bowed their thanks from afar. He then urged everyone at the table to drink three more great cups apiece.",`Those two performances had taken a great deal of time. Ziyun, seeing that Young Master Hua's spirits were running exceedingly high, ordered the plays stopped and summoned the ten immortal maidens forward, still in full stage makeup, to toast him — each one presenting a great cup. Young Master Hua refused not a single one, smiling as he accepted and drank them all. He then demanded that the others follow suit, but everyone's capacity fell far short of his, so they had no choice but to switch to smaller cups, and each still drank ten in all. Young Master Hua then had the whole troupe of dan actors called before him and studied them once more, then said to Ziyun: "Last year I entrusted Zhang the Second to select eight performers and form a troupe; looking at them now, they cannot compare with yours by a wide margin. In future I shall have to buy new beauties and build another golden house for them. Only I fear that natural talent is limited, and Elder Brother Duxiang has already claimed all the romantic fragrant fortune — what remains is merely leftover powder and scattered rouge. I would not dare parade inferior treasures before the Golden Valley of your house." Ziyun laughed: "You are far too modest! Your own estate is a heaven embroidered with brocade, lined with city-toppling beauties. I merely borrow another man's wine cup to pour out my own frustrations. And mine is a patchwork of single fox-pelts stitched together — how can it compare with your household, where the rouged beauties come from your own ranks and the golden hairpins are hidden within your own walls? I fear that a foot of plain silk can hardly rival the seven-shuttle brocade." Young Master Hua said: "I would not dare! I would not dare! Elder Brother is being far too humble — a penalty toast is in order!" And he at once toasted Ziyun with a cup. Then Young Master Hua called for Shanzhi and ordered the Ba Ling troupe to come up. This Ba Ling troupe was one he brought along whenever he attended a banquet, complete with their own costumes and stage properties. Shanzhi led them up, and they kowtowed to Ziyun. Ziyun hastily ordered a page to help them to their feet, praising them repeatedly as "Excellent!"; the others echoed their agreement. Young Master Hua said: "Beyond the immortal maidens, I have my own motley crew. If you do not disdain their humble appearance, let them sing one piece as well, just for a laugh — what do you say?" Everyone said: "Splendid! If that can be arranged, it will truly be pearls strung alongside jade." Having received their cue, the Ba Ling troupe went into the greenroom, costumed themselves, and performed a piece called 'Immortals' Grand Assembly.' They too were resplendent in bearing and charming in manner, and the company applauded endlessly. Ziyun whispered a few words to his attendants; shortly afterward, two men came forward bearing two trays, which they set before the banquet table. Upon them were fifty-tael silver ingots — four to each tray, eight in all. A Xu household servant addressed Shanzhi: "One portion is from the three guests, and one is from our master." The Ba Ling troupe kowtowed their thanks from the stage. Young Master Hua also rose and expressed his gratitude, saying: "Such wretched little performers — do they even deserve a reward? You have spent too generously." Ziyun said repeatedly: "I am ashamed — it is nothing." Everyone urged Young Master Hua to be reseated. Young Master Hua glanced at Shanzhi and murmured a quiet word; Shanzhi immediately went out. After about the time it takes to drink a cup of tea, he returned bearing in both hands a vermilion lacquer tray covered with a cloth of red satin pressed with gold. He lifted the cloth and presented the tray before Young Master Hua.`,"Everyone stared — a tray blazing with gold ingots — lucky shapes, ruyi shapes, plum shapes, water-chestnut tips — each above one tael, perhaps a hundred pieces — portioned among the ten performers. After Shanzhi handed them out the ten kowtowed thanks; Ziyun hastened thanks as well.",`By now the clock showed nearly the end of the wei hour; the banquet was cleared. Young Master Hua rose and said: "I came to stroll the garden — today again there was no time — yet the lotus must be seen." Ziyun had the feast carried to the Chanting-Autumn Waterside Pavilion, ordered lotus-gathering boats prepared, and told the ten performers to dress as maidens gathering lotus and paddle into the pool; guests were shown to the pavilion. Young Master Hua and company stepped inside — lotus stretched on every side, red perfume thick, emerald leaves layered — a glorious realm of color and scent. They sat again to wine. Four or five little boats slipped to the pool's heart; aboard sat the celebrated dans, costumed in long skirts and short sleeves, lotus faces complementing peach cheeks, threading through the blossoms — each lovely profile half-lost among the flowers, blurring reality and illusion. Along the shore lay a craft with brocade sail and silk hawser; within, an ensemble struck up the full wind-and-percussion "ten-fold." The gatherers sang the "Lotus-Picking Song" song — voices tender and winding — heard like a clear midnight song, seen like the Goddess of the Xiang River at play — as if Zhang Lihua had dressed as an immortal and Zhu Gui'er played Chang'e. All grew dizzily happy, nearly rolling from their seats in drunken delight. Only Young Master Hua's high spirits mounted: he said to Ziyun: "Earlier plays never finished — that one alone took half the day. Why not sing 'Golden Thread' again and dance 'Nishang' — let each show a glimpse of his art — worthy of elder brother's choosing colors and tones — let the troupe give their utmost and not waste the stage — what say you?"`,`Ziyun laughed: "Exactly my wish." He had the whole troupe summoned up. Hearing the order, they plied orchid oars toward the pavilion, landed, and drew up in goose formation beyond the railing. Young Master Hua called four men in by name: Huifang, Qinyan, Baozhu, Sulan. To them he said: "In 'Immortals of Mount Emei' just now the whole cast stood forth but not every gesture appeared. Each of you name a piece you do best." The four thought and each named one. Ziyun said: "Those are not yet your acme — only Meixiang and Xiangwan's 'Exclusive Possession' and Yaoqing and Yuyi's 'Waking from a Dream' and 'Pursuing the Dream' are peerless — no one else performs them — pity they overlap." Wenze said: "Why not pair the two sides and sing together — each perfecting his part — pearls strung on one string?" Cixian and Zhongyu both said: "Excellent — though evenly matched there will still be shades to savor." Young Master Hua laughed: "Fresh — I have never seen two sing the same piece together — even 'Pursuing the Dream' may be doubled." Ziyun relayed orders to the troupe to wait in the wings and had tables shifted forward. Baozhu and Qinyan withdrew to dress. Soon rose grieving bamboo and piercing strings — bright as bells. Young Master Hua watched Qinyan emerge from the east — like the bright moon first rising on a gentle breeze — his steps like spring clouds drifting slowly toward the edge of the heaven of longing. Baozhu came from the west — like a shy blossom about to open, dawn dew still clinging — his steps like slender willow strands trailing into the soft red dust. When they sang, both voices turned and flowed, clear and round; amid green trees and kingfisher bamboo fledgling phoenix answered fledgling — each word honey-warm as jade, each tone shaking the soul and stirring the bowels. One cast languid autumn glances, his powdered neck bent low; the other drew brows faintly knit, like spring stars briefly meeting. The whole company sat entranced — hearts adrift, eyes drunk.`,'Ziyun only nodded and smiled. Young Master Hua smacked the table and cried: "Splendid! Splendid! Today I first believe peerless beauty exists under heaven — I deeply regret filling embroidered curtains with Momu and Wuyan look-alikes." He added loudly: "I blame Elder Brother Duxiang for hoarding goddesses — selfishly feasting his eyes — only after your fill do you invite guests to pick up what fragrance remains — today you drink ten huge cups before anything else."',`He ordered Lin Shanzhi to bring his own great jade wine-cup. Shanzhi saw the hour late and knew his master's temper — once roused he would go day or night — though he answered aloud he stood frozen, eyes on Ziyun. Ziyun took the hint; knowing his drinking could not match, he said: "My shallow capacity truly cannot stomach more — let my door cup stand for your great dipper." Young Master Hua still refused until Cixian, Wenze, and Zhongyu mediated: "Not only Duxiang — none of us could match those cups — fine him three small cups instead." Knowing Ziyun drank little, Young Master Hua yielded. They made Ziyun drain three cups in succession while he himself, needing no urging, emptied a dozen large cups and still shouted praise — forced Wenze through three more and Cixian and Zhongyu five or six. Suddenly he turned to Baozhu and Qinyan: "Sing on — when done, sing again." He studied them again minutely and told the company: "These two each have marvels — like five sparrows and six swallows, weight in balance — Lady Zhao and Consort Yang, lean and plump each right. Baozhu's tenderness stirs pity at sight; Qinyan's bones shine like jade — who could bear to send him away? Apart each is supreme — together both shine. Elder Brother Duxiang — today you have truly gladdened me." Ziyun saw Young Master Hua somewhat drunk and knew his habit — at height of mood nothing could stop him — yet could hardly check him — resigned himself to drinking till dawn.`,`Meanwhile on stage the two finished singers were not permitted to descend — they must sing again. Baozhu, seeing Young Master Hua's fervor, rejoiced inwardly; seeing him watch once and demand again, he poured still more grace into gesture — both to bind the master's eye and give Duxiang face. Qinyan inwardly resisted — Young Master Hua could not be offended — he swallowed shame — and resented sharing the stage only with Yuan Baozhu as rival, striving to outdo him in skill and feeling. He recalled Young Master's face bore something of Yuxiang — remembered that day performing "Waking from a Dream" when Yuxiang appeared — hearts had recognized each other across the hall — pity Yuxiang was not here today — else these two songs would not be wasted. "Let me treat Young Master Hua as Yuxiang on that balcony — replay that scene — perhaps spirit moves spirit and Yuxiang dreams me — even if he does not see me, it is as if I saw him." With that he poured himself into every glance — sang again with utter abandon.`,`All sat transported — only Lin Shanzhi, watching his master's infatuation, seethed inwardly yet dared not show it. The hour pressed toward you — linger longer and the city gates would shut — yet he could not urge aloud — slipped out and told retainers to hold the gate open, then returned and stood behind Young Master Hua, eyes pleading at Ziyun; everyone understood but none wished to prompt. When Young Master Hua stepped out briefly to relieve himself, Shanzhi bowed low to Ziyun and whispered: "Beg Second Master persuade our lord to drink less and return soon — the gates will close — palace duty waits — delay is no joke." Ziyun nodded: "Quite right — and it is time." Young Master Hua returned and saw Shanzhi whispering: "Still early?" Shanzhi said: "The watch reads you hour proper." Young Master Hua said: "This watch runs fast." Ziyun said: "Since brother is so merry — as I said this morning we should revel to third or fourth watch — stay tonight — do not re-enter the city — Qianzhou and Zhongyu live outside the walls — they fear no gate." Hearing both an invitation to lodge and the remark about outsiders needing no gate, Young Master Hua wavered — charmed by the pavilion yet uneasy about staying.`,'Twilight neared lamp-lighting — stay or go tore at him; his train stood in neat ranks below the steps — he half wished to leave; Baozhu and Qinyan were finishing their song — he said to Ziyun: "I had better return to the city." Shanzhi cut in: "Gates will shut — if you go, hurry." Young Master Hua had no choice — rose, dressed formal wear, thanked Ziyun and took leave of the company.',`By now Baozhu and Qinyan had washed off greasepaint and came to see guests off. Young Master Hua clasped Qinyan's hand: "Your singing tops the capital — rest a day then visit my mansion — I have much to ask in detail." From his waist he unclasped a Han jade paired-dragon pendant with purse and gave it to Qinyan — Qinyan bowed thanks. Young Master Hua took two steps, turned back to Baozhu: "You two are matched opponents — hard to declare a winner. You are Duxiang's darling — that is why you seldom come to my house." He asked Ziyun: "Second Brother — may I give him a gift?" Ziyun smiled: "As you will — why ask me?" Young Master Hua undid another jade plaque for Baozhu — Baozhu thanked him. All ten performers escorted them out; Young Master Hua reeled yet turned again and again toward Qinyan, Baozhu, Huifang, Sulan and the rest: "When idle, visit often." At Han Wan Lou he bowed once more to Ziyun and guests, boarded his sedan chair — Shanzhi and Eight Ling aside, sixteen personal attendants and five titled servants shouldered the poles and flew over hill and grove. The ten performers saw them to the garden gate and bowed farewell. Young Master Hua shifted to his green-canopied carriage and nodded repeated injunctions to the actors; followers mounted — the driver feared the gate and lashed once — the carriage flew; luckily Shanzhi had held the gate or they would have missed entry.`,"Young Master Hua's ride back to the city needs no further tale.",'Here the ten performers entered; Ziyun had them change into plain clothes, swapped for a larger round table, cleared scraps and ordered fresh dishes. Wenze said: "Today Xinbei had his soul stolen away — I never saw him cling so." Ziyun answered: "In his heart it still was not enough — I feared only he might fall ill from excess — luckily his nature is vigorous — drunk one night — sober next — no harm."',`Of today's ten acts the clearest triumph was Qinyan's — next Baozhu. Baozhu glowed inwardly; only Qinyan stayed cool as frost. Ziyun asked Qinyan: "Today you gained another true connoisseur — Young Master Hua seldom praises — sing for him again at his mansion and your name will ride highest."`,`Cixian observed, "I see Young Master Hua as a man who truly cherishes fragrance and pities jade — the rumors outside are simply not to be believed. Throughout this whole day he has been mild and warm, without a trace of a spoiled young master's arrogance. Yunong — you really shouldn't remain so unremittingly cold when dealing with people." Being spoken to like this by everyone, seemingly urging him to cozy up to Young Master Hua, Qinyan felt an angry resentment building up with nowhere to vent. He thought to himself: "It's one thing for the others to say this, but Duxiang of all people should know better. He knows full well of my affections for Yuxiang, yet he speaks these words today — how exactly does he view me?" The more he thought, the angrier he grew, until tears finally spilled down his cheeks. Zhongyu was already drunk, and seeing Qinyan in such a state, he let out a cold sneer and remarked: "This actor here is truly peculiar! Could it be that he's been praised so much it's ruined him? Others might exhaust their hearts and strength and still not manage to scrape up even a fraction of such praise." Qinyan was already harboring anger and lacked an outlet, so hearing this he couldn't hold back and retorted: "I never asked for anyone's praise, and I certainly don't know how to grovel. If he's so powerful and influential, that's his business. I'm not like those sycophants who fawn over people themselves and then demand others do the same — what's the point of all that?" Having said this, he unconsciously broke into sobs. Hearing this, Zhongyu felt both ashamed and furious, his face changing color. He wanted to erupt in earnest, but out of fear of Ziyun and the others, he swallowed it for the moment.`,`Ziyun knew Qinyan's blunt words had deeply offended Zhongyu, so he mediated: "Yuyong has simply drunk too much today — Yaoqing, take him over there to amuse yourselves until the wine wears off, and then come back." Baozhu immediately pulled Qinyan inside and gently chided him: "You spoke far too bluntly — that Second Master Zhang is not someone who takes things lightly, you must be careful." Qinyan was still sobbing softly. Baozhu brought out the gift Young Master Hua had bestowed to compare, noting it was indeed a bit smaller. Meanwhile, Wenze, having arrived extremely early and sat for the entire day drinking past his limit, also wanted to return and rest. The ten performers had served all day and sung their plays, and being exhausted, those who needed to leave also wanted to depart first. The weather was still hot, and Ziyun himself felt fatigued, so he had the banquet cleared, served some watermelon and lotus root, and then escorted the guests out of the garden, while the performers each went their separate ways. That single remark from Qinyan would go on to spawn countless bitter hardships; even Xu Ziyun would struggle to shelter him, let alone Ziyu. To know what troubles were to arise, one must listen to the next chapter's unfolding.`]},m=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations25:o},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),i={26:[`Word has it that by the time Young Master Hua returned to his city mansion, the evening lamps had long been lit. He rested briefly in the main hall while Lady Hua inquired about the affairs at Pleasant Garden. After sharing a few details, he summoned two young maids to carry lanterns and guide him to his Star-Lattice bedchamber. Beneath the soft lamplight, he found the ten maids waiting, all clad in identical unlined gowns of pristine white silk. Their hair was elegantly swept into Magu chignons, with long braids trailing behind and single jasmine blossoms pinned proudly at the crown. Below, their trousers were trimmed with dark silk borders, faintly revealing their delicate three-inch lotus steps. As he entered, they formed two graceful lines, greeting him with beaming smiles. Young Master Hua looked them over and asked, "Why the change of attire today?" One stepped forward and replied, "Today, Madame visited the Guanyin Pavilion at the family temple to offer incense. She asked us to change our clothes and accompany her." The Young Master took a seat. The Ten Pearls, all fifteen or sixteen years of age, were remarkably similar in beauty, each as lovely as the last. Mingzhu first offered him a bowl of iced plum broth, while Zhangzhu stood by, gently fanning him with a goose-feather fan. Chengzhu (Pearl) came forward to remove his boots and help him into his cloud-stepping slippers, as Hezhu draped a light gauze robe over his shoulders. Baozhu then asked, "Has the Master dined? Shall we order the inner kitchen to prepare anything?" "I drank too much today and feel parched," the Young Master replied. "After the first watch of the night, follow the recipe I gave you the other day for the ledebouriella porridge. Mix it with the fruit powders and floral dews, and simmer it slowly over alternating high and low heat. It must not touch copper for even a moment. You must make it yourself; do not let those clumsy old women in the kitchen handle it. In this sweltering summer heat, it is a time for dispersion, so use less ledebouriella and peppermint dew, and add more rose, sweet rice, lotus, and osmanthus dew. Portion out the poria, lotus seed, qiongmi, and swallow's nest powders exactly as prescribed." Baozhu agreed and pulled Huazhu along with her. Having prepared the ingredients and fetched a bowl of sweet rice and a jar of snow water, they avoided the kitchen altogether. Instead, under the eaves of the octagonal glazed pavilion just outside his room, they lit a small copper brazier and began to slowly simmer the porridge in a silver pot. Huazhu squatted nearby, a long crimson sash trailing onto the ground, and remarked, "The Master seems quite drunk today. He keeps staring at us, smiling to himself for no reason." "I heard Madame say yesterday that she plans to take you in as a concubine by autumn," Baozhu teased. Huazhu spat lightly in mock disdain. "If she takes anyone, it will be you! You have a sharp mind and know exactly how to curry favor. I doubt you'll even have to wait until autumn!" Baozhu shoved her playfully, sending Huazhu tumbling to the ground. Her legs sprawled, knocking against the silver pot and nearly tipping it over. Scrambling up, Huazhu grabbed Baozhu by the shoulders, trying to wrestle her down, and the two collapsed into a fit of laughter.`,`Presently, Aizhu emerged carrying an amber silk lantern and said, "It is almost the first watch, yet we are still supposed to summon Lin Shanzhi!" "Why call for Lin Shanzhi?" Baozhu asked. "How should I know? It must be something urgent," Aizhu replied. Wearing wooden-soled bow shoes, her rapid steps clattered sharply against the floor. Reaching the side gate, she relayed the message to the older women on duty, who passed it to the inner gatekeepers, until it finally reached the outer servant quarters. Finding Lin Shanzhi enjoying the cool evening air in the courtyard, attended by two young pages preparing his pipe and fanning him, they informed him of the Master's summons. Shanzhi had no choice but to don a long gown, fasten his belt, light a small horn lantern, and follow the inner gatekeeper. He halted by the octagonal glazed pavilion and, after exchanging greetings with Aizhu and the others, asked, "What business does the Master have?"`,`Aizhu lifted the amber lantern to Shanzhi's face, smiling playfully. "Your face is flushed red from drinking; you are sure to meet a rebuff when you go in." Shanzhi chuckled, "When did I drink? It's your red lantern reflecting on my face that makes you think I'm drunk." Aizhu smiled again and led him slowly into the inner chambers, where the Young Master was chatting and laughing with the maids. Aizhu entered first to announce his arrival, and the Master called him forward. Standing by the window, Shanzhi saw the Young Master sitting cross-legged on a Drunken-Elder couch, flanked by four of the Pearls. Upon seeing him, Young Master Hua instructed, "Go and invite Secretary Wei to the Quiet-Green Retreat. I will head over from here; there is something I wish to discuss." Shanzhi replied, "The first watch has already sounded, and the East Garden gates are locked, as are the three main gates. If it is nothing urgent, perhaps it would be better to tell him tomorrow. Furthermore, opening several sets of doors to fetch him from the East Garden will take us well into the second watch, and the secretaries have likely already retired." Knowing the summons was merely to discuss the day's opera, Shanzhi deliberately dissuaded him. Realizing it was indeed not pressing, the Young Master conceded, "Since the gates are locked, tomorrow will not be too late." After a pause, he added, "It was my first time seeing that boy Baozhu perform, and I never expected him to be so magnificent. Why does their troupe never enter the mansion?"`,`"They always arrive early on the first and fifteenth of the month," Shanzhi explained. "But the gatekeepers, seeing the Master is not yet awake, turn them away. When actors come to pay their respects, it is not recorded in the ledger. Even that boy Qinyan was once brought here by his master, though he never made it inside." "Whose apprentice is Qinyan?" the Young Master inquired. "He is Changqing's apprentice." "Changqing... wasn't your master also named Changqing?" "Yes," Shanzhi replied. "This servant originally belonged to the Lianjin Troupe before joining the Dengchun Troupe." "Why did you switch?" "Changqing has a terrible temper, and I offended him. As a result, he traded me for Xiufang of the Dengchun Troupe. Only after Xiufang completed his apprenticeship did Changqing buy Qinyan—he has only been with him for a little over half a year."`,`"And what do you think of this Qinyan?" the Young Master asked. Shanzhi remained silent. When pressed again, he finally answered, "He is quite good, but he is the favored darling of Second Master Xu. Word has it he refuses to entertain anyone else." "Second Master Xu favors Yuan Baozhu, not him," the Young Master countered. "I hear Second Master Xu cherishes them both equally," Shanzhi replied. "They say he has already spent thousands of silver taels on Qinyan." The Young Master fell silent. After a moment, he spoke again. "The other day, Secretary Wei mentioned that Qinyan was extraordinary, yet I only saw him for myself today. There is a young master named Mei whom Qinyan truly favors; his attachment to Second Master Xu is merely an act." "This servant is unaware of such details," Shanzhi said cautiously. "I only just saw Qinyan today as well." "You are of the theater yourself," the Young Master continued. "Tell me, in their joint performance of Pursuing the Dream today, who was truly the better of the two?" Shanzhi thought for a moment before replying. "If we judge strictly on acting, one must consider expression and bearing. In terms of appearance, they are quite matched. However, as for the performance, Baozhu's singing is far more seasoned. Qinyan seemed slightly raw in the first scene, though he improved in the second." The Young Master nodded in agreement. "He is but a beginner, while Baozhu has been performing for two or three years; naturally, he is more polished. Yet, to my eyes, Qinyan possesses the finer appearance. He moves with an ethereal grace, as if set apart from ordinary mortals." Shanzhi lowered his head and offered no reply.`,'Zhangzhu, meanwhile, had been fanning the Master while listening to their conversation. Her mind wandered, and the fan slipped from her grasp, tumbling to the floor. Mingzhu let out a muffled giggle, and Zhangzhu, flushing deeply, hurriedly retrieved it. The Young Master laughed aloud. "Have you never heard opera before? Is mere talk of it enough to steal your hearts away? Someday, I will summon the entire troupe to perform for Madame for a full day, and you shall all reap the benefits." Aizhu chimed in, "What outside troupe could possibly compare to our own within the mansion?" Smiling, the Young Master replied, "There are ten of you as well. If you were to play the male leads and they the female leads in a joint performance, you would soon realize their brilliance." The maids blushed and lowered their heads. "We do not know how to perform," they murmured. "If you wish for an opera, there is always the Eight Ling Troupe." "You could learn," the Young Master laughed. "Female actors are not so uncommon." Shanzhi offered a faint smile. After standing for a moment longer without receiving further orders, he took his leave, finding three or four of the maids still gathered around the brazier. He exchanged a few words with them before departing. Once the fragrant porridge was ready, it was served alongside several delicate, handmade pastries. After cooling himself for a while, the Young Master retired for the night, and the Ten Pearls returned to their respective quarters.',`The following morning, still sluggish from the previous night's wine, Young Master Hua rose late. It was not until the hour of Si that he finally washed his face, allowed the maids to comb his hair, and dressed for the day. Lady Hua, concerned that the wine might have harmed his constitution, sent a maid with a bowl of medicinal ginseng soup, which he drank. Baozhu then entered to report, "Shanzhi is waiting outside to ask for your instructions. Do you still wish to invite Secretary Wei, as you ordered last night?" The Young Master hesitated briefly before replying, "Tell him to invite Secretary Wei to breakfast at the Quiet-Green Retreat." Baozhu agreed and left. The Young Master then visited Lady Hua's chambers, where her morning toilette was complete, with maids attending her on both sides. Seeing her delicately drawn brows, faint rouge, glossy green tresses, and a high chignon piled like clouds, he realized she bore a striking resemblance to Su Huifang. He sat down and exchanged pleasantries, announcing he would dine in her quarters. He excitedly recounted the previous day's opera, declaring the Eight Ling Troupe lacking even a tenth of their skill, and casually mentioned her resemblance to Huifang. Lady Hua was inwardly displeased but held her tongue. They were normally a deeply devoted couple, harmonizing like the qin and se lutes. Though the Young Master loved luxury and extravagance, he was by no means debauched.`,`Lady Hua had several times suggested taking Huazhu and Baozhu as concubines, but the Young Master always refused. "Once they become concubines, their charm will fade," he would say. "It is better to let them serve for a few years and then arrange marriages with handsome youths—a truly meritorious deed. Furthermore, if you were plain-looking, I might naturally seek comfort among the maids. But since you are so beautiful, even the loveliest maids pale in comparison. They are like miscellaneous wildflowers—pretty enough on their own, but if planted upon the peony terrace, they become mere awkward mimicries, maids attempting to play the mistress, only worsening their own embarrassment." Lady Hua was delighted by these words, and thus, no matter how lavishly the Young Master lived, she never harbored jealousy. Even with the Ten Pearls tumbling together in his presence daily, she felt no envy. As for Lin Shanzhi and Feng Zipei, they were mere shadows of suspicion without any real interference. After all, everyone's temperament is different, and he was not obsessed with carnal indulgences like Xi Eleven.`,`The Young Master breakfasted with Lady Hua, and their laughter and conversation carried them past noon, completely forgetting that Wei Pincai was waiting at the Quiet-Green Retreat. When Lin Shanzhi had gone to summon him earlier, Pincai had been awake for quite some time and was just about to eat. Hearing that the Young Master had invited him to breakfast, he was as elated as a scholar hearing his name called at the palace examinations. He hurriedly pulled on his boots, changed into a fresh gown, and, grabbing a round fan, strutted confidently out of the garden alongside Shanzhi without even pausing to inform Zhang or Gu. Along the way, he endlessly flattered his companion, calling him "Old Shan" and "Little Brother," walking shoulder to shoulder until they reached the Retreat. Believing this to be an urgent summons from his patron, he paid no attention to the garden's scenery and walked straight inside. Four young lackeys sitting beneath the gallery rose at the sight of him. Shanzhi asked, "Is there any word on when the Master will emerge?" "Silence," they replied. "We do not know if he will come out at all." Shanzhi turned to Pincai, "Secretary Wei, please wait here. I will go and inquire." With that, he left. Pincai took the opportunity to study the room's appointments, finding them unspeakably sumptuous—even finer than the west salon. The windows, railings, and screens were intricately carved with hollowed miniature landscapes, the figures inlaid with precious gems, creating the illusion of jade pillars and beams. It was an eye-opening sight. However, a full two hours passed, and still the Young Master did not appear. The lackeys refreshed his tea repeatedly, washing his intestines hollow until his stomach rumbled like a hungry kite, yet he could only sit and endure.`,`Meanwhile, Lin Shanzhi waited outside the Crimson Wash Pavilion, chatting with the Ten Pearls, unable to ascend and disturb the Master. Zengzhu remarked, "Earlier, I heard the Master dining with Madame upstairs. Their conversation was quite lively, so he is unlikely to emerge anytime soon." Shanzhi sighed, "What is to be done? Secretary Wei has been summoned since dawn and has waited over two hours. I am famished myself. Have you all eaten?" Mingzhu replied, "We finished long ago. There are leftovers, if you don't mind eating them. It's impossible to guess when he will come out." "Dear sister, your scraps are far beyond my meager fortune," Shanzhi laughed. "I would be honored; how could I possibly disdain them?" Aizhu teased, "Such a silver tongue! I wager your eyes are hungry too." She led him to a side room where summer dishes requiring no heating—both meat and vegetables—were laid out. Shanzhi ate his fill, wiped his hands, and resumed joking with the maids, but the Young Master still did not appear. As the afternoon wore on, Shanzhi grew anxious. "Will he come out or not? Keeping someone waiting like this... Dear Aizhu, please go and tell him that Secretary Wei is still waiting at the Quiet-Green Retreat." "I won't carry your messages," Aizhu retorted. "Go tell him yourself." "Dear sister, if I could step inside, I wouldn't need to beg you." After much delay and teasing from Aizhu, Hezhu finally relented and went inside. Half an hour later, footsteps sounded, and the Young Master emerged. He and his wife had talked until they were exhausted, and he had lain down on her bed to rest. Naturally, no one dared to wake him, and he only stirred as the afternoon waned.`,`Seeing Hezhu inquire, the Young Master suddenly recalled his morning appointment. Realizing it was already late, and that he could only offer supper now, he slipped through a side gate that opened directly onto the courtyard of the Quiet-Green Retreat, which was just one courtyard away. Shanzhi hurried ahead to receive him, and Pincai stepped forward to the window as the Young Master arrived. Pincai bowed, but the Young Master seized his hand. "I meant to speak with you this morning, but yesterday's wine made me oversleep, keeping you waiting. Have you had breakfast?" Pincai lied and said he had eaten. Shanzhi, pitying the man's half-day fast, interjected, "The Secretary arrived at the crack of dawn; he likely hasn't had breakfast yet." The Young Master scolded Shanzhi, "What is your job, if not to feed a guest?" "This servant also entered at dawn and has been waiting inside ever since," Shanzhi explained. The Young Master immediately ordered pastries to be fetched. Shanzhi sprinted off, and soon, eight refined dishes filled a heated kang table. The Young Master urged Pincai to eat, then enthusiastically recounted the entrance of the eight female leads from the previous day, especially praising the duet of Pursuing the Dream by Qinyan and Baozhu.`,`"I lavished great effort in buying those eight, intending to form a troupe that would eclipse all outsiders," the Young Master lamented. "Who would have guessed that outsiders would eclipse us instead? Have you seen their acting?" Catching his drift, Pincai flattered him further. "I have seen them. Eclipsing outsiders is not difficult; fine flowers need not crowd a garden. Simply purchase the choicest few, and that will suffice." Inwardly, Pincai plotted: 'He fancies Qinyan, little knowing that Qinyan's heart belongs to Mei Gengxiang and is unlikely to favor him. Furthermore, Qinyan's snobbery scorns me. If admitted to this mansion, he might change, or he might not, but once inside, he would never glimpse Gengxiang again. The Young Master is unaware of Qinyan's eccentric temper; in time, disgust will surely follow. Better to drag him in, make him suffer for two years, and then my spite will be fully vented.' Aloud, he suggested, "Why not purchase both Baozhu and Qinyan? Join them with your eight to make ten—would that not easily surpass the outside troupes?"`,`"I hear both are Duxiang's favorites; it would be hard to wrest them away," the Young Master replied. "Duxiang truly cherishes Baozhu, not Qinyan," Pincai countered. "If the Master desires him, I can assure you it is possible. I know his master, Changqing, quite well, and often patronize his troupe. Changqing loves silver above all else. If he learns the Master wants him, he will crawl to oblige and deliver the boy gift-wrapped." The Young Master hesitated, weighed down by the thought of Xu Ziyun. Furthermore, acquiring Qinyan alone would only bring the troupe to nine, and obtaining Baozhu seemed impossible. Seeing him waver, Pincai argued and cajoled until the Young Master was finally swayed. "Go tomorrow and probe the matter," he commanded. "If they are unwilling, drop it. Even if we purchase him later, we will trail Duxiang and always be second best." Driven by pride and a loathing to appear as though he were copying Duxiang, he supped with Pincai, their conversation overflowing with praise for Qinyan's talents, and even commending Qiguan, until they parted late in the evening.`,`The next morning, after breakfast, Pincai took Si'er and a large saddle-cart out into the city to find Ye Maolin. Maolin rode with him to Changqing's residence. Along the way, they ran into Zhang Zhongyu. Both carts halted, and Maolin yielded the right of way as Pincai stepped out to speak. When Zhongyu inquired of their errand, Pincai explained the Young Master's commission. "No wonder he was so generous the other day," Zhongyu remarked. "He bestowed over a hundred gold pieces and even gave his own jade pendants to Qinyan and Baozhu."`,`Zhongyu then whispered at length into Pincai's ear. Ye Maolin could not hear the words, but saw Pincai nod emphatically. "I know my own mind," Pincai muttered. "Once he is inside, will he not have to bend?" After exchanging a few more words, they parted ways.`,`Arriving at Qinyan's door, Maolin alighted first and entered alongside Pincai. Fortunately, Changqing was home and invited them to sit. Sizing Pincai up and noting Maolin's modest escort, Changqing assumed he was a person of minor importance and offered a cool greeting. Maolin interjected, "This is Secretary Wei, the closest confidant of Young Master Hua. Hearing of your fame, he has come especially to discuss a matter of business." Changqing instantly bloomed with obsequious warmth, calling him "Secretary this" and "Secretary that." Recognizing Pincai's cleverness, he eagerly courted him. "Young Master Hua favors me beyond measure, and with two of my disciples already in his mansion, his grace is as high as heaven and deep as the earth—truly inexpressible." Tea was served, and after more pleasantries, Changqing prepared his opium lamp, inviting them to lie down. "I do not partake," Maolin declined. "You entertain Secretary Wei; it will make talking easier." Pincai added, "I am but a beginner and cannot prepare the pipe myself." Changqing roasted a prime bowl and passed it to Pincai, who smoked it and returned the pipe. "I am an outsider; I won't offer a courtesy smoke in return." When Pincai asked about Qinyan's recent situation, Changqing boasted, "The lad is doing wonderfully, and he is quite clever. Just the other day, he sang at Second Master Xu's garden, and your patron, the Young Master, rewarded him with ten gold ingots—over fourteen taels, worth some seven hundred strings of cash! Second Master Xu also piled gifts upon him, and the Young Master even gave him his own purse ornament, a yellow-red jade. I asked Sha Huizi at the Degu Studio, and he appraised it at two hundred strings. Without the boy's flattery and charm, who would pamper him so?"`,`Changqing offered another puff, which Pincai accepted before continuing. "Today's business is exactly what I wish to consult you about. Yesterday, our patron saw his performance of Pursuing the Dream and was completely besotted. He praised him all day and wishes to bring him into the mansion. Could you bear to part with him?"`,'Changqing listened, pondering for a moment. "Secretary, it is not that I refuse the honor, but the lad is truly pitiful. He only arrived in the capital last October. I bought him, and he has been a quick study with a handsome face. Even Huifang and Baozhu, at the height of their fame, can barely rival him. You can judge for yourself:"',`"Since his stage debut on the first day of the twelfth month, a mere seven months have passed. Not to mention others, Second Master Xu alone has poured oceans of wealth upon him." Changqing stretched out his hand. "This much! My purse is vast, but the money is spent as fast as it is earned. You see my household of over twenty mouths? We rely entirely on him. If he goes to the mansion, he will thrive, but I will founder. He is only fifteen; with five years left until he completes his apprenticeship, would he not earn tens of thousands for me? How can I relinquish him? He is not like Lin Shanzhi, who had a surly temper, ate too many oil cakes, and constantly vexed me until I traded him for Xiufang. When Xiufang graduated, he brought in eight thousand strings of cash—everyone knows this. Qinyan surpasses Xiufang many times over. Secretary, I beg you to plead with the Young Master. He is my only fine disciple, my lifelong reliance. The other boys are worthless; they only cost me money for costumes, attend a few wine banquets a month, and constantly cause trouble..." Pincai was about to reply when Ye Maolin stroked his beard and smiled. "Old Qing, everything is negotiable. You know Young Master Hua's ways. Others crawl to enter his doors, yet he seeks you out. This is your great fortune! If he catches the Master's fancy, it secures not just one lifetime, but two. Later, you could enter the mansion yourself, obtain a minor post, a minor rank, and great face—surely that beats eating opera rice?" "Maestro Ye speaks plainly," Pincai added. "A mere glance from him rewards one with gold. For a serious hire, would he ever stint? Your entire life would be secured. An early graduation pays more; a late one pays less. He is fourteen or fifteen, and his body is still developing. What if he takes a bad turn in a year or two? Then what? Thousands of strings wasted! I urge you to reflect on this. Anything is negotiable, and you will face no injustice." Having smoked over a dozen bowls, Changqing sat up. "That sounds fair enough. We can discuss it, but I must also ask his will." Pincai laughed coldly. "Old Qing, bright folks hide nothing. You know Qinyan's temper as well as I do. Aside from Second Master Xu, who else favors him? Who else will he oblige? Offend Second Master Xu..." He paused with a sneer, "And your lifelong reliance will reverse. At home, you surely know why he fell ill. It was all over Young Master Mei! Everyone knows they click. When he drinks with Mei, he is pleased; without Mei, there are only tears, and everyone tires of it. You are not blind; you merely protect your disciple's face, though the truth is ugly. If you are banking on Young Master Mei to lift him up, you are waiting for a rooster to lay eggs! Think carefully: is my counsel kind or cruel?" Changqing resented Qinyan's temper, which was hindering his ability to inflate the price, and the truth of Pincai's words struck hard. Unable to argue, he conceded, "The boy is indeed stubborn. You are a glass lantern, seeing right through it all. Whatever the case, I must lean on him. If the numbers don't work, there is no deal." Maolin pulled Changqing toward the window and whispered, "Name your price, so Wei can report back." Thinking the Young Master was a legendary sucker, Changqing wanted ten thousand but settled on whispering a figure of five or six thousand to Maolin. Maolin relayed this to Pincai, who shook his head. "Impossible. A boy for ten thousand? He is not some miraculous good. Four or five thousand is suitable." "It is a ballpark figure," Maolin reasoned. "The best actors graduate for three or four thousand strings; this is more than double that." Changqing shook his head repeatedly before finally saying, "At that price, it is impossible. He has only been here half a year. Money aside, my heart cannot bear to release him. I never wished him gone." He wiped his eyes, feigning tears. Pincai thought to himself, 'Crafty devil! Asking for a sky-high price! How can I report this to the Young Master? Even if he doesn't suspect graft, others will. I must crush him with bluster.' Feigning outrage, he stood up. "Splendid! Go grow rich yourself, then! I meant to show you kindness, and you bite my hand. In a few days, you will crawl begging to me, entirely worthless." With that, he stormed out.`,`Ye Maolin eyed Changqing, who, seeing the rage, smiled placatingly. "Secretary, please calm down. Sit, let us discuss this." "Discuss what?" Pincai retorted. "I have no patience for hollow talk. Maolin, you may sit, but I am leaving." He marched out, and Maolin chased after him, clutching his arm. "Wait for me at the Six Harmonies Inn," Pincai whispered, shaking him off and vanishing into the cart. Unable to escort him, Changqing dragged Maolin back inside. "He left in wrath," Maolin warned. "Speak plainly! Offending Young Master Hua is ill-advised. When Wei returns with gossip, a storm will brew. What will you do then?" "I am not dull," Changqing replied. "But he is my cash tree; I cannot bear it. I must ask his will. Give me two days to reply, and I will curry favor. Perhaps yes, perhaps no." Hearing little flexibility, Maolin took a cart and left. He found Pincai and repeated Changqing's words verbatim. Helpless, Pincai returned and had Shanzhi report that he could not locate Changqing, deciding to try again later. Whether this would decide Qinyan's fate remains to be seen in the next installment.`]},c=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations26:i},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),s={27:["As for Wei Pincai returning from Changqing's, he had caught the slippery tone—a ransom of perhaps ten thousand taels of silver.","He meant to find a way to harry him, leaving him no peace, until he submitted to entering the mansion. Pincai had no other schemes; he looked for Zhang Zhongyu once but missed him. He planned again to send the carter Ji San and the boys to kick up trouble, but wanted to delay a few days so they wouldn't smell the plot. Young Master Hua had been enthusiastic on a whim; besides, his pleasures were inexhaustible, and he would hardly fix on one person alone.","This installment turns to another man. Earlier you heard of him, but now you must meet him in person. Who is he? It is Xi Eleven. He had hidden for a month with the Changlu salt administration, just as a fleet of foreign ships arrived. His family being foreign-trade merchants with old ties, he wired twenty thousand taels, threw in five thousand taels' worth of foreign goods, and was flush again. The wooden barrel affair had broken and the scandal had cooled, so he returned to the capital and raised hell just as before.",`Xi Eleven came from a great clan, so had he no proper name? His formal name was Xi Zhengshen, but folk had called him Eleven so long that it stuck. In Lingnan slang, "eleven" sounds like "earth," so they dubbed him Xi the Rustic. First entering the capital, he had spoken thick Cantonese that was barely intelligible, but now he was sliding into mandarin speech. He kept company with two others on good terms: Zhang Zhongyu and Pan Qiguan. Zhongyu haunted the bustling haunts and flattered the account shops. Seeing Xi Eleven's wave-rider spending, he had ferreted out his family's bottom line and befriended him thoroughly. When Xi's silver ran dry and loans were due, Zhongyu went to Pan San's silver shop for him for a ten-thousand-tael note, of which nine-tenths was actually paid out. Zhongyu skimmed one thousand, and Xi received only eight thousand, but when silver was wanted, he had to swallow it. Now that his money had returned, he paid that loan first. Back in the capital with nothing to do, he spent his days eating, drinking, and watching opera with Zhongyu and Pan. The three were cut from one cloth, and their intimacy deepened daily. Pan too loved "carriage" gossip; when dry-road pleasures were discussed, Xi counted him a soul mate. Comparing Xi with Pan, Pan was somewhat better. Repulsive though he looked, his nature was soft: first, he was timid; second, his wife was fierce; third, he was rich and feared being shaken down. He had lust in his heart yet tiny courage, and never dared indulge, which nearly counted as a virtue. Xi Eleven, riding on wealth and influence, was utterly lawless; people nicknamed him the Smoke-Scorched Grand Divinity. With hangers-on egging him, nothing was sacred. His fixed notion was to linger in the capital barely a year, and once selected, to leave. Unless he debauched to the limit, folks would sneer that he was unworthy of a gentleman-official's son. Lately awaiting selection, he had hired a clerk for lawsuits and accounts, recommended by Director Wang at twelve hundred taels a year salary. They already lodged together, and the clerk began playing sycophant in advance. Guess who? That same Mr. Ji from the dung-cart. Seeing Xi due for posting—a Zhili department magistrate, a fat stint where one or two years could yield a fortune—and knowing Xi was a lavish, muddle-headed son, the yamen work would surely fall to him. He had begged Sun Liangong to speak to Wang Wenhui, and it was settled at once. Birds of a feather, another sewer-raker of matching stench, within days he had read his patron's temper and set out to curry favor. He had already lent Ba Yingguan several times, and Xi was delighted. Ba Yingguan having served grandees, the clerk grew arrogant before long, got new robes pressed, and felt splendid. When Ji Liangxuan begged him for urgent favors, he now got grudging half-service, not the old obedient ease.`,"Enough digression. One day Zhang Zhongyu breakfasted at Xi Eleven's lodgings; the host and two guests summoned two young actors. Zhongyu chased coin, not flesh; in those circles he feigned manners and never indulged truly, while Xi and Ji, without this, had no hobby. Xi Eleven was especially low; even during one meal he had to slip inside twice. Once there was only one boy, but now Ba Yingguan was added, even more devoted than Chunlan. Ba Yingguan's skin, though dark, was sleek and glossy—the oil trick—so lovers swarmed, as Ji Liangxuan, drunk, had told people.",`That day over wine, Xi Eleven called Chunlan inside once, emerged, sat a while, then called Ba Yingguan in. Zhongyu guessed his errands and chatted with Liangxuan. The two actors, Rongguan and Chunlin, were Xi's usual calls. Rongguan signaled Chunlin, and Chunlin smiled. Liangxuan too rolled his eyes; Zhongyu glimpsed it but didn't grasp it and didn't ask. Rongguan suddenly asked Zhongyu: "You know a patron surnamed Wei. I met him first when he came to the capital, and he was nothing special then. The other day at Master Fu San's, he had a grand saddle, hot carriage, and lackeys mounted. What office does he hold now?" Zhongyu replied, "No office yet; he's a secretary at Young Master Hua's, struck silver, and is naturally plush." Liangxuan added, "I hear Young Master Hua's house tops wealth short of the throne. Is it true?"`,`Zhongyu laughed: "Outside gossip. Still, Hua lands span forty-odd estates, and the annual rent rivals a prefecture's grain taxes; wealth indeed." Liangxuan nodded: "Our patron mentions often that Young Master Hua is his father's generation uncle, and Lord Hua was my old patron governor's teacher. With such a connection, yet our patron won't cultivate it. I've urged visits, but my patron says that was from army promotion ties; Young Master Hua was a child, wouldn't recognize him, and won't go. It's the same with Xu Zhongtang, now governor-general at Guangdong—same army bond. Only Second Young Master remains in the capital. The other day I saw him with seven or eight horses trailing a flying-eave carriage; he looks destined for governor rank. Our patron still won't visit. Who knows his temper." Zhongyu laughed: "Second Master Xu is lavish indeed, a pair with Young Master Hua. For your patron I begged and strained, but he doesn't even know, and I didn't tell him. By rights your patron owes me heavy thanks." Liangxuan pressed to know what matter, but Zhongyu smiled: "You'll learn. No need now." Xi Eleven emerged, sliding in grass sandals, triple-dyed fragrant-cloud trousers, and a pheasant-pattern ramie jacket, with Ba Yingguan behind holding a black lacquer foreign fan, pattering heavy-light laughter. Liangxuan rose, Zhongyu nodded, Xi seated himself, and Zhongyu asked: "Busy today?"`,`Xi smiled and then said, "Small errands—cleared." He added, "Sorry to leave you; a few more cups?" Zhongyu said, "Enough wine." Xi pressed, "Good words—two more rounds, and we'll finger-guess." Zhongyu agreed, "Fine." Xi guessed ten rounds with Zhongyu, Liangxuan, Rongguan, and Chunlin. His early calls were clear, but later came bizarre yowls; Cantonese guessing sounds the worst, like slaughtering dogs and ducks.`,`With guessing done and in idle talk, Zhongyu suddenly asked Xi: "There's a prime actor called Qinyan lodging at Autumn-Water Hall with his teacher Changqing. Have you met him?" Xi replied, "Not seen, but heard he's good." As Zhongyu began to speak, Rongguan cut in: "Good how? Only two or three plays. Summon him to pour wine, and he won't speak the whole feast. Pour for him? It never happens." Chunlin added, "Such airs! Visit his house, and he never receives. Even Wang Jiqing and Li Chunfang of old, or today's stars Yuan Baozhu and Su Huifang, never had such pretense. He only seats those he favors. Those he dislikes are ignored outright; gifts get no thanks, and he never bows." Xi boasted, "Such an actor hasn't met me. If he met me, I'd curse the whore-born; could he bite off my balls?" Zhongyu sneered, "Forget it, Xi the Rustic. Your two elder patron friends, famous young lords, couldn't bend him and often ate his temper. You'd never cross his threshold, let alone dream of seeing him." Liangxuan interjected, "How so? I don't believe it. Won't a grandee's silver tempt him?" Zhongyu explained, "With others, silver smokes them out. But this cub? Silver won't move him." Xi said, "Preposterous. Don't spin tall tales. Watch me go; he'll come out and treat me fine."`,`Rongguan said, "Unlikely. Maybe a glimpse counts as a favor, but treat you well? Never. I've never seen him warm; even to great officials, he's cold. His junior disciples Tianfu and Tianshou butter people up, but they have ugly faces and folk dislike them. His teacher Cao Changqing has an odd temper, loves only silver, and with silver in hand, ignores people." Xi refused to believe it and said to Zhongyu, "Wait, come with me, and see how it is." Zhongyu replied, "I'll go anywhere but there, and the other day I cursed him." They ate, sat a while, and then Zhongyu left. The two actors carried on awhile, then departed.`,"Xi Eleven stayed overnight. The next morning after breakfast, remembering Zhongyu's boasts about Qinyan's fierceness, he had to test it. After taking opium, along with Ji Liangxuan, Chunlan, and Ba Yingguan, he wore a fresh gauze jacket and took a carriage. Chunlan and Ba Yingguan rode on the footboards, while Liangxuan hired another cart, and they set off to Autumn-Water Hall.","Meanwhile, Qinyan was miserable. Changqing, seeing Pincai storm off, had begged Ye Maolin to mediate, but with no word returned, he grew uneasy. He remembered the two men raising a row in May, requiring a four-string payoff and apologies, rumored to be Hua mansion carters. With yesterday's threatening tone from Pincai, it seemed inevitable. Changqing worried, and entered to consult Qinyan. Since returning from the Garden of Contentment, Qinyan had wept daily, himself unsure why, feeling only grief, as if calamity loomed. His mind was roiling: first, Young Master Hua favored him and was soon summoning him to the mansion—how would he navigate that crowd? Second, at every lively scene Gengxiang was absent, so his sorrow deepened. He had never expected Pincai would come wanting to buy him.",`Changqing entered, found Qinyan weeping, didn't know why, comforted him briefly, and raised Pincai's words: his angry exit, his intent to make trouble, and block his singing. He asked Qinyan's heart: entering meant pleasure, but also becoming a lifelong bondservant, forever a Hua household menial. Qinyan heard this and burst out wailing. Changqing panicked and comforted him. Qinyan sobbed, "Teacher, you took me as a disciple and trained me for half a year; I owe you gratitude, so I endured, and lived two months more. Now teacher discards me. I won't flee elsewhere to spare teacher trouble; simply, if I'm gone, teacher rests easy." Through more tears, Changqing sighed: "Not so. I can't bear to lose you. I'm only consulting you, for I fear crossing them will spark trouble and everyone suffers. They're not taking you for free; they promise thousands. But my sums don't work, and I'd lose on the deal, so I'm undecided. Entering means splendid days for you, but only pain for me." Qinyan replied, "If teacher needs silver, it's easy. I earned plenty here for a year. If I enter, that stops. Can I earn more? If teacher is short, there's no need to sell me; I'll feed teacher for years yet." Changqing said, "My mind matches yours, but there are many snags. Don't mind me, decide for yourself. Don't cry. I must go out; Tianfu and Tianshou are at the theater. If you're bored, summon Sulan. He's off stage today, and you two can talk." Speaking thus, he left and sent for Sulan at once.`,'Just inside, Xi Eleven reached the gate. Chunlan and Ba Yingguan descended and asked within. When answered that no one was home, Xi felt anger rising and entered himself. Liangxuan was still outside when a man stepped up grinning: "Sir, seeking whom? If actors, none are inside." He blocked the way with no "please." Xi was three parts furious and about to bark, but Chunlan spoke first: "Ah, this is Great Master Xi! Whether the actors are home or not, you always invite Master inside. How can you block the door?"','The man stepped back, "Please, Master, have tea inside," and opened the inner gate. Xi and Liangxuan crossed the courtyard and entered the salon, which spanned three bays: The east was partitioned as a guest room. Opposite were two bays: one gate room, and one empty.',`Both entered the inner seating area, while Yingguan and Chunlan remained in the outer room. The man served two cups of tea, recognized Chunlan, asked their provenance, and informed Changqing. Changqing said, "I already said I'm not home, so there's no need to entertain." He thought, "This Xi, I've heard he's a sucker and tasteless. Many actors have been ruined with no redress, and Qinyan is hopeless with him. Or I could summon Tianfu and Tianshou; maybe there are sweets to be had." He sent for them from the theater, donned his coat and boots, and went out to receive Xi Eleven.`,`Meanwhile, Lu Sulan arrived and asked Qinyan: "What's wrong?" He found him tear-stained and collapsed on the kang. Sulan comforted him: "Again? Why did you fetch me?" Qinyan answered, "This time I truly die; it's not like before when I was merely enduring." Sulan pressed for answers, and Qinyan relayed Changqing's dilemma. Sulan was shocked and silent, then asked, "What is your teacher's intent?" Qinyan replied, "Teacher is undecided; both paths are hard. Only my death can close it." Sulan admonished, "Death is your first word. Think slowly. There is no riot yet, no forcing; there must be an easy path." Qinyan lamented, "What path? Forget Wei Pincai; would he spare my life? It's all because Yaoqing ruined me." Sulan asked, "How did Yaoqing ruin you?" Qinyan wept mutely, and as Sulan pressed, he sighed: "Had Yaoqing sung 'Waking from a Dream' on the sixth day of the New Year, I wouldn't have mounted the stage. I was clean until then, with no attachments. He forced me to understudy, bringing half a year of joyless days, leading to the disaster the other day. That 'Pursuing the Dream' ended me—a duet with Yaoqing. Had he eased his performance, I wouldn't mind; but he crushed me on stage, a deep shame, so I strove to match him. This sparked everything. It is always Yaoqing's fault." Sulan countered, "I've seen Young Master Hua; he harbors no cruelty. I've never seen him ruin actors. If there are no worries in your heart, a few years there might bring profit; it is only missing Gengxiang that hurts you." Qinyan replied, "Even if Hua is good, won't Wei Pincai corrupt him?"`,`Sulan advised, "If you suit his taste, Wei Pincai cannot harm you; he must instead flatter you. But heed one word: do not harbor resentment. Our sort befriend hearts, yet we must not fall into excess. Duxiang and Gengxiang favor you equally. Gengxiang is fixated upon you and will not shift, so you fixate on him and will not budge—that is devotion. Yet Gengxiang is unlike others; he is young, rarely ventures out, and everything is inconvenient. If his parents learn of this, they will lecture him and block his outings; if they trace your bond, they will blame you. That is one point. Your heart may hold Gengxiang, but your face must not show it, or folk will say you profess equal treatment yet maintain a double standard. What bond can you truly have with Gengxiang? That is two. For now, Duxiang bears your slights, though you have long been cold. If you lose Duxiang, you suffer little, but your teacher suffers greatly; now Duxiang feeds him, saving you from seeking other patrons. If Duxiang cools, your teacher will squeeze silver from you. Who else treats you like Duxiang? The bitterness you will face then will exceed the Hua mansion. That is three. And if Gengxiang tries to rescue you but remains helpless, there will be only tears and news of illness. You two will be forced apart, dreaming of each other while your body turns like a millstone and your mind burns in alchemy; bearing that is true death. That is four. If you enter the mansion, there may be a way out. I've heard the Young Master loves uniting devoted lovers. If he learns of your deathless bond, he might unite you, or on rest days, you could leave, visit me, and call Gengxiang freely. If you ranked Xi and Pan among them, I would vouch this could never happen, but entering might just be better."`,'This speech was as limpid and clear as a crystal stream, completely unveiling the truth. Even a fool could grasp it, let alone the quick-witted Qinyan. He nodded: "I foresee this. It weighs on my heart now, and will haunt events later, leaving my soul heavy. I will ease my conscience and await whatever joy or pain comes. At worst, I will give my life to repay this feeling, with no regret."','Sulan sighed, "Die for love? We can. But weigh the cause. To die for Gengxiang—why? You yourself cannot articulate it. Once dead, you are not a martyred, chaste widow; the wise will pity a lovesick fool, but fools will call you an idiot, and guess at darker tales. After your death, Gengxiang will see through it all. He has parents to honor, a career to strive for, and a wife to wed for an heir. Why would he die for you? He will laugh and realize it was all an illusion of flowers in the mirror and the moon in the water, vanishing in the blink of an eye. You will be alone in a lonely underworld, waiting for him with no voice to sing your The Piteous Appeal ever again. Dying twice is a hard fate."',`Speaking thus, he laughed; Qinyan laughed too, and then cried: "Brother Lan, Brother Lan! I utterly admire you; whence comes such insight?" Sulan needed the privy and asked whether the small courtyard behind had one. Qinyan said, "There's a latrine, quite clean." Sulan opened the small rear door and went out. A knocking sounded, and the east-corner postern gate rattled. "Who's there?" A voice answered from outside: "It's Wang Lanbao from across the way, sending melons for Qinyan." Qinyan had it opened; a porter carried four watermelons in, saying Lanbao praised the fruit and had used the rear gate to save half a li of walking. Qinyan tipped him two hundred cash, sent his thanks, and asked if Lanbao were home; the porter said yes and left by the rear. When Sulan returned, Qinyan split a melon and they ate deliciously. Suddenly, an uproar erupted outside. The runner went out and came back frantic: "Disaster! That Xi is raising heaven-high havoc; your teacher's been knocked flat." Before he finished, Qinyan and Sulan's souls nearly fled their bodies. Sulan ordered, "Bar the doors and have them padlocked outside!" They slipped through the rear gate to Wang Lanbao's house.`,`Meanwhile, Changqing came out to Xi Eleven, bowed, and studied him. Xi was burly and tall, his smoky face dark with anger; his companion was weasel-snouted and mouse-eyed, short, with two handsome tail-boys. He recognized Chunlan and ushered them into the guest room. Xi declared, "I am surnamed Xi. You must have heard of me, so there's no need for much speech. I hear you keep a Qinyan here; I have come specifically to meet him. Bring him out at once." Changqing smiled apologetically: "Qinyan happens to be out; he has gone into the city."`,`Xi frowned: "He's not in the city every day; why today of all days? Nonsense! Bring him out quickly. Why hide him? You might hide others, but didn't you ask who I am? Do you think you can hide from me? Don't play stupid." Changqing saw his ill intent, smiled, and stalled, planning a delay; he had to deploy a big hat first to intimidate him. Heaping on smiles, he said: "In truth, our troupe lately staged new plays. After a month at the Garden, they just opened. The other day, Young Master Hua saw them at Lord Xu's and summoned them to the mansion. They have sung for two days, with three days remaining. Qinyan's roles are many, and the Young Master favors him. Anywhere else we would recall him, but for that mansion, we small folk dare not approach. If my Lord shares ties with them, send a steward with a card and demand him out; if he suits you, keep him. I have long heard of the Great Master's fame and often wished to invite you, but I feared your noble feet would not tread on low ground. Having never served you, I didn't presume. Don't suspect us of hiding him. Why would we? Even for middling callers, we produce him."`,`Saying this, he stepped close to Xi, fanned him, and grinned: "Loosen your jacket. Lie on the kang if you like; I have aged mud opium." Seeing such deference, Xi's anger faded, and he couldn't explode. Hearing opium offered—exactly his vice—he said, "If he is truly away, let it be. I do not boast lightly; every actor in the capital knows me. If you can read cues, bring him to call in a few days. Even Young Master Hua and I are family friends; you tell him I summoned him, and he cannot refuse to give face." He undid two buttons. Changqing peeled off his jacket, folded it, handed it to Chunlan, and ushered him into the smoking room; Liangxuan followed.`,`Xi's smoking apparatus traveled with him; Chunlan unpacked it from a pocket piece by piece onto the kang. Changqing attended him and burned several bowls. A wicked idea struck him, and he smiled: "This small one has two more disciples, one named Tianfu and one Tianshou. Today let them serve you, and another day I will bring Qinyan to your mansion, if the Great Master would honor us." Xi, smoke-lulled and lazy to rise, seeing such zeal, nodded: "Call them for a look." Changqing sent for Tianfu and Tianshou. They reached the kang, and Xi raised his eyes: one had a round face, the other sharp, both with clear and fair features, matching lake-blue gauze robes, and powder-soled boots.`,"After bowing, they greeted Liangxuan. Changqing had them attend the pipe, and then slipped away.",`Tianfu lay opposite Xi, and Tianshou perched on the edge of the kang. Liangxuan dragged a stool beside the kang, watching them smoke. Chunlan and Ba Yingguan peered from the doorway curtain. Tianshou climbed beside Xi, studying his jade bracelet, while Tianfu leaned across the lamp tray, holding Xi's hand; both admired him. Liangxuan climbed onto the kang, and the two actors took turns burning the opium. Tianfu pressed against Xi, while Tianshou leaned on Liangxuan, whispering on both sides. Liangxuan ignored the heat and hugged Tianshou. Ba Yingguan coughed, hawked, and spat inside; Liangxuan fanned himself, muttering, "Sweltering." Xi pinned a leg over Tianfu and passed one mouthful of smoke split two ways.`,"Chunlan and Ba Yingguan couldn't stomach it. They wandered inward and saw some women—Changqing's kin. The small east door was ajar, so they pushed through into quietness. There was a big plum tree and three rooms; the east windows had green gauze with the blinds drawn. They crept to the window and peeked through the crack. There were two peerless actors on the kang: one silent, one whispering. Chunlan knew them both.",`Sulan suddenly turned, saw a shadow in the window crack, and asked, "Who's there?" The two tittered and bolted. Sulan rose to look, but Qinyan said, "Why look? It is naturally Tianfu and Tianshou making mischief." Sulan was still uneasy, still frightened from before, and ordered the door barred to all visitors. Chunlan told Ba Yingguan, "They told the Master that Qinyan wasn't home, but isn't he sitting right there on the bed?" Ba asked, "Who's the other?" Chunlan replied, "It's Sulan. Wait till we tell the Master; he won't spare them." They returned to the door and still heard Xi asking, "When does Qinyan return?" Tianfu was about to answer when Chunlan cut in: "They lied, Master! Qinyan is inside talking with Sulan on the bed, and they told you he was singing in the city!" When Xi heard this, his heart caught fire, and he leapt up. Tianfu and Tianshou tried to grab him, but Xi flung them off, and both fell. Xi demanded, "Where's Qinyan?" Chunlan pointed: "Behind, through the small gate." Xi was beyond furious and stormed inward. Changqing heard the commotion and rushed to intercept him, smiling: "Great Master, where are you bound? Inside are the women's quarters." Xi roared, "I am not here for your women!" and tried again. Changqing knew word had leaked—the gatekeeper must have seen and sent word within—so how could he block him? Xi shoved him, and Changqing stumbled flat.`,`Xi entered the yard. The windows were lowered; he poked the gauze, found it empty, turned to the center, and found the door locked. He demanded the keys. Changqing ran up: "That is my kinsman Wu's room; the keys were carried off, and there's nothing worth seeing inside." Xi hesitated, but Chunlan interjected: "This small one saw him, and so did Ba Yingguan; he must be under the bed." Changqing protested, "In broad daylight, you are seeing ghosts!" Chunlan shot back, "I saw no ghosts; you're the one talking ghost-talk." Xi could endure no more. With two or three kicks, he shattered the door and searched around. Chunlan lifted the bed curtains and looked under the bed, too—it was empty.`,`Xi saw a small rear door open, looked into the yard, and found the back gate ajar. Knowing they had fled, he was beside himself. First, he ripped down Qinyan's bed hangings, flipped the table, and smashed odds and ends to the floor. Changqing was inwardly furious but dared not vent it. When he tried to argue, Xi seized him and delivered five slaps in a round-robin. Changqing wanted to strike back but was overmatched, half his face turning red. He shouted, "My ancestor, release me! Let us speak outside. Who paid you to raid us without cause? I eat the rice of the opera, and I have never seen such unprovoked beatings; there is street justice!" Xi stayed silent, dragged Changqing out, and threw him again. Liangxuan finally intervened and pacified him, as the family pulled them apart. Xi sat while Changqing rose, his eyes wide, only gasping. Liangxuan dressed Xi and dragged him out. As he walked, Xi cursed, "Today you slipped away, but tomorrow I will search this turtle nest and find you! I will smash this whore-womb den; hide him in your sea of women, and I will still dredge him out!" Liangxuan pulled him into the cart, still cursing. Liangxuan boarded as well; Tianfu and Tianshou had vanished.`,`Changqing took that beating mutely, shut the gate, and sent someone to Lanbao's house for Qinyan. Sulan had sent Lanbao back as well. They all spoke of Xi Eleven's brute savagery and found it terrifying. Perhaps someone had stirred him up, and he wouldn't rest. The feud with Wei Pincai was unsettled, and now Xi had joined in; what then? Changqing felt even more helpless and frightened, while Qinyan only wept. Lanbao offered, "I have a sound plan, if Yuyi agrees. Tomorrow, deliver him to Young Master Hua's. If they offer a ransom, refuse. Say the boy might breach etiquette, so let him serve on trial for two months, then judge. If there is trouble, request leave and bring him out on days off. When the Young Master sees him not on stage, he must reward him, bringing wealth and power, and idle folk won't come knocking. Once inside, if he proves unsuitable, he will still be dismissed, leading to the same end. If you demand a ransom first, they will haggle and anger will rise, which is bad. Once inside, his life and death are in their hands." Before he finished, Sulan clapped his hands: "Sound! Boss Cao, will you hear this?" That speech cleared every face; even Qinyan, having seen today's havoc, yielded. Changqing was convinced. That night they moved to Sulan's. The next day, Xi came searching, found him gone, and left cursing.`,"As to what followed, listen to the following chapter for the explanation."]},w=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations27:s},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),r={28:["Word came that after his beating, Changqing was beside himself. He had no choice but to beseech Ye Maolin once more, and together they made their way to Secretary Wei’s study in the Young Master Hua mansion. There they offered thorns on their bare backs to beg pardon, pledging to hand the boy over immediately without taking a single string of cash. Seeing him so meek and compliant, and refusing all purchase money, Pincai swelled with triumph. He thought his earlier fit of anger had successfully shamed them into delivering the goods directly to his door, exactly as he had predicted. He then sought out Lin Shanzhi, had the Young Master called, and made his report. Young Master Hua asked, 'Why ask no price?' Pincai replied, 'First, he fears the child may not know the proper rules; second, if there is any fault and your lordship wearies of him, he can still quietly take him back. Thus, he dares not accept the money.' The Young Master nodded. 'That will do. He may enter tomorrow. But once he is part of my household, whether a price was paid or not, he shall not be permitted to drink wine or sing in the outside world.' Pincai readily agreed, 'Of course. How many heads could Changqing possibly have to dare such a thing?' Young Master Hua then instructed Shanzhi, 'Tell the accounts office to allocate two hundred taels of silver a month to Changqing, and have him draw it at the mansion monthly.' Shanzhi assented, departed with Pincai, and upon meeting Changqing, explained everything. Pincai piled on the supposed favors he had done, leaving Changqing overjoyed. He kowtowed his profound thanks to Pincai and took his leave. Returning home, he informed Qinyan of the arrangement. At this juncture, Qinyan knew there was no escape. This disaster had arisen within his own walls, yet Ziyu remained completely unaware. Tomorrow, he would enter the Hua mansion, and who could say when they might ever see each other again? He longed to bid Ziyu a proper farewell, yet he feared a meeting would only shatter them both; moreover, there were too many prying eyes and insurmountable obstacles. He wanted to write a letter, but his mind was a tangled knot of sorrow. Ten thousand words could not find their way to the brush, so he could only entreat Sulan to convey his innermost feelings. After much thought, he took the silk handkerchief he always used to dry his tears, selected four medicinal herbs and wrapped them separately, enclosed them in the handkerchief, sealed the entire bundle in an outer paper, and entrusted it to Sulan, imploring her to place it directly into Ziyu's hands when she saw him.","The next morning, Changqing delivered Qinyan to the Hua mansion. The Young Master scrutinized him again in fine detail, found himself exceedingly pleased, and assigned him to attend to them at the Quiet-Green Retreat. Qinyan was also led in to kowtow before Lady Hua. Observing his frail, graceful form and noting the complete absence of any vulgar actor's air, she uttered a single word of approval: 'Good.' As for the Young Master's satisfaction, it goes without saying. Yet Qinyan's heart remained anchored to Ziyu; he could only weep in secret and sigh softly when no one was looking. After a few days, observing that the Young Master’s temper was quite proper and devoid of any indecent pestering, his mind eased slightly. However, toward Wei Pincai, he maintained the stony silence of Lady Xi of old. Pincai found himself utterly powerless; any malicious design to 'settle' the boy was, for the moment, entirely out of the question.","As for the items Qinyan had left behind at parting, which were entrusted to Sulan to give to Ziyu, Sulan planned to invite Ziyu over in a few days to speak with him heart to heart.","Now concerning Ziyu: ever since that single, fleeting conversation with Qinyan in the fifth month, it was not that he hadn't tried to visit him, but countless misunderstandings had intervened. One cool, pleasant day, after his morning meal, Ziyu made his way to Sulan’s place. He first had Yun inquire if she was home; upon hearing this, Sulan was delighted and hurried out to welcome him in. However, two others emerged from the room: Ziyu recognized one as Wang Lanbao, and the other as Qiguan. Not having seen Qiguan for some time, Ziyu took a closer look. His face was as smooth as almond paste, his willow brows arched like verdant peaks. Radiant and gracefully slender, he appeared about the same age as Sulan, though slightly smaller in stature. Ziyu stepped forward and greeted them.","Ziyu said, 'I truly did not expect Xiangwan to be hosting such distinguished guests today.' Lanbao teased, 'This is your little sister-in-law. Have you two paid your familial respects yet?' Ziyu replied, 'What kind of talk is this? Where does such a title come from?' Sulan chimed in, 'Actually, the title makes some sense.' Qiguan felt embarrassed and protested, 'Jingfang, please don't joke.' Lanbao countered, 'It hardly counts as a joke. You are Yunong's junior apprentice-brother, does that not make you his little sister-in-law?' Ziyu laughed, 'That is utterly absurd.' With that, they all took their seats. Noticing the table was strewn with cups and plates from a recent meal, Sulan had the servants clear it away. She then personally brought Ziyu a bowl of tea and asked, 'Your visit today is quite a coincidence; you must have already heard the news.' Ziyu listened with bewilderment and asked, 'What news should I have heard? I genuinely do not know.' Lanbao stared at Ziyu and said, 'You really don't know? It has already been five or six days. Even if you haven't left the house, has truly no one told you?'","Ziyu grew even more baffled, the thought never crossing his mind that this might concern Qinyan. He said, 'I honestly have no idea what you are talking about. I haven't stepped past my front gates, and no one has visited me these past two days. How could I know of matters in the outside world?' Qiguan offered a small smile, while Sulan said, 'Since you truly don't know, I have no choice but to tell you. But brace yourself. Jingfang, Yuyan, you two support him while I explain.' Ziyu replied, 'Xiangwan, you have always been straightforward. Why adopt these dramatic airs today? Surely it cannot be anything so astonishing; you are merely trying to startle me.' Sulan looked at Ziyu again and again, which provoked laughter from Lanbao and Qiguan. Observing their behavior, Ziyu's suspicions grew. 'Xiangwan, please, just speak,' he urged. Sulan hesitated, 'It is somewhat difficult to say. But showing you a certain item will make it clear.' Ziyu still had no inkling of what was happening. He watched as Sulan retrieved a paper bundle from a small visiting-card box. It looked like a sealed letter, but the label bore no writing, and the shape was square. Taking it in his hands, it felt practically weightless; pressing it gently, it yielded softly, as though something was folded inside. Tearing away the paper seal, he found a piece of white silk, resembling a handkerchief. His doubts intensifying, he gave it a shake, and four smaller paper packets tumbled out. Lanbao and the others stepped closer to look. Ziyu opened the packets and spread them on the table; they contained four distinct medicinal herbs, none of which he recognized. Sulan asked, 'What medicines are these?' Ziyu replied, 'I do not know. Let me ask you: what is the meaning of showing this to me? And how is it that you yourself don't know?' The three remained silent, their eyes fixed on the packets of herbs. Seeing their apparent confusion, Ziyu's bewilderment deepened. He asked Sulan, 'Whose belongings are these, really? You are all speaking in riddles.' Sulan answered, 'I am not the one giving this to you. The one you dream of day and night left this for you upon his departure, instructing me to pass it on. I assumed it was something of great importance, little knowing he had simply wrapped up the medicine he takes daily. He believed you would recognize this handkerchief, hoping that seeing the object would stir your memory of him.' Hearing this, Ziyu's heart gave a violent lurch. He stared at the silk handkerchief, a dreadful thought forming: 'From the way she speaks, has something terrible happened to Yunong? It sounds like an ill omen.' With this grim realization, a surge of bitter sorrow rose from his heart, flooded his senses, and gathered at his eyes and nose until it became an unstoppable torrent of tears. The three onlookers sighed in unison. Witnessing their reaction, Ziyu dared not ask further, feeling as though the tragic truth was already clear. He wiped his face with the handkerchief, thinking, 'These must be the medicines he took on his deathbed, sent for me to see.' A sensation of ten thousand arrows piercing his heart overwhelmed him. At a loss for what to do, he stood up and moved to the outer room, desperate to wail aloud. Yet, finding it inappropriate to do so in Sulan's home, he merely covered his face, sobbing in a daze. Sulan, seeing his extreme distress, regretted her rashness. Realizing her words had made it sound as though Yunong were dead, she hurried out to Ziyu and explained, 'Please, don't be so anxious. Let me finish. Yunong is fine. Come sit inside, and I will explain everything in detail.' Ziyu, hearing this, grew frantic. 'Xiangwan, if you have something to say, say it directly! Stop speaking in half-truths and terrifying people. What exactly has happened to Yunong?' He stepped back into the inner room. Lanbao and Qiguan looked at him with deep sympathy. Sulan began, 'Listen carefully to what transpired this fifth month.' She then recounted the entire ordeal: how Wei Pincai had schemed, how Xi Eleven had caused a chaotic scene, how she and Lanbao had tried to intervene and devise a plan, how Qinyan had hidden from Xi Eleven, and finally, how he was sent into the Hua mansion. She detailed his tearful parting instructions, his refusal of the purchase price, and the possibility of him requesting leave to come out. She went over every detail meticulously, offering comforting words throughout.",`Hearing that Qinyan still remained in the realm of the living, Ziyu's heart eased a fraction. He paused, then said, 'With Yunong gone there, it is as though he has vanished from the mortal dust.' Fresh tears cascaded down his cheeks. After a long moment of stunned silence, he examined the medicinal herbs once more. Recognizing only the peony root, he turned to Sulan, 'Yunong sending these herbs must carry a profound, hidden meaning. But I do not know what they are. Could you have someone take them to the apothecary to inquire, and write their names on the packets?' Sulan agreed, 'A sensible idea,' and was about to call for a servant. Qiguan interrupted, 'No need. The servant who accompanied me will recognize them; he used to work in an apothecary.' Qiguan summoned the man and showed him the four herbs. The man inspected them and stated, 'This is morning-glory (Qianniu), this is angelica (Duhuo), this is peony (Shaoyao), and this is stephania (Fangji).' Qiguan took a brush and wrote the names down, but could not decipher the riddle. Sulan offered, 'He has been separated from you, so he is now "living alone" (Duhuo). I understand that one.' Lanbao added, 'Fangji means to "guard oneself," telling you to rest assured. But I truly cannot fathom the other two.' Ziyu, tears shimmering in his eyes, said softly, 'Yunong's entire heart is revealed here. This peony, Shaoyao, is also known as Jiangli, meaning "about to part." It signifies that by entering the Hua mansion, he is already parted from me. Since we are parted, he is naturally "living alone" (Duhuo). Living alone in the Hua mansion, how can he simply drift along with the crowd? Naturally, he must be extremely cautious, constantly on guard, protecting himself (Fangji), keeping his body as pure as jade. As for the morning-glory, Qianniu, there can be no other interpretation. It must mean he will return on the Seventh day of the Seventh Month to meet me. It represents the reunion of the Weaver Girl and the Cowherd (Qianniu).' Sulan exclaimed, 'Exactly! Brilliant! You have guessed it flawlessly; that is precisely the meaning. Yunong's mind works differently than others. If he were to write you a letter, he would fear it falling into the wrong hands. Moreover, such endless sorrow is difficult to put to paper; this hidden message is far better. When the Seventh Eve arrives, you must spend the day here with us. We will go in and recount your deep devotion today; your bitter longing will not have been in vain.' With these earnest reassurances, the three managed to comfort Ziyu, and he forced himself to endure. This was Qiguan's first time spending a prolonged period with Ziyu. Observing his elegant bearing, Qiguan was filled with profound admiration; it was no wonder Yunong held only him in his heart. Seeing such profound devotion, Qiguan thought it was like a beautiful bride perfectly matched with her general. It was only a pity their destiny was so thin, doomed to brief meetings and long partings. He truly believed the Heavens delighted in tormenting mortals.`,"To Ziyu they also recounted the vulgar and despicable behavior Wei Pincai had exhibited on the boat when they previously shared passage. The mere mention of Xi Eleven caused them to grit their teeth and curse his name. Sulan urged Ziyu to eat, but his spirits were too low. Desiring an early departure, he took his leave and headed straight home. Already in the carriage, he began to feel an unnatural unease creeping into his limbs.","Upon returning home, he paid his respects to Lady Yan and then retired to his study. He lay down, muttering to himself, alternating between heavy sighs and sudden tears, as though intoxicated. By the following day, a severe illness had taken hold. His mind was in turmoil, his speech disjointed. Throughout the day, he broke down weeping multiple times. Initially, he could suppress his tears when others were present, but gradually, he lost all control. Even in the presence of his mother, tears streamed down his face, his expression vacant and his spirit crushed. Lady Yan, believing he had been afflicted by some evil spirit, summoned physicians to treat him. She even resorted to drawing lots, consulting diviners, making vows, and praying to the gods. For over ten days, there was not a sliver of improvement. During a single day, he would alternate between stupor and clarity. In his stupor, he was entirely oblivious, seemingly deaf and blind to his surroundings. When clear-headed, he appeared as healthy as anyone else. In his sleep, he muttered incessantly—sometimes calling for 'Yunong,' sometimes 'Xiangwan,' and at times, vehemently cursing Xi Eleven and Wei Pincai. Lady Yan was frantic with worry. Yan Zhongqing and Wang Xun visited every few days; they understood the root of the malady but were powerless to cure it. After discussing the matter, they felt they could not reveal the truth to Lady Yan, offering only gentle, evasive comforts. Hearing Ziyu constantly cry out 'Yunong' in his sleep, Lady Yan grew suspicious that he had become entangled in some illicit affair outside. Assuming 'Yunong' was a woman's name, she suspected a scandalous secret. Knowing Ziyu rarely ventured out, and never without Yun, she summoned the servant one day for a rigorous interrogation. 'Where exactly have you been taking the young master?' she demanded. 'Who is this Yunong? A courtesan, or someone else?' Yun initially denied everything, insisting, 'The young master only goes to the Garden, or to Young Master Wang’s and Young Master Shi’s residences. He has never seen a woman. If this lowly one lies, may I not live to see tomorrow.' Lady Yan thought, 'Gentle questions will yield nothing; he won't confess.' She ordered the servants to bring the heavy bamboo rods. 'Beat the truth out of him!' she commanded. Seeing the rods, Yun fell to his knees and kowtowed frantically. 'There truly is a young dan named Qinyan! The young master often seeks him out. When they meet, they cry more than they laugh. During the fifth month, there was a day they claimed to be visiting Lord Xu at the Yiyuan Garden, but it was a lie. The young master went with that young dan and another young dan to boat on the canal east of the gate for half a day, and they cried for half a day. This lowly one was at the bow of the boat and heard nothing else. The other day, the young master visited that young dan’s home. The other young dan told him Qinyan had entered Young Master Hua's mansion, and handed the young master a paper bundle—this lowly one does not know what was inside. The young master wept right there. They tried to comfort him, but ever since returning, he has been like this. Not a single word is false, and nothing else has happened!' Hearing this, Lady Yan burned with anger and scolded Yun fiercely, 'You deserve a severe thrashing! Why didn't you tell me sooner, waiting until the rods were brought out to speak? If not for your honesty today, I would have had you beaten to death!' She dismissed Yun, her heart filling with resentment toward her son. He was young and reckless; to squander his health and fall so desperately ill over a mere young dan! She wanted to lecture him harshly, but seeing him so profoundly sick, and realizing she was past forty with only this one son, she held back. If she lectured him and worsened his condition, what good would it do? It was better to wait until he recovered. Pondering the situation from every angle, she summoned Li Yuanmao to uncover the full truth.","Yuanmao said, 'This student has never gone out with him. Whether there is a Qinyan or not, I truly do not know. Last year, I heard old Wei Pincai often praising that Qinyan, and my uncle took notice. On the sixth day of the New Year, at the guild gathering, my uncle saw his performance and heard the praises. After that, this student has witnessed nothing. You must ask Wei Pincai for the detailed truth.' Lady Yan immediately ordered the gatekeeper, Xu Shun, to go to the Hua mansion and invite Secretary Wei over, stating there was urgent business to discuss. Pincai had no inkling it concerned this matter. He had grown distant from Ziyu recently and hadn't visited for over a month. Hearing Lady Yan's summons, he assumed some favorable opportunity awaited him. The next day, dressed in splendid attire, he rode out and arrived at the Mei residence. Upon meeting Lady Yan, he noticed the careworn expression on her face. He first offered standard pleasantries, asking for news from Jiangxi and if any letters had arrived, then inquired about Ziyu, confidently predicting he would secure top rank in the upcoming exams.",`After exchanging pleasantries, Lady Yan cut to the chase. 'Ziyu has contracted a strange illness,' she said, describing the symptoms and repeating the testimonies of Yun and Yuanmao. She pressed him, 'What exactly is the bond between my son and this Qinyan?' Pincai felt a sudden wave of unease; his conscience pricked, and a guilty flush colored his face. He hesitated before answering, 'Last year, when this nephew entered the capital, I shared a boat with a theatrical troupe. Among them was a young dan named Qinyan. On the day of the guild gathering this year, I happened to see his performance. As for how elder brother came to know him later, I heard they met during the lantern riddles at the Garden, and elder brother gifted him a qin. This nephew is a coarse man; I cannot mingle with such refined literati. How close they became, or what bond they share, this nephew truly does not know. Elder brother’s usual circle of young lords—madame is acquainted with them—they must know the depth of this feeling. Why not ask them?' Lady Yan demanded, 'Where is Qinyan now?' Pincai replied, 'Qinyan was studying new plays at the Garden, and Young Master Hua favored him—' He half-rose from his seat. 'This nephew owes you and the lord deep kindness. Knowing elder brother's illness stemmed from that actor—wasting his studies and ruining his health—I urged Young Master Hua to purchase him. Unexpectedly, a Mr. Xi caused trouble, wanting to ruin them. Qinyan’s teacher was terrified and at a loss. I persuaded him, and a few days ago, Qinyan was sent into the Hua mansion. Once inside, he will not easily come out. This nephew rejoiced inwardly, thinking elder brother might finally close off that part of his heart and turn to proper pursuits. Otherwise, that actor would drag him down forever.' Hearing this, Lady Yan’s anger surged. She hated her son for his foolishness, but she felt oddly grateful to Pincai. She thanked him and said, 'Your brother is now gravely ill. In his dreams, he raves nonsense, crying and laughing, his mouth constantly calling for Yunong. It is surely because that actor entered the Hua mansion. Your brother may be worthless, but he is my only son. If any harm comes to him, how will I face his father when he returns? You are a capable man. You must think of a way to cure him.' Pincai shook his head, 'It is very difficult. The proverb says, "Heart's disease requires heart's medicine." This nephew knows your strict household rules. Would the lord father ever allow him to roam outside causing such scandals?' Lady Yan retorted, 'I am not suggesting we indulge him in chasing young dans. Even if I dote on him, I would never go that far. I heard Yun say that when he met the young dan, they only wept. Children's whims are hard to decipher; perhaps there is some fate between them. Now he dreams only of that actor. Since you reside in the Hua mansion, think of a way to bring that actor out to comfort him. Perhaps he will heal faster.' As she spoke, tears spilled from her eyes. Pincai knitted his brow and sighed, 'But this man is utterly unreasonable and devoid of human feeling. Moreover, his wage is two hundred taels a month. How can I possibly summon him out?'`,"Lady Yan asked, 'Why two hundred taels a month? Is this person considered some sort of living treasure? And since they demand so much money, and your brother has none, how did he even come to know him?'","Pincai replied, 'Qinyan originally never wanted money, but his teacher absolutely refuses to budge without silver. This nephew has thought long and hard, but there is no easy path. We must negotiate with the teacher, and the teacher will not accept empty talk.' Lady Yan pressed, 'What kind of man is his teacher?' Pincai explained, 'He is exceedingly difficult, a man who lives inside the eye of a coin. To negotiate with him, unless you promise a substantial sum, it is uncertain if he will agree. He fears offending the Hua mansion; if he loses his two thousand four hundred taels a year, it would be disastrous. In my view, relying on mere words with this creature is utterly hopeless.'","Hearing that money must be spent, Lady Yan declared, 'Just bring him here. I do not mind spending the money, but how much will it require?' Pincai replied, 'This nephew will go and negotiate. I will spare no effort. Please set your mind at ease, Madame.'","With that, he rose to take his leave. Through her tears, Lady Yan said, 'I am causing you much trouble, nephew. I will say no more for now. Since you are willing, please go today. If possible, a reply today would be best.' Pincai agreed. Before leaving, he looked in on Ziyu. Even in his severe illness, Ziyu had not forgotten past grievances; upon seeing Pincai, he shut his eyes, feigning sleep, and completely ignored him.","Pincai exchanged a few brief words with Yuanmao before departing. He went straight back to the Hua mansion, sat in his room, and pondered deeply, yet no plan came to mind. He sought out Shanzhi, repeated Lady Yan's desperate plea, added his own embellishments, and pleaded, 'This brother and I are bound by three generations of friendship. Madame Mei has only this one son, a clever and handsome youth. Please, do this good deed and help cure him. I will owe you an endless debt of gratitude.' Shanzhi replied, 'What can I do? I can only report to the Young Master, say that you asked, and see if he will allow a visit.' Pincai waved his hands frantically. 'You cannot! We both know the Young Master's temper. If you ask like that, he will not only refuse, but it will look ugly for everyone. We need another plan.' Shanzhi shrugged, 'You make the plan, then. I don't meddle in such affairs.' Pincai bowed deeply. 'Good third brother, good friend. If you secure this, my brother will send you two bolts of fine, patterned treasury gauze.' Pressed until he could no longer resist, and recalling his own jealousy, Shanzhi finally said, 'Fine, let him go tomorrow. If anyone asks, I will handle it and keep your name out of it.' Pincai beamed, bowed in gratitude, and early the next day, took a carriage to see Lady Yan. He played the part of the beleaguered negotiator, claiming, 'His teacher finally agreed, but demands two hundred taels of silver just to call him out. He also fears that if the boy is barred from re-entering the mansion, it will be a disaster, and he might demand the full two thousand four hundred taels. From what this nephew sees, the man is incredibly slippery and detestable. I scolded him harshly, which frightened him a bit. He said whether the boy can go back in or not is besides the point, but the two hundred taels now is non-negotiable, otherwise he won't take the risk.' Lady Yan, pained for her son, had no choice but to agree. 'Let him come tomorrow, then. We will give him the two hundred taels, and deal with the rest later.' Pincai, seeing his trap sprung, was exceedingly pleased. He took his leave, went straight to the silk shop, purchased two bolts of fine gauze, and delivered them to Shanzhi the following day.","Why did Shanzhi dare to orchestrate such a scheme? Qinyan had only been in the mansion for half a month, yet even Lady Hua doted on him, frequently bestowing gifts and praising him: 'This child is honest; he doesn't act like a theatrical performer at all.' This favoritism stoked the fires of Shanzhi's jealousy. He reasoned, 'I have been here for over three years, holding the first rank. He's been here a matter of days and already shows such a clever head. Won't he eventually crush me?' He fully intended to squeeze Qinyan out. Hearing Pincai's request presented the perfect opportunity. He went to the Quiet-Green Retreat and informed Qinyan of the plan. Upon hearing the news, Qinyan’s eyes filled with tears. 'How could Gengxiang fall so gravely ill? Brother Lin, can you truly send me out? Does his family really want me to visit him?'","Shanzhi replied, 'Why would I lie to you for no reason? Rest easy. The Young Master won't descend from his quarters for half the day. Even if he calls for you, I will simply say you took leave to visit your sick teacher and will return shortly. If there's any trouble, I'll answer for it. Just make sure you go and return quickly. Furthermore, you must cure him this time. A second visit will be incredibly difficult to arrange.' Qinyan flushed and remained silent, but his heart swelled with profound gratitude toward Shanzhi. He felt he relied entirely on Shanzhi's care in this mansion, and to be granted the chance to see Gengxiang was a debt he must never forget. In the glow of this favor, even his deep-seated hatred for Pincai faded, acknowledging that this opportunity was also owed to him.","The day passed without incident. Finally, dawn broke, and heaven itself seemed to smile upon their endeavor. The Young Master felt unwell and remained in Lady Hua’s quarters, not emerging. Tremendously relieved, Qinyan quickly ate his meal and sought out Shanzhi. 'How do I get out? I don't know the way.' Shanzhi instructed, 'Go with Secretary Wei. The gatekeepers won't ask questions, and even if they do, the rumors won't pierce the inner walls.' Qinyan swallowed his pride and found Pincai. Pincai was inwardly delighted but maintained a cold exterior. 'Go on, but be careful. If this is exposed, do not say I accompanied you.' Qinyan agreed. They passed through the gates, layer by layer. The guards recognized Wei and dared not question them.","Once outside the gate, Qinyan was seated in the carriage, the curtains drawn tight. Pincai stood on the footboard, with the servant Four riding on the tail. Before long, they arrived at the Mei residence. Pincai did not wait for an announcement; he led Qinyan straight toward the study. The gatekeeper, Xu Shun, was startled but could not block their path, so he followed them in. Lady Yan was waiting anxiously. Seeing Xu Shun enter as if to make a report, she asked, 'What is the matter?' Xu Shun replied, 'Master Wei brought someone who looks like an actor. This lowly one dared not fail to report it.' Lady Yan said, 'I know. Bring them in quickly.' As Pincai entered with a youth of about fifteen or sixteen, Lady Yan took one look and was profoundly startled. She felt she had seen him before, suddenly realizing he bore a striking resemblance to her un-wedded daughter-in-law, Qionggu. She marveled inwardly, 'I have watched many operas and seen countless actors painted like women, but I never studied them closely offstage. But this Qinyan—jade bones and ice flesh, radiating a noble and resplendent air. If his hair were dressed, he would possess the bearing of a grand lady. Even bare-faced, he looks like a refined scholar from a noble house, bearing a remarkable resemblance to my own son.' Her initial anger melted away, the harsh words she had prepared vanishing, replaced by an involuntary, gentle smile.",`As Qinyan stepped over the threshold, his mind was solely on Ziyu's grave illness. Driven by a love he could scarcely endure, he had ignored the risks of fortune and disaster, steeling himself for Lady Yan's harsh scolding. Pincai’s threatening words in the carriage had left him timid and trembling. He approached, kowtowed deeply to the Lady, rose, and stood with his head bowed. Lady Yan called him closer, studied him intently once more, and bid Pincai sit. 'Where are you from? When did you arrive in the capital? How did you come to know my son? And how close is your bond? Tell me the complete truth, and I will not harm you.' Seeing her gentle expression, Qinyan relaxed slightly, though tears still shimmered in his eyes. He mumbled a few indistinct words. Sensing his fear, Lady Yan soothed him, 'Do not be afraid. This is my son's failing; he sought you out, not the other way around. Put your mind at ease. I swear I will not hurt you, but you must not evade the truth. Speak plainly.' Qinyan paused, then said, 'This humble one is from Suzhou. I arrived in the capital last winter with the Lianjin troupe. Both my parents are dead; my uncle's wife sold me. On the sixth day of the New Year, at the Suzhou guild, I saw the young master for the first time. Oddly, it felt as if we had known each other before. During the Lantern Festival, I went to Lord Xu's Yiyuan Garden to see the lanterns and the riddles they were making. I loved the riddle, "Fallen flowers, a man stands alone; fine rain, a pair of swallows fly." Second Master Xu offered a jade qin as the prize, saying he would introduce the winner to me. That day, it was the young master who guessed it. Two days later, there was a feast, and I was called to serve. That was our first true meeting. The second time was when Sulan invited us boating on the canal, where we spent half a day. It was only those two times. This is the absolute truth. If Madame does not believe me, please ask Secretary Wei. Besides, Madame surely knows of the young master’s outings.' Before he could finish, tears spilled uncontrollably down his cheeks. Pincai interjected, 'This is all true. They truly haven't even met three times.' Lady Yan listened, puzzled, yet moved to deep pity by Qinyan's demeanor. She thought, 'How could they have only met twice in half a year?' She asked, 'Your words are naturally true, but my son now dreams of you constantly. Surely you understand why.' Qinyan replied, 'With Madame's grace, how would this humble one dare to lie? It is truly strange. Not only did I feel I had seen the young master before, but upon seeing me, he also said it was as if we were already acquainted. The moment we met, it felt as though we were family, yet neither of us could explain the cause.' Lady Yan smiled, 'Hearing your words, it is indeed strange, and I cannot fathom it. But my son is now this ill because you entered the Hua mansion. I called you here to comfort and heal him. If you succeed, I will not blame you; instead, I will reward you.' Qinyan felt a deeper surge of grief, stifling a sob and murmuring an indistinct reply. Pitying him further, Lady Yan invited Pincai and Qinyan into Ziyu's room, while she and Pincai remained in the outer room to listen to what they would say and observe the scene. Xu Shun, having stood by and finally understood the source of the young master's illness, followed them into the bedroom to listen closely. As to how Qinyan cured Ziyu's sickness, listen to the explanation in the next installment.`]},f=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations28:r},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),h={29:[`When Qinyan finally arrived at the Mei residence, his heart trembled with profound dread; he was entirely prepared to face a torrent of humiliation and abuse. Yet, to his astonishment, upon meeting Lady Yan, he was greeted not with scolding, but with a deep, compassionate pity. She gently ordered him to go and comfort Ziyu—a kindness he had never dared to anticipate. A surging tide of joy and sorrow overwhelmed him. Unsure of Ziyu's true condition or how he might possibly offer solace, Qinyan simply obeyed her command. Bracing himself, he stepped into Ziyu's bedchamber. The room was cast in shadow: the curtains hung unrolled, a thick layer of dust coated the desk, the medicinal tripod sent up thick tendrils of smoke, and the incense ashes had long since grown cold. There sat a small nanmu wood bed, veiled in sheer white gauze. Yun'er was the first to lift the bed-curtain, calling out softly, "Young Master! Qinyan has come to see you." Ziyu was caught in the twilight of sleep, murmuring two vague sounds that were neither fully an answer nor entirely silence. Qinyan approached the bedside and sat upon the edge, lifting his eyes to look carefully at his friend. Ziyu's face was sallow and shockingly thin—wasted away by illness. Qinyan leaned close to the pillow and called his name in a low, trembling voice. In an instant, his tears burst forth like a spring, drenching Ziyu's pallid face. Suddenly, Ziyu gave a soft, ethereal laugh and whispered, "'On the seventh day of the seventh month, in the Palace of Long Life—at midnight when no one hears whispering'—this is the very hour." He laughed softly twice more. Realizing that Ziyu was lost in delirium, Qinyan's heart ached terribly. He patted him gently. Knowing that Lady Yan was just outside, he did not dare call him by his intimate name, Gengxiang, but instead changed his address to "Young Master!" Yet Ziyu remained trapped in his dream. He believed it was the night of the Seventh Eve, that he was already meeting Qinyan at Sulan's house, the three of them sitting together in the courtyard sharing fruit, tea, and intimate conversation. It was this dream that brought the lines of the *Song of Everlasting Sorrow* to his lips. His dreaming soul was plunged so deep that it could not easily be awakened. Qinyan watched him laugh again and heard him murmur, "I thought that 'the Yellow Springs and the azure heavens—both were too hard to seek.'" With that, Ziyu weakly slapped the bed and turned inward, falling back into his slumber. Qinyan's tears flowed faster, unable to do anything but stare. He dared not call out again. He watched as Ziyu shook his head slightly, whispering to himself, "Such heavy rain... if tomorrow morning is like this too, what then? The boat is so far away."`,`After a brief pause, Ziyu murmured, "Beneath the thoroughwort and stephania, I ought to add one more dose of angelica root." Outside the door, Lady Yan heard his words and knew he was completely consumed by delirium. Though she could not parse the meaning of everything he said, a profound grief washed over her, and two streams of tears traced down her cheeks. She could only stare blankly at Pincai. Pincai, too, felt a stark bleakness in his heart and gently urged, "Yuyi, wake him." Qinyan called out twice more, "Young Master!" Ziyu merely tittered and replied, "How foolish you are!" He then chided, "Yun'er, why keep calling? The road is so short, what is there to fear? Do you still think we are outside the Great East Gate?" Qinyan desperately wanted to shout to him, but his throat was tightly strangled by emotion; he could only pat his shoulder in silence. Suddenly, Ziyu opened his eyes, fixing his gaze toward Qinyan. "Xiangwan," he said warmly, "once again you have saved me. With such devotion, my heart can finally rest easy." Qinyan leaned closer, resting a gentle hand on his shoulder. "Young Master, Qinyan is here. Are you feeling better?" But Ziyu's mind remained clouded, his vision swimming in a blur of disorienting colors. Unable to see clearly, he let out a cold sneer. When Qinyan repeated his question, Ziyu burst into loud laughter. "You've already tested me once! Do you think I don't recognize you?" In the adjacent room, Lady Yan heard this exchange and felt her heart breaking. Unable to bear it any longer, she came to the doorway, where she saw Qinyan sitting on the bed, holding Ziyu's hand and weeping quietly, while Ziyu simply continued to laugh.`,`Lady Yan sighed softly, "He cannot even recognize anyone. What can we possibly do?" Hearing this, Pincai felt compelled to step up to the bedside. He called out several times, "Dear brother! The Qinyan you have been longing for has come especially to see you. Let me help you sit up; once you talk, you will surely feel better." He then instructed Yun'er to wring out a hot towel. After washing Ziyu's face and gently wiping his eyes, Pincai carefully raised him into a sitting position, folding the brocade quilt to prop him up comfortably from behind.`,"Lady Yan chose not to enter the room, fearing that her sudden presence might cause her son shame, while Wei Pincai respectfully kept his distance.",`After sitting up, Ziyu felt a measure of clarity return to his mind. His vision suddenly sharpened, and noticing Qinyan sitting beside him, he asked, "Who are you, sitting here?" Qinyan wept openly, replying, "How can you not know me?" Seeing that the windows were closed and that Qinyan was sitting with his back to the dim light, Ziyu naturally struggled to see his face clearly. Qinyan shifted outward, turning in half-profile so the light might catch his features. "I am Yuyi. Madame sent me. I never thought that in just a dozen days, you would waste away to such a state." As he spoke, he broke down into whimpers once more. Hearing his voice so clearly, Ziyu's heart gave a violent leap. He straightened himself and stared intently. "You are Yuyi? I do not believe it. How could you possibly come here? Is this yet another dream?" Qinyan fought back his tears and said, "I am Qinyan. Madame sent me. Why have you let yourself become so terribly ill?" Ziyu let out a cold sneer, murmuring, "You do resemble Yuyi somewhat."`,`Hearing this exchange, Lady Yan turned to Pincai and whispered, "Those words sound exceedingly strange." She then heard Qinyan say softly, "I came because of your sickness." Ziyu laughed dismissively. "If you truly were Yuyi, how could you come here? Even if you were willing, would they ever release you?" Qinyan replied earnestly, "I truly am Yuyi. I have been here for a long time. By Madame's own order, Secretary Wei brought me here. I beg you to ease your mind and stop this endless brooding. Your health is what truly matters; you must heal quickly. Now that I have taken this first step outside, I can come to visit you often." More tears cascaded down his cheeks. As Lady Yan saw Ziyu's mind growing clearer, a wave of relief washed over her. She ordered a maid to place a chair just outside the curtain, and Pincai stood quietly behind her. Now much more lucid, Ziyu leaned toward Qinyan, studying his face intently before laughing in disbelief. "Yuyi... you truly came? It isn't false?" Qinyan turned to answer, but his throat choked with emotion, and he could only weep. From outside the curtain, Pincai whispered, "Yuyi, steel yourself. Do not draw more tears from him." Qinyan hid his face behind a handkerchief and forced out a single, trembling sentence: "It is true." Ziyu pressed, "Truly true?" Qinyan nodded firmly, "True. True. True." At that, Ziyu let out a wild peal of laughter. Lunging forward, he collapsed onto Qinyan's shoulder, giggling ceaselessly with boundless joy.`,"Pincai could not help but laugh along with him, and the maids and matrons in the room all joined in the mirth. Lady Yan nodded, a deep sigh escaping her lips. She watched as Ziyu, with both hands firmly grasping Qinyan's shoulders, tried to pull himself up, laughing the entire time.",`Qinyan spoke softly, "What kind of illness is this? I beg you, do not be sick anymore. From today on, you must heal. Spare those who suffer for your sake, and end Madame's relentless worry." Fresh tears welled in his eyes. Ziyu laughed effortlessly, "What illness? My illness comes when it pleases and goes when it pleases. It has never truly mattered." Qinyan rebuked him gently, "Do not say it has never mattered. This is not like the times before; your face is utterly consumed by sickness. A thousand words can be distilled into one plea: ease your heart. You always used to say that you could see through the illusions of the world—why is it that today, you cannot?" Ziyu laughed again, "When did I ever fail to see through it? If I didn't, I would be the very opposite of thoroughwort. It is lucky that I do see through it; otherwise, there would be no today." He laughed once more. Qinyan continued, "I am doing very well at the Hua estate. Young Master Hua is a very proper gentleman, and everyone, high and low, treats me kindly. You need not fret over me." Ziyu asked, searching his eyes, "Are you truly well?" Qinyan nodded, "Truly. You may ask Wei." Ziyu smiled warmly, "If it is good, then it is good. Why should I ask?" He laughed softly. Qinyan pressed on, "Only your swift recovery makes my life better. If you heal slowly, my suffering deepens. But if you are in perfect health, then I experience immortal joy." He ended with a forced, hopeful smile. Ziyu was overcome with immense joy, looking as though he might dance despite his frailty. Qinyan added, "There, they actually permit me to take leave—it is not like the strict bonds of my former teacher. I could never come before, but now that I have, I will visit often. If you are well, I can sit with you longer; if you are ill, I fear I will only tire you, and seeing you suffer will only make my heart drearier." Ziyu's eyes widened. "You truly can take leave?" Qinyan smiled gently, "Is today not a leave of absence?" Ziyu clapped his hands, laughing, "Wonderful! I thought that once you entered those gates, we would never meet again. I never dreamed you could come here. It is strange... it is wonderful! Oh, Heaven!" Seeing him clap and laugh so fiercely, Qinyan began to wonder if this exuberant joy was merely another symptom of his illness. His heart ached quietly, but he held his sadness inside.`,"Seeing Ziyu laughing so freely and happily, much of the heavy gloom that had settled over Lady Yan's heart finally lifted.",`She reasoned to herself that if he could laugh with such unburdened joy, his mind must be opening, and his illness would soon mend. Yet, noticing that Qinyan seemed perpetually wretched and sorrowful, she could not fathom the true cause of his despair. Ziyu laughed again and asked eagerly, "Inside the mansion... what exactly do you do?" Qinyan replied evenly, "Nothing at all. They keep me at the Quiet-Green Lodge. The mansion's grounds are like another world, completely separate from the Garden, and their strict rules cannot easily be slipped."`,`"There is no need for me to sing; we do essentially nothing. I merely serve as a quiet page boy in the study." Ziyu asked, "Did they know you were leaving today?" Qinyan answered carefully, "The Young Master spends most of his time in the upper halls. He has several studies, and after resting for days, he might visit one place, sit briefly, and then vanish again. Servants do not dare enter those rooms unbidden." Qinyan was usually a young man of unwavering truth, but today, solely to cure Ziyu, he wove a tapestry of gentle lies. He spoke of the Hua household as a place of joy and freedom, promising he could visit often, just to ease Ziyu's troubled heart. Hearing these reassurances, Ziyu's mind steadied, and a genuine, profound happiness washed over him. He laughed warmly. Qinyan continued, "Before, under my teacher, going out was exhausting. I never came because I dared not. But today, I saw Madame, and she showed me great favor. She ordered me to visit you frequently. Under her command, who could possibly block my way? I have free coming and going." Hearing this, Ziyu's brow knit slightly, a flicker of flustered anxiety passing over his face. He lowered his voice, "You have seen Madame? She didn't scold you? Who brought you in and permitted your entry?" Qinyan reassured him, "Secretary Wei brought me. I told Madame that I could cure your illness, and she rejoiced. She instructed me that if you heal, not only will she allow my entry and frequent visits, but when the Lord returns, they will discuss buying me so I can serve you forever. She asked if I was willing, and I said I was willing—that I only begged for her grace." Ziyu looked at him intently. "You have never lied to me. Are you spinning lies today just to coax me into health?" Qinyan stood firm. "If you do not believe me, I will fetch Madame right now, so we may speak face to face." He took a deliberate step backward, but Ziyu waved his hand urgently. "No, no—is every sentence true?" Qinyan asked softly, "When have I ever lied to you?" Ziyu nodded, relieved. "Never. You are never false." His spirit steadied completely, and an even greater joy bloomed within him, erupting into a resonant laughter that carried all the way to the outer room. Lady Yan smiled in response, and Pincai puffed up with pride, whispering, "Nephew sees that dear brother may heal completely today. The credit belongs to Qinyan's teacher. Though he vexed us endlessly, it was well worth it." Ziyu paid no heed to the murmurs outside; his attention was entirely focused inward. Slowly, deliberately, he swung his legs off the bed. With Qinyan's gentle support, he took two steps, his legs soft but willing. He laughed, "I am well! I had no real illness—just a summer muddle, a thick fog in my head. They mistook it for a grave sickness and fed me stifling medicine until I was nearly stifled to death!" He then called out loudly to Yun'er, "I am hungry! Bring me food."`,`Lady Yan quietly slipped out of the room, and Pincai quickly followed her. Lady Yan whispered in amazement, "Strange... truly strange. I simply cannot read those two little ones. But today, we truly owe everything to Qinyan. I will reward him heavily." Pincai grinned broadly, "It is rare indeed, Madame. You can see they are completely open, without a single shameful secret between them. Today, we owe him a great debt; otherwise, even if the divine physician Ye Tianshi were reborn, he could never have cured him so fast." Lady Yan smiled kindly, "Still, it is through your power, Nephew, that we were able to call him out. The credit is yours, and we are deeply grateful." Pincai beamed with pride. "I dare not claim it. This nephew owes your house a great favor and should serve you faithfully. Not just in this—even through fire and water, I would oblige." Lady Yan thanked him gracefully and ordered Yun'er to prepare two bowls of delicate lotus porridge. Inside, Qinyan kept Ziyu company. Seeing Qinyan and hearing his comforting words, all of Ziyu's gloom vanished entirely. He ate his bowl of porridge with a clear, revitalized spirit and said to Qinyan, "I am healed now. You should rest and leave early today so as not to stir up any gossip; it might make it hard for you to take leave later. I will heed every word you have spoken, but you must take care of yourself too. Yield to those above you; it is a far better life than singing. I thought we would never meet again, yet today came so swiftly, and all my worries are gone. My only shame is that I have never met Young Master Hua, or I might have been able to visit you myself. Did Wei go with you?" He asked where Pincai was. Qinyan replied, "He came in, called out to you, and helped you sit up, but you must have missed him. He is in the upper halls with Madame now." Ziyu said softly, "Let us forget past grudges and befriend him. Today, we truly owe him our thanks." Qinyan nodded gently. As the time for parting approached, it proved difficult, and Ziyu finally shed a few quiet tears. Qinyan urged him to remain strong, and Ziyu promised he would, insisting on seeing him to the door. Qinyan stopped him, saying, "You are only just well. Do not come out. I will go and see Madame now." Ziyu looked anxious and whispered, "Speak carefully to her. Do not tell her all the truth."`,`Qinyan agreed and stepped out, making his way to the upper hall. Lady Yan smiled warmly at his approach and said, "Today we owe you a great debt; you have cured him completely. You must not let him fall sick again." Qinyan flushed slightly, pausing before he spoke. "The Young Master is exceptionally bright; he sees through the world's troubles. After today, there will be no more illness." Lady Yan studied his face thoughtfully and asked, "When will you return?" Qinyan replied softly, "Whenever my leave permits, I will come, to ease Madame's heart." Lady Yan sighed gently. "You two little ones... I simply cannot guess your minds. Today, one of you was crying while the other laughed, with barely a word spoken between you. If anyone else had not witnessed it with their own eyes, they would surely gossip. They would say I spoil and indulge my son. Nephew, you saw it yourself—it was almost funny. How could I ever be harsh and let him simply waste away?" Pincai was about to speak, but Lady Yan turned her full attention back to Qinyan. "Only you truly understand him. We must rely on you to serve him completely." Qinyan murmured his assent, bowing low. In his heart, he thought: *I never imagined Madame could be so merciful. Others would never be so kind. Even if they loved their son, that love would never extend to someone like me.* A profound gratitude settled deep within his soul.`,`Seeing that much time had passed, Pincai knew he must return with Qinyan. Qinyan, growing anxious about his long absence, quickly bid his farewells. Lady Yan raised a hand. "Wait. I have words for you." She stepped into the inner room and called for Xu Shun, ordering him to weigh out two hundred taels of silver. "Give this to the young gentleman Wei," she instructed. Pincai had his servant, Four, hold the heavy silver. A servant then brought out an elegant bundle, and Lady Yan presented it to Qinyan. "Madame rewards you. Take this, and then you may offer your thanks." The bundle contained a delicate silver knife, a pair of large, exquisite purses, a pair of smaller ones, a fine handkerchief, a luxurious foreign watch, ten silver ingots cast in the shape of plum blossoms, and an elegant fan. Qinyan accepted the gifts and, alongside Pincai, bowed deeply in gratitude. Pincai offered a vague, polite thanks as they stepped outside. Lady Yan graciously escorted them to the corridor, offering final instructions. At the gate, Xu Shun stood grinning broadly. "Today's affair... whatever could be the cause? I simply cannot guess." He then asked which theater troupe Qinyan belonged to. Pincai answered dismissively, "He was once of the Lianjin Troupe, but he is no longer acting. He now has duties at the Hua mansion. You will come to know the cause soon enough." Xu Shun saw them out to their carriage. As they rode away, Qinyan thanked Pincai endlessly, while Pincai waved off the praise with false modesty, clearly exceedingly pleased with himself.`,`They entered the city just past the hour of Shen. At the mansion gate, Qinyan followed Pincai as the superintendent decisively struck the register to record their return—an upper book protocol that Qinyan knew all too well. Pincai had his servant Four carry the silver straight to his room to lock it safely in a chest, while he went to find Lin Shanzhi. When Shanzhi saw Qinyan, he remarked sharply, "You have been gone so long; your illness must surely have healed by now." Qinyan flushed with shame and asked timidly, "Did the Young Master summon me?" Shanzhi replied coldly, "He summoned you thrice, and when there was no return, his anger grew great. He even sent for your teacher." Shock drained the color from Qinyan's face, leaving him speechless and trembling. Pincai quickly interjected, "He cannot take such a scare! Do not frighten him." Shanzhi's expression turned grave. "Why would I scare him? At the hour of Wei, the Young Master emerged and found him missing. When he asked, I told him it was his teacher's birthday, and that Qinyan had gone home to offer felicitations. I said I had waited for your descent to ask for leave, but hearing you weren't coming down, and knowing he couldn't wait, he asked me to report it. The Young Master was furious. He said, 'If it is truly his teacher's birthday, fine. But if this is a lie to cover for some other outing, I will not spare him.' He immediately sent a man to your teacher's house to investigate. When that man returns, I will be fiercely scolded, and you will fare far worse. The gate book clearly shows an escort by Wei; Wei will certainly not escape unscathed either." Qinyan's heart hammered violently against his ribs, and his eyes grew red with impending tears. If the truth were found out, the scolding might be mild, but the crushing shame would be far worse. Pincai, half-believing the threat, stepped forward. "Third Brother, do not bluff. I am clean in this matter. You were the one who sent him; he will naturally ask you first." Shanzhi sneered, "Ask me? I will tell him straight exactly what you two plotted!" Qinyan broke down in tears, begging for mercy. Pincai laughed, snapping his fan open and bowing dramatically. "Good brother, cover for us! Do it for your brother's face, and I will owe you a great debt." Seeing their genuine panic, Shanzhi finally chuckled. "Ease your minds. It is true, I did not lie to him—but I already arranged everything with Zhu Gui. There will be no real investigation. The man will roam outside the gate and return to say that it truly was the teacher's birthday and that Qinyan will be back soon. But if you two won any grand prizes out there, you had better split the thanks with him." Qinyan hastily promised, "I will give him silver." Shanzhi turned to Pincai, "Erase the entry in the gate book, or it will surely be seen later." Pincai pleaded, "I beg the Third Master to work his magic; I cannot ask for such a thing." Shanzhi crossed his arms. "You get off far too cheap. Yesterday, you offered me two bolts of gauze. I disdained them; the colors were awful, so I am returning them." Pincai countered, "That gauze was the finest quality! If you wish, I can change the pattern." Shanzhi waved him off. "I have enough clothes. I have a dozen undyed sets sitting in my room that I want to give away. It is only a favor of a few taels anyway. But lately, I saw two things that I absolutely love, yet I cannot afford to buy them outright..."`,`Before he could even finish, Pincai pressed eagerly, "What things? I will fetch them for you immediately." Shanzhi smiled with satisfaction. "They belong to Sha Huizi. One is a flower basket woven entirely of gold wire—barely two liang in weight, but the craftsmanship is unimaginably fine. The other is his Shi Dabin Yixing teapot, set with a piece of perfect jade in the lid. My thumb ring doesn't even match its quality. I offered forty taels for the basket, and he agreed, but I offered twenty-four for the teapot, and he flatly refused. Bring them both to me tomorrow. Sha claims the teapot is a priceless treasure, a century-old Shi Dabin original. He says the spout never drips, and whether you brew it with or without leaves, just pouring boiling water into it yields a fine, pale green tea. I actually dumped out his leaves and tested it myself—it is entirely true. Isn't that brilliantly odd?"`,'Pincai replied confidently, "Old pots ripen beautifully with age. I will buy them both for you, and you may keep the gauze as an extra gift." Shanzhi laughed, his mood completely lifted. "I suppose I cannot refuse empty-handed, so I will accept them grudgingly." With that, he turned and returned to his room.',"Later, at the Quiet-Green Lodge, Shanzhi gently questioned Qinyan, and Qinyan outlined the true events of the day in quiet murmurs.",'Shanzhi listened, his brow furrowed in doubt. "Why would he fall gravely ill for nothing? Many people saw your play, yet not all of them fell ill. Is there some deeper bond between you, or perhaps something else entirely?" Qinyan burned with shame. Unable to answer, he felt like a bucket dropped into a deep well, swallowing his helpless anger in silence.',"As for Ziyu's grave illness—and Qinyan's dramatic excursion from the heavily guarded Hua mansion—Wei Pincai swelled with immense pride. He thought the entire affair striking and romantic, and he hawked the story loudly outside the mansion walls until the common folk took it up as fresh, scandalous news. Actors who harbored petty dislikes for Qinyan, or who simply lived to sow wicked tales, eagerly reshaped and embroidered the rumors, until the relationship between Ziyu and Qinyan sounded utterly unspeakable. One evening, Wang Wenhui ran into the actors Rongguan and Erxi at a lavish feast. There, he heard the yarn spun as vividly as living dragons. He had seen Ziyu's sickness with his own eyes and had thought it odd at the time, but he had never once guessed it was lovesickness. Listening to the wild tales now, his heart sank heavily, and dark thoughts clouded his mind:",`"The affairs of young men fiercely resist all restraint," Wang Wenhui reasoned with himself, "and one can only play along when at a feast. Besides, Ziyu is only eighteen—the very bud of youth just forming. Why would he let himself sink into such scandalous depths? Just last year, we joyfully betrothed our beloved daughter to him. The first and most fatal poison in any marriage is a wandering fancy; if this continues, the future peace and harmony of their household will never stay pure." Upon returning home, he immediately summoned his son, Wang Xun, and interrogated him. Xun answered vaguely, trying desperately to argue Ziyu's side, insisting that the situation was nowhere near as bad as the rumors claimed. But Wenhui's doubts remained deeply entrenched. Lady Lu happened to overhear the conversation. Though her daughter was not yet officially wed, the sharp sting of protective jealousy—a mother's bitter vinegar—already burned within her. She confronted Wenhui furiously: "Brother Yu's looks may be superb, and it is true that last year I readily agreed to the Sun family's match. But now you simply wish to wash your hands of it? How can this possibly end well? The betrothal is not even fully sealed, and already he is the subject of lewd jokes! If he keeps up this debauchery, how is Qionggu supposed to live a decent life? His father is far from home and cannot leash him, and his mother simply spoils him, indulging his every passing whim. He will inevitably turn out good-for-nothing. He is so young, yet he harbors a shameless love for a young *dan* actor! It is no wonder—with those old roués endlessly carousing outside, how could young men resist being led astray?"`,'"What earthly good are these young *dan*? Have they absolutely no shame?" In the heat of her fury, Lady Lu turned her righteous anger upon Wang Wenhui as well. He could only force a hollow smile, not daring to speak out of turn. Finally, he offered gently, "The matter is indeed strange, but I have looked into it carefully. Even Xun knows the truth of it—they have only ever met two or three times."',`"It is only a distant longing for each other—nothing more. They are both just young children; how could they possibly grasp the true weight of such things? Ease your heart. I will speak to Cousin Yan and advise her to keep a stricter gate, allowing no more frivolous outings. It will be fine. They shall marry this winter as planned. Once Qionggu crosses the threshold and takes her place in the household, perhaps her presence will naturally leash him." Lady Lu let out a bitter sneer. "Such ruinous habits spring directly from the heart. I have seen it time and again—you may leash the body, but you can never bind the heart. Thieves do not suddenly change their nature." Hearing himself so pointedly targeted by her wrath, Wenhui laughed a hollow, uncomfortable laugh and quickly escaped to the quiet refuge of his study. Left alone with her indignation, Lady Lu turned her endless railing upon her daughter, Qionggu. Qionggu remained perfectly silent, though a quiet impatience stirred within her. She worried that her future groom might truly be dissolute, reflecting sadly on the old proverb, "talented men often trip short." Yet, she also remembered her brothers constantly praising Ziyu's unwavering goodness and noble character. Perhaps, she hoped, it was only a fleeting, innocent lust for beauty. At that thought, a deep blush painted her cheeks as she sat pondering her fate. Seeing her daughter so conflicted, Lady Lu felt a sudden pang of pity. She abruptly ceased her scolding and quietly left the room.`,"To return to the Mei household—after Qinyan's visit, Ziyu's spirit was profoundly eased. He now knew for certain that his mother did not blame him, and realizing that she had personally commissioned Pincai to bring Qinyan to him made his heart rejoice endlessly. Free from his despair, he mended entirely in under ten days. Only when standing before his mother did a lingering shade of shame still color his features. But Lady Yan loved him with a fine, perceptive grace; knowing that he had committed no truly shameful deed, she never once spoke Qinyan's name aloud. Wang Wenhui came to visit several times, as did Lady Lu. Before Lady Yan could even speak plainly, Lady Lu would needle her with sharp, pointed barbs—speaking as if warning a close kin to keep careful watch and cease her endless indulgence. Yet, seeing Ziyu fully healed—so handsome, graceful, and carrying himself like a truly worthy son-in-law with an air of pure honesty—half of Lady Lu's bitter doubt finally melted away.","Days later, however, Ziyu found he still could not rest easily. He begged his mother for permission to go out, using the pretext of visiting Pincai, solely to see Qinyan once more. He met with Pincai and relentlessly begged Shanzhi, until they finally called Qinyan out to see him. They talked earnestly for more than an hour, and only then did Ziyu's heart feel truly at peace. But within the sprawling Hua mansion, there were countless watchful eyes and wagging tongues, each harboring its own secret agenda. After a few days, a treacherous servant carried the tale directly to Young Master Hua. The Young Master grew absolutely furious. He summoned Shanzhi at once. Shanzhi pled desperately on Qinyan's behalf, but was fiercely scolded for his efforts. As a direct consequence, Young Master Hua barred Qinyan from ever going out again. He confined him to the Crimson Wash Residence, placing him under the strict, unyielding watch of the Ten Pearls maids. Qinyan was entirely cut off from the outside world, trapped like a bird in a gilded cage. Though the Pearls were inherently kindly, Qinyan—so young and possessing an oddly pure nature—remained fiercely guarded even against them. Seeing this, the Young Master came to implicitly trust his honesty among the maids, treating him almost as though he were just another maid himself. He strictly forbade any outside dealings; even Lin Shanzhi was forbidden from reaching him unless explicitly summoned, to say nothing of anyone else. Qinyan could only endure the suffocating solitude of the long gate. By day, he chatted quietly with the Pearls, though his words were few and guarded. By night, he sat entirely alone beneath a solitary lamp, bitterly resenting the endless autumn wind and rain. As to what unfolded next—listen to the following chapter for the explanation."]},p=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations29:h},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),d={30:["Of Qinyan's return from Ziyu's, the Young Master Hua knew no particulars, yet blamed him for quitting the mansion on his own. Thinking it a first offense, he waived punishment for the moment and reassigned him to the inner chambers—a secluded place isolated from the outside. One day, Qinyan sat alone beside the crystal rockery, weeping as he gazed at clusters of balsam flowers. He reflected that men live in this world, and if they cannot establish a name or achieve anything, mingling only with superficial people, the years will easily slip away and they will decay like grass and wood. Take the balsam for example: it blossoms, everyone finds it lovely, so they pluck it to place in a vase or pin in their hair. In a day or half a day it withers. They call it loving the flower, but it actually destroys it. But if the balsam remains planted here, the flowers leaning against each other like siblings, they possess a natural charm; even when wind and rain batter them, it is but a temporary trial that hardly harms their roots.","But if you uproot them and rear them in golden basins and jade urns, they lose their very nature. Then there are those who artificially manipulate them: trimming branches, plucking leaves, binding them with ropes and threads, pulling the grass, and peeling the moss. This pleases the eyes of men but diminishes the flower's natural color. How is this any different from imprisoning and shackling a person, making him afraid to laugh or cry?","Thinking further, a man is even less fortunate than a flower. The flower blooms this year and will bloom again the next, but a man deteriorates year by year. Just like me, fallen into this dusty world, trampled upon at whim, enduring every kind of affectation and tasting all sorts of hardships and bitterness. When the day comes that people despise me, I will be like a fallen flower swept into the privy or the mud—soiled and adrift with nowhere to end up. Does this thought not ache and bite? As he pondered, tears fell. He then thought of Yuxiang: though his illness had mended, I once fed him lies. If he knew my current plight—that he cannot come and I cannot go—I fear his old illness might flare up again. And if he calls for me then, I'm afraid I won't be able to go. Pondering over this back and forth, he felt dreary all day long. As days dragged on, time flew by; in a blink, over a month had passed, and the sweet fragrance of cassia filled the courtyard.","Today was the twelfth of the eighth month. Young Master Hua remembered the play at Yiyuan on the twenty-first of the sixth month, when he had said he would invite Duxiang over once the autumn air cooled. Thinking that the fifteenth would be a family banquet and inconvenient for guests, he set the date for the fourteenth, inviting Ziyun, Cixian, Wenze, and others. He arranged several spots in the West Garden and prepared lantern plays. Because they lived outside the city, a daytime gathering would definitely not be enough for them to fully enjoy themselves, so he specified a night banquet in the invitation. Today happened to be the second session of the autumn examinations. Why didn't Liu Wenze participate? The chief examiner this time was Wenze's father, Grand Secretary Liu Shouzheng, and the associate examiner was Wang Wenhui, who had been promoted to Grand Secretariat. Lu Zongyuan, Yang Fangyou, Zhou Xijue, and Sun Lianggong—this group of notable figures all served as associate examiners, which meant more than half of the well-known scholars had to avoid the exams due to conflict of interest. Among them, except for Xu Ziyun and Shi Nanxiang, who were already provincial graduates from the previous exam, and Xiao Cixian, who considered himself above worldly affairs and had no intention of participating, only Tian Chunhang and Gao Pin entered the examination hall. Ziyu, Wang Xun, Wenze, and Zhongqing all withdrew according to the rules. Ziyu stayed at home, brooding unhappily. Moreover, because there was no news from Qinyan and they were completely cut off from each other, unable to exchange messages, he wanted to visit Pincai several times. However, deterred by the strictness of the Hua mansion and the arrogance of its servants, Ziyu refused to go and could only sit quietly and gloomily in his study.",`On the morning of the fourteenth, Ziyun discussed with Cixian: "Today Young Master Hua has invited us for an all-night feast and to view lanterns. There must be quite a lively scene today, and I heard five famous troupes will perform together." He then ordered his servants to assign attendants, check clothes and belongings, and prepare assorted treasures and tips. The steward got everything ready and had the family members double-check, to avoid missing anything at the last minute. By early afternoon, the two of them had already eaten their morning meal, dressed, and taken a carriage straight to the Hua mansion.\\n\\tMeanwhile, Young Master Hua had personally decorated various spots. The scenery in the West Garden was marvelous; though not as expansive as Yiyuan, its exquisite craftsmanship and splendor were quite comparable. The only drawback was its narrow footprint, being only a third the size of Yiyuan. The garden contained twelve pavilions. The West Flower Hall where Pincai had previously been was just the first entry point. A close friend of the late Lord Hua named Xie Lishan, an excellent painter, had spent twelve years designing it, achieving a perfect balance of light and shade, density and sparseness. As Young Master Hua grew up with a love for extravagance, he altered much of Lishan's original work. Now, it was entirely about dazzling embellishments, leaving no trace of rustic simplicity.`,"That day, Zhang Zhongyu arrived early. After having breakfast at Pincai's place, he spent half the day chatting and laughing with Zhang, Gu, and the others. Around noon, he pulled Pincai and Lin Shanzhi along to stroll through the West Garden. Zhongyu had only visited one or two spots before, and although Pincai had toured it twice, he hadn't seen everything either.","The beauty of this garden lay in its winding, layered pathways that connected the entire grounds. Covering twenty mu in a square shape, a pond named the Jade Belt River had been excavated. It meandered through six bends, with a bridge built at each bend, making six bridges in total. Along the pond were long corridors and winding pavilions, shielding and complementing each other from front to back, displaying beauty from every angle. Three or five small boats were moored at the shore. The bank along the pond was called the Little Su Causeway. There were many large trees in the garden, including twisting pines and tall bamboo. The artificial mountains came in two styles: the smaller ones were built from Taihu stones, intricately hollowed and exquisite; the larger ones were stacked with yellow stones, reaching several zhang high, covered in green moss and creeping vines, with pavilions leaning against them and flowers and trees setting them off. To summarize its layout: where there was water, there were mountains; below the mountains lay water; empty spaces were filled with houses, and the connecting areas were planted with trees. There were places where you couldn't see the sky when looking up, and places where you couldn't see the ground when looking down.","Zhongyu and Pincai, following Shanzhi, walked along the mountain paths. Following the high and low winding paths, they entered a fairy cave. Going up from the left side, past a few cassia trees and less than a dozen steps, they reached a curved corridor shaped like a concave 'U'. Light curtains and blinds half-concealed the interior. When Shanzhi led them in to look, there were seven rooms in total: the two side wings stretched out like wings, with three flat rooms in the middle, and large glass windows at the back, close to the edge of the pond. The furnishings inside were magnificent, and the place was named 'Returning Geese Islet'. Below were a few lines of postscript, written by Lord Hua himself. The two admired it for a while, then walked out through the small door at the west end of the right corridor. It was a small courtyard with a few spiritual rocks and banana trees. They saw a small building with an octagonal moon gate made of nanmu wood in an ice-plum pattern. Inside, a flight of zigzagging stairs led up. Once upstairs, there were carved windows on all four sides. Dozens of bookshelves were arranged inside, stacked and placed neatly, containing tens of thousands of scrolls, emitting a fragrant scent. There were two such buildings, forming a 'T' shape—one with three stories and the other with two, both with bright windows all around, and four large cabinets locked in the middle. Below, a long table was set, with a precious tripod emitting incense and vase flowers smiling brightly.",`The three of them sat for a moment and then came down the stairs behind the screen door. Following a stretch of red railings, a row of weeping willows was planted below, with a few parasol trees in front. The pavilion they entered was extremely elegant. Several jade lutes were hung on the walls, adorned with ancient, colorful brocades; the tables displayed ancient bronze tripods and vessels, completely devoid of any vulgarity. After admiring it for a while, they walked down again to an open area right by the pond. The room was spotless, with tables and chairs made of Xiang bamboo and lacquered with an unpolished finish. Two rows of tall bamboo stood on the sides, the autumn breeze rustling through them movingly. A chess table was placed in front of the railing, with two foreign-lacquer chess boxes. Zhongyu said, "This place is quite secluded; it really washes away all the extravagant atmosphere." Shanzhi replied, "Although the Young Master loves liveliness, he actually enjoys tranquility as well." Zhongyu walked down the steps, went along the pond, and crossed the red bridge. Opposite was a white stone terrace with jade-like carved railings. On it stood a flat pavilion with three rooms, with Xiang blinds hanging down. Upon entering, they felt a dazzling light piercing their eyes and a chill penetrating their skin; it was a summer retreat. All the tables, stools, and desks had glass surfaces. On both sides were mica folding screens, and in the middle was a large tank of clear water containing some large goldfish, with a crystal mountain over four feet high placed inside. Now, in the cool autumn, it felt ominously cold. As they walked out, they heard the distant sound of watchman's clappers. Shanzhi said, "This is the servants getting ready. The Young Master is about to come out and the guests are arriving. Lest I'm needed, I'll go out first." With that, he left. Zhongyu and Pincai also came out, returning to the East Garden to change clothes and wait.`,"As for Young Master Hua's banquet today, it was held in three places: during the day, a banquet and theatrical performance in the Enqing Hall.","Between five and seven in the evening, a variety show at Xiaoping Mountain in the West Garden. At night, a lantern play at the Liuqing Retreat. Young Master Hua had already come out fully dressed and was waiting for the guests in front of the Enqing Hall. Right then, Xiao, Xu, and Liu arrived together. They entered the main gate, got out of their carriages, changed into sedan chairs inside, and went straight through the festooned gate, alighting at the main hall. Young Master Hua came out to greet them and exchange pleasantries. He opened the middle gate, and the host and the three guests walked slowly inside, passing through two more courtyards before reaching the Enqing Hall. Since this was Xiao Cixian's first time there, he looked around carefully. The Enqing Hall was extremely magnificent, with towering roofs and carved walls, grandly and beautifully furnished with colorful decorations. The foundation of the hall was deep and spacious. In the middle, facing outwards, were railings on three sides. Colorful curtains hung above, and a plush carpet was laid below—this was the stage. The retiring rooms on both sides connected to the backstage. The host and guests exchanged greetings again. Just as they were about to sit, Wei Pincai and Zhang Zhongyu came out. They all greeted each other one by one, and then sat down according to age. After some small talk and three rounds of tea, two men in sixth-rank official attire led four people in to set the tables, arranging two banquets. The musicians backstage began to play, and soon silver plates, golden bowls, and fine wine were served. Young Master Hua stood up to seat the guests. Ziyun, Wenze, and the others politely declined, suggesting they combine into one table and use a round one instead, but Young Master Hua insisted. Consequently, Cixian took the seat of honor at the first table, with Wenze next to him. At the other table, Ziyun took the seat of honor, followed by Zhongyu, while Pincai and the host acted as companions.",`Today, the five great troupes were performing together. A man holding an ivory tablet came up to kowtow and ask them to select plays. Each person picked one, and they were performed in order. The opening acts were just the usual crowd-pleasers, which everyone knows, so we'll skip them. The host offered wine, and the four guests drank a few cups. Several dishes were served, featuring a spread of mountain and sea delicacies—it's impossible to describe all the rich, fresh, and savory flavors. Refined conversation and witty remarks were paired with humorous jokes. At the other table, Pincai asked Cixian about the sights of Yiyuan. Cixian briefly described a few places. Next came Baozhu, Huifang, Sulan, Shufang, Yulin, Lanbao, Guibao, Chunxi, Qiguan—nine in total—plus one more, performing the play 'Flower-Viewing Assembly on the Qinhuai'. Some were demure, some coquettish, some quiet and gentle, and some romantic, exhibiting the utmost alluring charm. Everyone applauded. Ziyun, Cixian, and the others gave rewards right in the middle of the performance. Young Master Hua smiled and said, "This is plagiarized from my older brother's old writings; it feels very familiar." Ziyun said, "They sang very well. The demure ones were extremely demure, and the wild ones were extremely wild; no two were alike." Wenze added, "I've never seen them perform this play before." Cixian said, "The Qinhuai River has declined nowadays. Even Ma Xianglan's looks back then were only average, and there were remarks about her unbound feet not matching." Ziyun replied, "I've seen a portrait of Xianglan; she definitely had literary grace and charm." Pincai and Zhongyu echoed their agreement and they discussed it for a while. Young Master Hua's mood for drinking rose, and he urged everyone to drink heartily. Ziyun noticed that Qinyan was nowhere to be seen today, so he asked, "I heard that Qinyan is currently at your esteemed residence. Why don't I see him today?" Young Master Hua replied, "This boy's temper is a bit odd, but he's honest. He's now assigned to the inner study and will come out shortly." Ziyun looked around again and didn't see Lin Shanzhi or the Eight-Age Troupe either. He wondered to himself, on such a grand occasion today, why are these people missing too? Could they all be backstage getting in makeup? After the play ended, Young Master Hua summoned the ten Dan actors to come up and offer wine. They all came up together—plump, slender, delicate, or voluptuous, each beautiful in their own way. Ziyun saw that besides the nine, there was an additional one: Quan Gui from the Quanfu Troupe, who was also very alluring and charming. After paying their respects, they all held golden cups and went to different people to offer wine.`,`When Huifang offered wine to Ziyun, Ziyun asked how Chunhang's examination essays went. Huifang replied, "The other day, Shi Zhujun said his essays were very good and he will definitely pass." Hearing this from the other table, Wenze laughed and said, "I heard you burn incense and pray every day at home. Even if Xiangfan's essays are poor, he will still pass." Huifang smiled, "Who said that? If passing exams could be achieved by praying, I'd be willing to pray for everyone." Young Master Hua asked, "What are you talking about?" Ziyun was about to answer, but Huifang hurriedly poured a cup of wine to urge Ziyun to drink, entangling him so he couldn't speak. Young Master Hua stared blankly at Huifang, waiting for Ziyun to explain. Seeing this, Wenze said, "Let me tell you." Huifang shot a glance at Wenze, while Zhang Zhongyu laughed, "Meixiang, there are so many people and mouths here today; even if you want to silence someone, you can't silence all of them." Huifang replied, "Why would I want to silence anyone? I have nothing to be afraid of; say whatever you like." He smiled and walked over to offer wine to Wenze. Over there, Young Master Hua rewarded Baozhu with a cup of wine, which Baozhu drank and expressed thanks for. Young Master Hua said, "The singing in today's play was excellent too; whether lightly powdered or heavily made up, each suited their role." Baozhu smiled but said nothing. Young Master Hua then asked about Huifang's situation. Baozhu smiled, "I don't know." Young Master Hua laughed, "You all protect each other; it's so detestable. I will definitely find out eventually." He then called Huifang over. Huifang had no choice but to comply. Young Master Hua said, "I'm impatient and can't stand confusing matters. Whatever hidden situation you have, why must you keep it from me?" Huifang lowered his head and said, "Young Master, don't listen to their nonsense; they are just making fun of me." Ziyun smiled and said, "Meixiang, everyone outside the city knows about your affairs. Even inside the city, I'm afraid some people know too. Why not tell the Young Master?"`,`Huifang said, "What do I have to say?" Zhongyu suddenly laughed, "When you were in a crisis, you used someone as a shield, and now you are being ungrateful." This puzzled everyone. Huifang paused for a moment, his face unwittingly flushing red. Young Master Hua saw this and, recalling what was said a few days ago, felt a bit of pity. He guessed there was some hidden matter that was hard to speak of, and decided he would slowly ask Duxiang later, so he changed the subject. Amidst the chatting and laughing, they drank a lot of wine, watched several plays, and had two courses of snacks. Young Master Hua stood up and said, "Let's take a stroll in the garden." Cixian and Ziyun agreed, "Very well, we've already had plenty of wine." The guests all stood up together. Four or five servants hurried through a shortcut under the corridor to inform the garden gatekeepers to get ready.`,"The East and West Gardens flanked the main hall, with doorways connecting them everywhere. Young Master Hua led the group through the covered walkways, bypassing several courtyards until they reached a winding corridor. There, inset into the wall, was a woodblock carving of Zhu Zhishan's cursive script, over six feet tall. Just as everyone was about to admire it, a servant came over and pushed it; it turned out to be a door. Ahead of them was a vista filled with green shade and the murmuring sound of water. They deferred to each other and entered the garden. Crossing a red bridge, they found a bluestone terrace bordered on three sides by short white stone railings, sheltered by a small green silk awning. To the left were rocks, and on the earthen slope grew dozens of cassia trees; to the right, a winding stream flowed, its banks overgrown with bamboo and trees, blocking the view.","Above sat a small pavilion with three rooms. Inside, a plaque read 'Deep Pool and Mountain Dwelling'; it was extremely tranquil and elegant.","Ziyun and the others were about to sit down, but Young Master Hua ushered them further inside. Walking behind a screen, they found a space that was narrow on the inside and wide on the outside, shaped like a fan on three sides. The beautiful windows were carved, with the centerpieces made of ebony, ivory, red sandalwood, and boxwood fashioned into exceedingly delicate patterns. The space was partitioned into five or six sections. The front had no curtains, only a green gauze enclosure. When everyone looked out from the front of the pavilion, there was a clear stream below, with two small painted boats moored there. Opposite was another water pavilion, but with heavy Xiang blinds hanging down. Young Master Hua ordered a long table to be set in front of the green gauze. Several burners of fine incense were lit in the room, and fragrant tea was served. As everyone sat down, they felt the autumn scenery was like a painting, cleansing their hearts and minds. When Ziyun casually looked back, he saw Shanzhi leading Qinyan up. They bowed to Ziyun and the others. Ziyun and the rest quickly greeted them.","Ziyun saw Qinyan, who was now lowering his eyebrows and bowing his head, lacking his former proud demeanor. Moreover, two months had passed; they used to see each other day and night, but now it was like meeting a stranger. Facing each other without words, Ziyun couldn't help but feel somewhat emotional. He called him closer, asked a few questions, and was about to ask about Ziyu, but held back. Cixian and Wenze also asked a few questions.","The group chatted leisurely for a long time. It was already around four in the afternoon. Young Master Hua ordered several fruit plates and snacks to be served, and they began to drink lightly. He also called the group of Dan actors in to attend to them. On the opposite water pavilion, a troupe of ten-variety acrobats began their performance, telling jokes, doing vocal mimicry, and performing an act from the 'Cases of the Dragon Diagram'. Cixian and the others didn't enjoy listening to it much, so they played guessing games and drinking games with the Dan actors, passing cups back and forth. Young Master Hua also called for a magic act, which they performed for a while. As the moonlight gradually appeared, the banquet was cleared, and they took a stroll in the garden. Soon, dozens of pairs of red lanterns came to lead the way. Young Master Hua and the guests all changed their clothes and, following the red lanterns, walked out of the garden and back towards the Enqing Hall. They saw bright lanterns glowing like a colorful sunset. Walking eastward from behind the screen, the sounds of sheng and song filled their ears everywhere, and the lantern lights were like a rainbow. They entered a moon gate, and in front of it a large archway made of multicolored silk had been erected, hung with hundreds of glass painted lanterns. In the center was a plaque made of glass, flanked by a long couplet. Entering the archway, under the moonlight, they saw eight tin branch-lamps set in the center of the courtyard, fashioned into various flowers. Lamps were lit in the heart of each flower, shooting out flames, looking truly like trees of fire and flowers of silver. Ahead was another lantern shed, and when they reached the stage, it was even brighter. Sweet songs drifted from both wings, and the fragrance of orchids and musk filled the air. Opposite was the Liuqing Retreat.","The guests were invited inside. They took their seats, the host arranged the places, and the opera resumed. The joy and uninhibited drinking that ensued are hard to describe. They drank until the second watch; both host and guests were a bit tipsy. The dishes were stopped, fruits were served, and they sat loosely for a while.",'Suddenly, a servant came up and reported a message from the gate: "Young Master Feng has arrived and wishes to come in."',`Young Master Hua was slightly taken aback and said, "Alright, invite him in; there are no strangers here anyway." Pincai asked, "Why is he arriving in the middle of the night?" Young Master Hua said, "He must have been locked inside the city with nowhere to stay." They waited for quite a while before hearing footsteps. Guided by two small horn lanterns, Feng Zipei hurried forward. He greeted Young Master Hua and the others, as they were all acquainted. Young Master Hua seated him above Pincai. Just as he was about to ask him a question, Zipei smiled and said, "Great! You have such a lively banquet, yet you didn't even invite me, forcing me to crash the party." Liu Wenze said, "I thought you were busy with social engagements. If I knew you were free, I would have brought you along this morning." Everyone laughed. Zipei ignored it, glanced at the actors, and then proposed a toast of two cups to the whole table before eating a few bites of food himself.`,`Seeing that he seemed hungry, Young Master Hua asked, "Where were you today? Why are you so late?" Zipei shook his head, "Don't mention it." He ate a piece of apple and continued, "It was a perfectly good gathering, but it ended in unhappiness." Having said this, he seemed reluctant to continue. Young Master Hua pressed, "Why did it end unhappily? Tell us." Zipei said, "Today, I went with my wife's uncle, Gui Zirong, to his wife's uncle Wu Dashan's house to celebrate his aunt's birthday." Zhongyu wanted to laugh when he heard this, and Ziyun said, "With Wu Dashan there, things naturally wouldn't go well." Wenze nodded, "This story is definitely worth hearing; hurry up and tell us." Zipei continued:`,`"Gui Zirong also invited his young father-in-law and brought along his four musicians. Dashan also invited two tables of guests, including some friends from the south." There were a few amateur actors among them, and everyone agreed: "Each person chips in ten diao of cash, making ninety diao in total, and we'll hire the Quanfu Troupe to perform." Gui Zirong was in high spirits and performed a scene from 'Exclusive Possession' with a man named Lü." Wenze interjected, "Gui Zirong is quite handsome himself; he just shouldn't keep his concubine separately outside the city. I heard he's still very strapped for cash." Zipei shot him a look, and Wenze stopped talking. Xiao Cixian gave a cold sneer. Pincai seemed like he wanted to say something but held back.`,`Zipei said, "They just love acting, but they insisted on dragging me into it." Young Master Hua said, "That's true, you are the best at singing; I think you're even better than them. What did you perform today?" Zipei replied, "Singing with anyone else would have been fine, but that idiot Dashan pestered me to death and insisted on singing 'Capturing Alive' with me. The name of the play is unlucky to begin with, and Dashan is both dull and clumsy; he was incredibly amateurish. I can't even describe it all, but the audience couldn't stop laughing. Just as we were making our entrance and I had wrapped the handkerchief around his head, a group of people suddenly barged in. Without any explanation, they flashed a warrant from the Board of Punishments to the crowd, slapped a chain on the idiot Dashan, and dragged him away."`,`"The women inside started wailing frantically. No one knew what was going on. Before anyone could go out to intervene, they had already sped off. Not knowing what to do, the guests scattered in an uproar. Zirong couldn't leave, and a few of Dashan's close friends stayed behind, so I came straight here." Everyone was amazed upon hearing this. Zhongyu said, "I've guessed about eighty percent of it. This matter actually stems from Gui Zirong. Wu Dashan was just an extra, caught in the crossfire, and ended up bearing the brunt of Gui Zirong's disaster." Feng Zipei asked, "I don't know about that. Do you know the reason?" Zhongyu replied, "It's just a guess. I heard someone say that Wu Dashan forged a property deed to borrow six hundred diao of cash for Gui Zirong. The creditor found out and wanted to sue him. I think this must be it." Feng Zipei said, "That sounds plausible. The money was split between Gui Zirong and Dashan, but now Wu Dashan is the only one in trouble." Liu Wenze said, "This Wu Dashan is truly bizarre. He's dumb and foolish, yet he's obsessed with opera. There are ten theaters outside the city, and he visits every single one of them every day, going to one theater at least four or five times. With his sloping shoulders, the most annoying thing is that he wears a pair of worn-out black boots, shuffling around aimlessly. If he has even a passing acquaintance with someone, he'll stand there leaning sideways. When people ignore him, he has no choice but to shuffle on. If I don't go to the theater, it's fine, but if I do, I'm bound to run into him." Cixian laughed, "He's also from our Zhejiang province. From his looks, it seems he's studied a bit." Zhang Zhongyu said, "Not necessarily. Although I don't understand literature much, the characters he writes are completely illegible." Young Master Hua said, "Stop talking about these people, who cares if he's foolish or not. Zipei, you know how to sing; why don't you get on stage and perform a piece to show off your skills? Besides, there are many connoisseurs here; if you impress them, it might be beneficial for you." Zipei spat, "I'm not an actor, who do I need to curry favor with?" Xu Ziyun said, "Who's treating you as an actor? Even if you go on stage for the love of music, it's an elegant amusement. Moreover, with your beautiful looks, not having people praise you would be a tragic waste." Everyone chimed in, which moved Feng Zipei a bit. He said, "I don't have a partner today, so I can't sing." Young Master Hua said, "Why not? Just sing with someone from the troupe." He pouted his lips and pointed, "Zhang Er and Wei Da are very skilled." Zhongyu shook his head, "I can't. Furthermore, I only know a few arias for older male roles; I wouldn't match him. Wei Da can do it; not only can he sing young male roles, but he can also handle second and third painted-face roles." Wei Pincai just smiled, neither volunteering nor refusing. Xu Ziyun said, "That settles it then; let's invite Brother Wei to try a piece with Zipei, they are evenly matched." Pincai said, "I'm just afraid our styles won't match, and I haven't consulted Zipei on how he does it." Young Master Hua said, "That doesn't matter. If there are discrepancies in the staging or the tunes, just coordinate beforehand. Besides, I have several Suzhou music teachers here; just have them accompany you." Pincai said, "In that case, we must have Zhou San on the flute and Qin Jiu on the clappers for it to be perfect." Young Master Hua immediately had them summoned to wait by the stage. Pincai then listed the plays he could sing, such as 'Plucking a Willow Branch', 'Exclusive Possession', 'Appreciating the Lotuses', 'The Small Banquet', 'Flirting with a Zither', and 'Stealing the Poem'. Zipei shook his head repeatedly; it turned out there were some he didn't know and some he knew but wasn't familiar with. He laughed and said, "I don't know any of those; it seems we can't sing."`,`Pincai asked, "What do you know?" Zipei replied, "I know 'Prior Enticement', 'Later Enticement', 'Counter-Deception', 'Lifting the Curtain', and 'Cutting Cloth'." Pincai laughed, "That's not right either; we simply can't perform." Behind Young Master Hua stood a few boys from the Eight-Age Troupe. One whispered to Lin Shanzhi, "Why doesn't Master Wei sing 'Capturing Alive'? Didn't we see him sing it the other day?" Young Master Hua had already heard this and said to Pincai, "Why don't you sing 'Capturing Alive' with him?" Pincai was still trying to hedge, but unable to withstand the unanimous urging from the crowd, he had to agree. Zipei laughed, "Sing it is, then, but let's hope it doesn't bring about a case with the Board of Punishments and have Wei Da locked up." Everyone laughed. Zipei felt quite pleased, deliberately delayed a bit more, and then went backstage with Pincai to dress up after the crowd pressed them again. Pincai was an expert in this and didn't suffer from stage fright at all. Without knowing how Feng Zipei would fare, they practiced the staging backstage first, and Feng Zipei managed to keep up well. Soon, the hand gongs clanged a few times, and Feng Zipei emerged. His performance was mournful and pitiful, his voice weeping, conveying the grace of light clouds following his steps and pale smoke brushing his sleeves. His delicate voice quavered, making the lantern lights seem dim in comparison.`,`Everyone was astonished, as if they didn't recognize him. Young Master Hua left his seat and walked to the front of the stage, and the guests all stood up to watch quietly. Young Master Hua said, "Strange! He actually looks like a fine woman; today he's going to outshine all the beauties." Hearing the praise, Zipei felt a bit bashful. He saw Huifang and Baozhu standing next to Xu Ziyun; noticing Huifang looking at him, he leaned over to exchange a few words with Ziyun, and then with Baozhu, seeing Baozhu smile. He also saw Liu Wenze and Xiao Cixian standing together, whispering in each other's ears, probably discussing him. In fact, Wenze and Huifang were not talking about Feng Zipei at all; they were talking about Gui Zirong.`,"This Gui Zirong was originally from Jiangxi but registered in Zhili, and had earned the title of Xiucai. In his youth, he was quite handsome. He was now twenty-seven or twenty-eight years old, and had a history of many shady dealings. His family was originally wealthy, his father having been a great merchant. In his youth, he had bought his way to becoming a Juren, and later purchased a position as a secretary in the Grand Secretariat, currently enjoying life in his hometown. Zirong had been in the capital for eight years without returning, squandering a vast amount of money. He already had a wife at home, but lied that he was a widower and married Wu Dashan's sister. Still unsatisfied, he took Ye Maolin's daughter as a concubine and lived separately in the southern part of the city. When the Ye girl was at home, she was not one to abide by the rules and loved to socialize, but Zirong doted on her obsessively. Her dowry was quite substantial, but Zirong squandered it recklessly, and within two years it was completely gone.","Neither of the couple could endure poverty, which inevitably led to quarrels and mutual recriminations. The Ye girl then began to flaunt herself at the door, flirting and selling her charms, and those old acquaintances gradually started visiting again. At first, Zirong was furious, but later, seeing the money roll in, he accepted it and openly played the role of a pimp, even hiring a helper at home. The scholarly circles had long despised him, yet Zirong often boasted of calling a certain Censor his mentor and a certain provincial graduate his study mate. And a group of shameless lechers also wanted to mingle with him. The residence of Gui Zirong and the Ye girl was close to Huifang's, so Huifang knew the details very well. Liu Wenze had also gone there to drink. However, when people went to drink, Zirong insisted on acting as the host and keeping them company, so some people were unwilling to go.","Zhang Zhongyu was even more familiar with him, only Pincai didn't know about it yet.","Young Master Hua disliked hearing about these matters, so he ignored them and just focused intently on Zipei. Suddenly, he called for a large cup of wine. A servant brought a large jade goblet, and Young Master Hua told him to present it to Ziyun. Whether Ziyun drinks it or not, listen to the next chapter to find out."]},b=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations30:d},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"}));export{b as _,p as a,f as b,w as c,c as d,m as e,y as f,g,u as h,l as i};
